 fill matching, replay PC, and target-plus-four resumption.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed alias checks showing that identical branch PCs with different positive and negative B-immediates miss, while exact displacement matches replay correctly across instruction and data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 450.2, \"delta_pct\": 4.24, \"vs_baseline\": 59.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 191.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10248, \"ff\": 2052, \"seeds\": [195.96, 191.17, 189.5], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:34:13.579251Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Prune post-execute pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX/MEM register still carries resolved branch_taken and branch_target values\\nthat no downstream stage consumes, and the full execute control bundle is copied\\nthrough EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most fields are dead after EX. These wide,\\nfrequently toggling registers consume flops and routing on an FPGA where routed\\nFmax now dominates fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX/MEM register still carries resolved branch_taken and branch_target values\\nthat no downstream stage consumes, and the full execute control bundle is copied\\nthrough EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most fields are dead after EX. These wide,\\nfrequently toggling registers consume flops and routing on an FPGA where routed\\nFmax now dominates fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce stage-specific compact control payloads and remove resolved-branch\\nmetadata after it has produced pc_next and the replay-buffer signals in EX.\\nEX/MEM will retain only memory, writeback, and trap controls; MEM/WB will retain\\nonly register-write, result-select, and trap controls. This is an end-to-end\\ndeletion of unused state with no new mux, comparator, interlock, or fetch-PC\\nfeedback and no change to instruction timing, CoreMark cycles, RVFI fields,\\nbus-stall behavior, or nret. Reducing pipeline-register width and control fanout\\nshould improve placement and routed Fmax while also lowering FF and LUT use.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control types, replace the full ctrl_t fields in downstream payloads, and delete unused registered branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM and stop registering branch outcome metadata after redirect, pc_next, and replay qualification have been computed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM control, propagate only retirement-relevant writeback and trap bits into MEM/WB, and preserve all alignment, memory-mask, held-forwarding, and RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, retirement, and trap references for the stage-specific control payloads without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select and qualify register writeback from the compact MEM/WB control payload with behavior identical to the existing full-control path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control types, replace the full ctrl_t fields in downstream payloads, and delete unused registered branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM and stop registering branch outcome metadata after redirect, pc_next, and replay qualification have been computed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM control, propagate only retirement-relevant writeback and trap bits into MEM/WB, and preserve all alignment, memory-mask, held-forwarding, and RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, retirement, and trap references for the stage-specific control payloads without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select and qualify register writeback from the compact MEM/WB control payload with behavior identical to the existing full-control path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 436.99, \"delta_pct\": -5.79, \"vs_baseline\": 54.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 185.56, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10185, \"ff\": 1971, \"seeds\": [179.92, 185.56, 198.93], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:52:35.726601Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9}\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Collapse MEM and WB into direct commit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline carries a wide MEM/WB payload for every\\ninstruction and keeps a second writeback forwarding source on both EX operand\\nmuxes. The register exists mainly to delay retirement by one cycle: EX/MEM\\nalready holds all architectural metadata, and the memory stage already forms\\nthe final load value, masks, trap state, and writeback value combinationally.\\nOn the Tang Nano fabric, deleting a pipeline boundary and a forwarding tier is\\na promising way to reduce both routed state and ordinary-instruction mux depth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline carries a wide MEM/WB payload for every\\ninstruction and keeps a second writeback forwarding source on both EX operand\\nmuxes. The register exists mainly to delay retirement by one cycle: EX/MEM\\nalready holds all architectural metadata, and the memory stage already forms\\nthe final load value, masks, trap state, and writeback value combinationally.\\nOn the Tang Nano fabric, deleting a pipeline boundary and a forwarding tier is\\na promising way to reduce both routed state and ordinary-instruction mux depth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the core to IF-ID-EX/MEM-commit by making the memory stage a purely\\ncombinational completion block over the held EX/MEM entry. An instruction\\ncommits directly when EX/MEM is valid and either is not a memory operation or\\nits dmem handshake is ready; a stalled memory entry remains held exactly as it\\nis today and cannot retire or write twice. Feed the final commit value to the\\nregister file's existing write-first port and drive RVFI directly from the\\nsame completion bundle, then remove the MEM/WB register, WB stage, and MEM/WB\\noperand-forwarding tier.\\n\\nPreserve the current one-cycle load-use interlock. Thus a load result crosses\\nonly into register-file writeback and same-edge ID capture, never directly\\ninto the following instruction's EX ALU cone; immediate ALU dependencies still\\nuse the registered EX/MEM bypass, while dependencies two positions back see\\nthe direct commit through the existing write-first register-file behavior.\\nThis should leave steady-state CoreMark IPC and bus semantics unchanged while\\nremoving hundreds of pipeline flops, their routing, and one 32-bit input from\\neach EX forwarding mux, increasing routed Fmax without speculative frontend\\nlogic, new global interlocks, or any change to the nret=1 contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the registered MEM/WB payload with a compact combinational commit bundle containing final writeback, RVFI, memory-mask, trap, and valid fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB register and emit a ready-qualified combinational completion bundle from EX/MEM, preserving fixed lane selection, alignment traps, memory masks, and exact single-handshake commit semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retire and write the register file directly from the completion bundle, increment RVFI order only on commit, remove MEM/WB and WB-stage wiring, and keep dmem-stalled EX/MEM entries held until ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce each EX operand selector to registered EX/MEM forwarding versus the ID/EX value, deleting the MEM/WB comparison and select encoding while retaining x0 and youngest-writer rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the reduced two-source forwarding selection without adding completion data or dmem input to the execute datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the load-use bubble and EX/MEM dmem hold, but remove MEM/WB hold generation because completion valid is directly qualified by dmem readiness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the obsolete standalone WB stage after its final-result selection and write qualification move into the direct commit path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for ALU and load dependency distances, write-first commit-to-ID capture, memory stalls, single store issue, single retirement, RVFI order, and load or misalignment trap behavior in the four-stage topology.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the registered MEM/WB payload with a compact combinational commit bundle containing final writeback, RVFI, memory-mask, trap, and valid fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB register and emit a ready-qualified combinational completion bundle from EX/MEM, preserving fixed lane selection, alignment traps, memory masks, and exact single-handshake commit semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retire and write the register file directly from the completion bundle, increment RVFI order only on commit, remove MEM/WB and WB-stage wiring, and keep dmem-stalled EX/MEM entries held until ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce each EX operand selector to registered EX/MEM forwarding versus the ID/EX value, deleting the MEM/WB comparison and select encoding while retaining x0 and youngest-writer rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the reduced two-source forwarding selection without adding completion data or dmem input to the execute datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the load-use bubble and EX/MEM dmem hold, but remove MEM/WB hold generation because completion valid is directly qualified by dmem readiness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the obsolete standalone WB stage after its final-result selection and write qualification move into the direct commit path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for ALU and load dependency distances, write-first commit-to-ID capture, memory stalls, single store issue, single retirement, RVFI order, and load or misalignment trap behavior in the four-stage topology.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): Direct combinational commit from EX/MEM makes post-edge RVFI observe a newly advanced load with stale io_dmemRData from the prior address, so load retirement still needs a response/commit holding boundary even if the full MEM/WB payload is removed.\"}\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-strike replay-entry admission\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The exact-target one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\\nthat has improved fitness, but its replace-on-every-miss policy lets a single\\ncold taken backward branch evict a frequently reused loop-head instruction.\\nAdding capacity or another identity tag has already imposed much more FPGA\\ntiming cost than CoreMark recovered, so replacement policy is the remaining\\nway to improve effective coverage without widening the replay datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The exact-target one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\\nthat has improved fitness, but its replace-on-every-miss policy lets a single\\ncold taken backward branch evict a frequently reused loop-head instruction.\\nAdding capacity or another identity tag has already imposed much more FPGA\\ntiming cost than CoreMark recovered, so replacement policy is the remaining\\nway to improve effective coverage without widening the replay datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one miss-history bit implementing two-strike admission for a valid replay\\nentry. A confirmed eligible miss immediately after a replay hit preserves the\\ncurrent entry and merely arms replacement; a second eligible miss before the\\nnext hit may start the existing accepted-fetch fill and replace the entry. Any\\nreplay hit clears the armed bit, while an invalid entry still fills on its\\nfirst eligible miss so cold-start behavior remains fast.\\n\\nThis policy should keep a hot CoreMark loop head resident across isolated\\ntaken branches and reduce destructive one-entry thrashing. It adds no target\\nstate, comparator, instruction mux, replay phase, or logic on io_imemAddr; the\\nreplay hit equation and all pipeline, stall, trap, RVFI, and nret=1 behavior\\nremain unchanged. If execution moves to a new phase, two eligible misses are\\nsufficient to admit its target, bounding the loss from stale-entry retention.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single replacement-armed bit and two-strike admission policy around the existing miss-to-fill state update, with immediate first fill when the entry is invalid and hit-based re-protection, while leaving the exact-target replay hit path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for cold first-miss fill, preservation after one isolated conflicting miss, replacement after two misses without an intervening hit, hit-based re-protection, and identical behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single replacement-armed bit and two-strike admission policy around the existing miss-to-fill state update, with immediate first fill when the entry is invalid and hit-based re-protection, while leaving the exact-target replay hit path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for cold first-miss fill, preservation after one isolated conflicting miss, replacement after two misses without an intervening hit, hit-based re-protection, and identical behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 322.0, \"delta_pct\": -30.58, \"vs_baseline\": 13.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 136.43, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4236949, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10211, \"ff\": 1961, \"seeds\": [119.22, 157.06, 136.43], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:50:50.397008Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-only reset for pipeline payload banks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB synchronously clear every bit of their wide packed\\npayloads even though invalid entries are architecturally ignored. This puts\\nreset and flush selection on hundreds of PC, instruction, operand, result,\\nand RVFI-data flops, increasing global control fanout and placement pressure\\nin a design whose fitness is now dominated by routed Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB synchronously clear every bit of their wide packed\\npayloads even though invalid entries are architecturally ignored. This puts\\nreset and flush selection on hundreds of PC, instruction, operand, result,\\nand RVFI-data flops, increasing global control fanout and placement pressure\\nin a design whose fitness is now dominated by routed Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split each pipeline register into a small validity/control bank and a wide\\ndata bank. Reset and bubble flushes clear only valid plus the control bits\\nneeded to suppress register writes, memory requests, forwarding, and traps;\\ndata fields retain or capture don't-care values while invalid and never\\nacquire a reset/flush mux. Keep the existing stall and MEM/WB held-forwarding\\nbehavior exactly intact. Unlike the failed register-file valid-mask attempt,\\nthis adds no lookup or select to an active operand path: validity already\\nqualifies retirement and side effects. Removing reset/flush fanout from the\\nwide payload flops should improve placement and median Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, pipeline latency, bus handshakes, RVFI semantics, or nret.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor ID/EX into separately clocked control-valid and data banks so reset and flush invalidate the entry without clearing PC, instruction, immediate, register tags, or operand payloads; preserve replay and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor EX/MEM so reset clears valid and side-effect controls only, while the wide execute and RVFI payload bank has no reset mux and continues to hold on data-memory stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor MEM/WB into valid, control, and payload banks, retaining the existing hold behavior needed for forwarding while removing reset from wide result, memory, instruction, PC, and operand fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_reset.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed reset, redirect-flush, load-use bubble, replay, and data-stall checks proving invalid payload values cannot retire, write registers, issue memory requests, perturb forwarding, or violate RVFI once a valid instruction arrives.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor ID/EX into separately clocked control-valid and data banks so reset and flush invalidate the entry without clearing PC, instruction, immediate, register tags, or operand payloads; preserve replay and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor EX/MEM so reset clears valid and side-effect controls only, while the wide execute and RVFI payload bank has no reset mux and continues to hold on data-memory stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor MEM/WB into valid, control, and payload banks, retaining the existing hold behavior needed for forwarding while removing reset from wide result, memory, instruction, PC, and operand fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_reset.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed reset, redirect-flush, load-use bubble, replay, and data-stall checks proving invalid payload values cannot retire, write registers, issue memory requests, perturb forwarding, or violate RVFI once a valid instruction arrives.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 402.27, \"delta_pct\": -13.28, \"vs_baseline\": 42.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 170.82, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10010, \"ff\": 1957, \"seeds\": [170.82, 170.56, 173.25], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:16:52.246722Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -13.28%): Splitting packed pipeline registers into separate valid/control and unreset payload always_ff banks can cost more in FPGA register-control packing and routing than it saves in reset fanout, so valid-only reset is not automatically a timing win for wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB payloads.\"}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed shared PC-relative target adder\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one shared PC-plus-immediate value combinationally in ID and register\\nit alongside each instruction in ID/EX. Use that registered value in EX for\\nconditional-branch targets, direct-JAL targets, and AUIPC results; retain the\\nexisting forwarded rs1-plus-immediate adder only for JALR. This retimes the\\nwide PC-relative addition across the ID/EX boundary and removes it from the\\nhigh-frequency EX-to-fetch redirect path without changing branch latency,\\nprediction policy, instruction count, or any bus handshake.\\n\\nThe registered sum must follow the instruction through every existing hold,\\nflush, replay, and reset case. EX remains authoritative for branch condition,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, target-alignment traps, redirect qualification, and\\nRVFI pc_wdata. Sharing the retimed value with AUIPC also prevents synthesis\\nfrom retaining a redundant PC-relative adder. The expected result is higher\\nmedian Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles, for a net fitness improvement with\\nonly 32 additional pipeline bits and no new global control or fetch-PC mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 422.03, \"delta_pct\": -9.02, \"vs_baseline\": 49.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 179.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10363, \"ff\": 1990, \"seeds\": [190.22, 179.21, 153.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:19:59.820672Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -9.02%): Retiming the shared 32-bit PC-plus-immediate adder from EX into combinational ID can merely trade the redirect cone for a decode-to-ID/EX path plus 32 routed payload bits, so PC-relative target precomputation is not a timing win unless the added decode and pipeline-routing cost is cheaper.\"}\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Direction-agnostic confirmed branch replay\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The exact-target, one-instruction confirmed replay buffer is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but it currently excludes every taken\\nforward conditional branch and spends a full-width comparison deciding whether\\nthe resolved target is below the branch PC. CoreMark repeats forward as well as\\nbackward control decisions inside loops, so this restriction leaves useful\\nreplay opportunities uncovered while retaining direction logic in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The exact-target, one-instruction confirmed replay buffer is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but it currently excludes every taken\\nforward conditional branch and spends a full-width comparison deciding whether\\nthe resolved target is below the branch PC. CoreMark repeats forward as well as\\nbackward control decisions inside loops, so this restriction leaves useful\\nreplay opportunities uncovered while retaining direction logic in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Admit every valid, legal, aligned, taken conditional branch to the existing\\nexact-target replay buffer, irrespective of displacement direction. A matching\\nentry will replay the cached target instruction only after EX confirms the\\nbranch is taken, exactly as today; misses will use the unchanged accepted-fetch\\nfill protocol. Remove the branch_target < branch_pc comparison and replace the\\nresolved_taken_backward interface with a simple resolved_taken qualification.\\n\\nThis broadens dynamic coverage and deletes a 32-bit magnitude comparator without\\nadding tags, entries, history bits, instruction mux levels, or any selection on\\nio_imemAddr. EX remains authoritative, so not-taken branches, illegal encodings,\\nmisaligned targets, redirects, bus stalls, RVFI ordering, and retirement behavior\\nremain unchanged. The expected gain comes from both fewer repeated forward-branch\\nbubbles and reduced execute-to-replay timing and routing cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace backward-only replay eligibility with a valid legal aligned taken-conditional-branch signal, eliminating the full-width target-versus-PC direction comparison while leaving redirect and trap resolution authoritative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the direction-agnostic confirmed-taken signal from EX to the existing replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface, hazard priorities, or fetch-address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow the unchanged exact-target hit and accepted-fetch fill protocol to operate for taken forward and backward conditional branches, with no added predictor state or datapath width.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for repeated taken forward branches, alternating taken and not-taken outcomes, exact-target conflicts, misaligned targets, and replay behavior across imem and dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace backward-only replay eligibility with a valid legal aligned taken-conditional-branch signal, eliminating the full-width target-versus-PC direction comparison while leaving redirect and trap resolution authoritative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the direction-agnostic confirmed-taken signal from EX to the existing replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface, hazard priorities, or fetch-address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow the unchanged exact-target hit and accepted-fetch fill protocol to operate for taken forward and backward conditional branches, with no added predictor state or datapath width.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for repeated taken forward branches, alternating taken and not-taken outcomes, exact-target conflicts, misaligned targets, and replay behavior across imem and dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 191.82, \"delta_pct\": -58.65, \"vs_baseline\": -32.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 81.65, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4256598, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10714, \"ff\": 1955, \"seeds\": [88.79, 81.17, 81.65], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:14:04.794042Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -58.65%): Admitting taken forward branches to a one-entry exact-target replay buffer makes every confirmed miss replace its pending or hot target, so CoreMark's forward control flow can thrash the high-value backward-loop entry despite removing the EX direction comparator.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'false.*load|load.use|uses_rs|source.*use|operand.*use|hazard.*opcode|spurious.*stall' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md; jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'false.*load|load.use|uses_rs|source.*use|operand.*use|hazard.*opcode|spurious.*stall' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md; jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:6:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -25.45%): Forwarding raw io_dmemRData through the EX/MEM operand mux into the following instruction's ALU trades a load-use bubble for a dmem-read-to-EX combinational path whose FPGA timing cost outweighs the CoreMark CPI gain.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:12:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -23.48%): Even a registered late load-to-store bypass puts a MEM/WB tag compare and writeback-data mux directly on the dmem write-data path, so removing CoreMark's rs2-only load-use bubbles can lose more FPGA Fmax than it recovers in IPC.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share one multiplier across all RV32M multiply variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one 64-bit unsigned product for every MUL-family operation and derive the\\nsigned high halves with 32-bit correction subtractors: subtract b from the high\\nhalf when a is negative, and subtract a as well when b is negative. MUL continues\\nto use the common low half. This identity is exact modulo 2^64 and should remove\\ntwo large parallel multipliers, lowering LUT pressure and improving routed Fmax\\nwithout changing pipeline latency, CoreMark IPC, or the existing ALTOPS behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 404.74, \"delta_pct\": 43.11, \"vs_baseline\": 43.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10073, \"ff\": 1860, \"seeds\": [181.79, 175.38, 185.53], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T12:55:00.760698Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product across RV32M MUL variants and correcting only the signed high halves avoids parallel inferred multipliers, substantially reducing multiplier fabric and routing pressure without adding pipeline latency.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with a two-entry instruction FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend ties the fetch PC directly to backend stalls. Whenever a\\ndata-memory operation freezes EX/MEM, an otherwise-ready instruction-memory\\ncycle is discarded; later random instruction-memory backpressure then creates\\nanother bubble. A shallow fetch queue can use those idle frontend cycles to\\nbuild slack without adding a cache or changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend ties the fetch PC directly to backend stalls. Whenever a\\ndata-memory operation freezes EX/MEM, an otherwise-ready instruction-memory\\ncycle is discarded; later random instruction-memory backpressure then creates\\nanother bubble. A shallow fetch queue can use those idle frontend cycles to\\nbuild slack without adding a cache or changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the tightly coupled IF path with a two-entry fall-through instruction\\nFIFO carrying PC and instruction, and give fetch its own request PC. Enqueue\\nonly when io_imemReady is asserted, dequeue only when ID can advance, and keep\\nfetching during backend and load-use stalls while space remains. On an EX\\nredirect, invalidate all queued wrong-path entries and restart the request PC\\nat the redirect target. Hazard control should stall decode on FIFO-empty rather\\nthan directly on io_imemReady. This should overlap independent iStall and\\ndStall intervals, improving CoreMark iterations/cycle with only a small bank of\\nflops and shallow control muxes, so median Fmax should remain nearly unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two-entry PC/instruction FIFO, independent fetch request PC, ready-qualified enqueue, decode-qualified dequeue, and redirect flush/restart behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady into the decoupled frontend and connect FIFO-valid/consume control between fetch, hazard control, and ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct instruction-bus backpressure handling with frontend-empty and dequeue control while allowing fetch to run during backend stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_fetch_fifo.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb checks for ready-qualified fetches, prefetch during backend stalls, FIFO-empty bubbles, in-order delivery, and complete wrong-path invalidation on redirects.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two-entry PC/instruction FIFO, independent fetch request PC, ready-qualified enqueue, decode-qualified dequeue, and redirect flush/restart behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady into the decoupled frontend and connect FIFO-valid/consume control between fetch, hazard control, and ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct instruction-bus backpressure handling with frontend-empty and dequeue control while allowing fetch to run during backend stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_fetch_fifo.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb checks for ready-qualified fetches, prefetch during backend stalls, FIFO-empty bubbles, in-order delivery, and complete wrong-path invalidation on redirects.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.25, \"delta_pct\": 8.99, \"vs_baseline\": 8.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.83, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4341607, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9940, \"ff\": 1981, \"seeds\": [133.83, 137.16, 133.6], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:00:58.223364Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR currently redirects only when it reaches EX,\\nflushing the younger fetch/decode work. CoreMark contains loop-closing backward\\nbranches and direct calls, so this repeated control penalty reduces iterations\\nper cycle. A decode-light static predictor can remove most of those bubbles\\nwithout a BHT, BTB, or return stack, which is important because the current core\\nis already close to its LUT target and fitness also depends on FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR currently redirects only when it reaches EX,\\nflushing the younger fetch/decode work. CoreMark contains loop-closing backward\\nbranches and direct calls, so this repeated control penalty reduces iterations\\nper cycle. A decode-light static predictor can remove most of those bubbles\\nwithout a BHT, BTB, or return stack, which is important because the current core\\nis already close to its LUT target and fitness also depends on FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict legal backward conditional branches taken and all word-aligned direct\\nJAL targets in IF, while predicting forward branches and JALR fall-through.\\nCarry the predicted next PC with the instruction to EX and redirect only when\\nthe resolved architectural next PC differs from that prediction. Correct loop\\nand direct-call predictions therefore avoid the existing two-younger-instruction\\nflush, while wrong predictions recover through the existing redirect path.\\n\\nKeep prediction combinational and stateless: recognize the B-type opcode plus\\nlegal funct3 and use the immediate sign bit for backward-taken, recognize JAL,\\nform each target directly from instruction bits, and require word alignment.\\nEX remains authoritative for branch conditions, JALR targets, misalignment\\ntraps, and RVFI pc_wdata. Redirect must retain priority over all bus stalls, and\\nprediction metadata must remain attached to a held instruction so random imem\\nand dmem backpressure cannot cause stale or duplicate execution.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with predicted-next-PC metadata so each instruction carries the exact fetch decision that EX must validate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add decode-light BTFNT conditional-branch and direct-JAL prediction, advance the PC to aligned predicted targets, preserve redirect-over-stall priority, and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX with the same stall and flush behavior as the instruction, PC, operands, and valid bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved architectural next PC against the carried prediction and assert redirect only on mismatch, while preserving JALR recovery and all existing misalignment-trap and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as misprediction recovery rather than every taken control transfer, preserving the existing flush, load-use, and bus-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward taken and not-taken branches, forward branches, direct JAL, JALR fallback, misaligned targets, and recovery coincident with imem or dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with predicted-next-PC metadata so each instruction carries the exact fetch decision that EX must validate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add decode-light BTFNT conditional-branch and direct-JAL prediction, advance the PC to aligned predicted targets, preserve redirect-over-stall priority, and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX with the same stall and flush behavior as the instruction, PC, operands, and valid bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved architectural next PC against the carried prediction and assert redirect only on mismatch, while preserving JALR recovery and all existing misalignment-trap and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as misprediction recovery rather than every taken control transfer, preserving the existing flush, load-use, and bus-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward taken and not-taken branches, forward branches, direct JAL, JALR fallback, misaligned targets, and recovery coincident with imem or dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 307.6, \"delta_pct\": 8.76, \"vs_baseline\": 8.76, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10330, \"ff\": 1895, \"seeds\": [114.32, 133.08, 131.03], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T12:58:54.342951Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.76%): Combinational IF opcode/immediate decode, parallel B/J target adders, and a 32-bit predicted-next-PC path can cost more FPGA Fmax than CoreMark recovers from avoided control bubbles, so prediction must stay off the fetch PC feedback cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share unsigned division across RV32M DIV and REM variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU expresses signed and unsigned quotient and remainder operations as\\nseparate wide division and modulo operators. Since only one RV32M operation\\nexecutes at a time, parallel signed and unsigned divider fabrics add LUT and\\nrouting pressure without increasing throughput, much like the duplicated\\nmultipliers removed by the previous winning micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU expresses signed and unsigned quotient and remainder operations as\\nseparate wide division and modulo operators. Since only one RV32M operation\\nexecutes at a time, parallel signed and unsigned divider fabrics add LUT and\\nrouting pressure without increasing throughput, much like the duplicated\\nmultipliers removed by the previous winning micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert both operands to unsigned magnitudes, compute one common unsigned\\nquotient and remainder pair, and apply the RV32M sign rules afterward: negate\\nDIV when operand signs differ, negate REM when the dividend is negative, and\\nleave DIVU/REMU unchanged. Preserve the explicit divide-by-zero and signed\\noverflow results, as well as the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch. Sharing\\nthe expensive magnitude arithmetic should reduce divider fabric and routing,\\nimproving routed Fmax without changing instruction latency or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace separate signed and unsigned DIV/REM expressions with common unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder arithmetic plus compact result sign correction, retaining every RV32M corner case and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed DIV and REM coverage around mixed signs, zero divisors, INT_MIN operands, and the INT_MIN divided by minus one overflow case.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace separate signed and unsigned DIV/REM expressions with common unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder arithmetic plus compact result sign correction, retaining every RV32M corner case and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed DIV and REM coverage around mixed signs, zero divisors, INT_MIN operands, and the INT_MIN divided by minus one overflow case.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 99.12, \"delta_pct\": -75.51, \"vs_baseline\": -64.95, \"fmax_mhz\": 44.52, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7699, \"ff\": 1855, \"seeds\": [44.52, 43.2, 46.97], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:15:57.880711Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (regression, -75.51%): Unrolling a 32-step restoring divider in always_comb creates a deep compare-subtract recurrence that overwhelms any area benefit from sharing DIV/REM arithmetic, so divider reuse must be iterative or pipelined rather than fully combinational.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"EX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\\n\\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 221.94, \"delta_pct\": -45.16, \"vs_baseline\": -21.53, \"fmax_mhz\": 93.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4234052, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 13089, \"ff\": 2982, \"seeds\": [93.97, 105.74, 93.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:23:20.366277Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Gate load-use hazards by actual source operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a pending load destination against both raw\\nsource-register bitfields of every following instruction. Several RV32IM\\nformats do not read one or either of those fields, so immediate bits or a\\ndestination field can accidentally match the load destination and insert a\\nneedless pipeline bubble. CoreMark contains frequent loads followed by\\nimmediate arithmetic, further loads, and control-flow instructions, making\\nthese false dependencies a direct IPC loss under the existing bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a pending load destination against both raw\\nsource-register bitfields of every following instruction. Several RV32IM\\nformats do not read one or either of those fields, so immediate bits or a\\ndestination field can accidentally match the load destination and insert a\\nneedless pipeline bubble. CoreMark contains frequent loads followed by\\nimmediate arithmetic, further loads, and control-flow instructions, making\\nthese false dependencies a direct IPC loss under the existing bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode only the inexpensive opcode-level rs1/rs2 usage predicates for the\\ninstruction currently in IF/ID, and qualify each load-use comparison with\\nwhether that source is architecturally read. R-type, branches, and stores use\\nboth sources; OP-IMM, loads, and JALR use rs1 only; JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\nand SYSTEM use neither for hazard purposes. This removes false stalls without\\naltering true load-use interlocks, forwarding, pipeline latency, retirement,\\nor the nret=1 interface. The added gates are local to hazard detection and\\nshould improve CoreMark iterations/cycle with little Fmax or area cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify pending-load rd comparisons with opcode-derived rs1_used and rs2_used predicates so only architectural source operands cause a load-use bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load dependencies still stall while false matches in unused instruction fields do not, covering each RV32IM opcode class.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify pending-load rd comparisons with opcode-derived rs1_used and rs2_used predicates so only architectural source operands cause a load-use bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load dependencies still stall while false matches in unused instruction fields do not, covering each RV32IM opcode class.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\n.vcd\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:35  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:35  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:35  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:36  Status: failed\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:36 (36)\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:13 (13)\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 10:34:07 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\n\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\nSBY 10:33:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 14..\\nSBY 10:33:32 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 15..\\nSBY 10:33: