ne_0: ##   0:00:06  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:07  Status: failed\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:07 (7)\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:03 (3)\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: liveness_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_liveness_check_sv_38_58 at rvfi_liveness_check.sv:38.5-38.28 step 20\\\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\\\n\\\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/last_run-53177.log\\\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-53177/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 7, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering the IF/ID instruction response is an elastic-buffer protocol change, not a pure retime; request-PC advance, response valid, decode consume/refill, and redirect/replay invalidation must be tracked independently or instructions can be dropped or stranded, as the liveness, PC-forwarding, and register-state checks expose.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:22:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-instruction confirmed loop-head replay line\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry confirmed branch-target instruction replay buffer is the only\\\\npredictor design that has improved fitness: it removes a hot loop bubble\\\\nwithout placing target selection on the instruction-address timing cone.\\\\nIt currently caches only the first target instruction, so target+4 must still\\\\nbe fetched normally and is exposed to the benchmark's roughly 22 percent\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure. Prior attempts show that another wide tag\\\\ncomparison is too expensive, but a second data word under the existing tag\\\\ncan extend useful coverage without another associative lookup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry confirmed branch-target instruction replay buffer is the only\\\\npredictor design that has improved fitness: it removes a hot loop bubble\\\\nwithout placing target selection on the instruction-address timing cone.\\\\nIt currently caches only the first target instruction, so target+4 must still\\\\nbe fetched normally and is exposed to the benchmark's roughly 22 percent\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure. Prior attempts show that another wide tag\\\\ncomparison is too expensive, but a second data word under the existing tag\\\\ncan extend useful coverage without another associative lookup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the existing entry into a two-instruction loop-head replay line sharing\\\\nthe same exact branch-PC and target tags. During a cold fill, capture both the\\\\ntarget and target+4 instructions on their respective accepted fetches. On an\\\\nEX-confirmed hit, inject the target instruction immediately as today, queue\\\\nthe cached target+4 instruction for the following accepted ID slot, and move\\\\nthe registered fetch PC directly to target+8. The queued second instruction\\\\ntherefore hides an instruction-bus stall at target+4 and extends the useful\\\\nreplay burst without adding any speculative lookup or mux to io_imemAddr.\\\\n\\\\nUse an explicit valid/consume handshake for the second replay beat so dmem\\\\nstalls and load-use hazards hold it without loss or duplication. Hazard\\\\nsource-address checks must select the pending replay instruction rather than\\\\nthe simultaneously visible live fetch. Entry validity is published only\\\\nafter both words have been accepted, and all legal/aligned/backward-branch,\\\\nexact-tag, RVFI, redirect, and retirement behavior remains unchanged. This\\\\nadds one 32-bit instruction register and a small replay-phase bit, but no\\\\nsecond tag, target adder, or parallel equality tree, so it should preserve\\\\nrouted Fmax while reducing CoreMark cycles under the scored stall model.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the replay payload/control representation for a stallable second cached instruction while leaving architectural pipeline and RVFI fields unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Capture target and target+4 instructions under one existing exact tag pair, publish the line only after both accepted fills, emit the first word on an EX-confirmed hit, and retain the second word until an explicit ID consume handshake.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire replay-valid/consume control, qualify the second beat against pipeline acceptance, and select replay instruction source fields for load-use detection without changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Advance the registered PC to cached target+8 on a confirmed two-word replay and suppress the live target+8 payload while the queued target+4 instruction is being consumed, keeping io_imemAddr driven only by the PC register.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Accept each replay beat exactly once, hold the queued second beat across dmem or load-use stalls, and preserve flush and redirect correctness without duplicating retirement.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks for two-word fill, ordered replay, target+8 resumption, instruction-bus-stall hiding, second-word load-use hazards, dmem-stall holds, tag misses, and single retirement of each cached instruction.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the replay payload/control representation for a stallable second cached instruction while leaving architectural pipeline and RVFI fields unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Capture target and target+4 instructions under one existing exact tag pair, publish the line only after both accepted fills, emit the first word on an EX-confirmed hit, and retain the second word until an explicit ID consume handshake.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire replay-valid/consume control, qualify the second beat against pipeline acceptance, and select replay instruction source fields for load-use detection without changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Advance the registered PC to cached target+8 on a confirmed two-word replay and suppress the live target+8 payload while the queued target+4 instruction is being consumed, keeping io_imemAddr driven only by the PC register.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Accept each replay beat exactly once, hold the queued second beat across dmem or load-use stalls, and preserve flush and redirect correctness without duplicating retirement.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks for two-word fill, ordered replay, target+8 resumption, instruction-bus-stall hiding, second-word load-use hazards, dmem-stall holds, tag misses, and single retirement of each cached instruction.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 283.98, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -34.25, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.41, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 119.08, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4193281, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 11070, \\\"ff\\\": 2115, \\\"seeds\\\": [119.08, 116.54, 121.55], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:15:58.133934Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 7, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.25%): Extending a confirmed one-word replay entry to target+4 can regress badly even with shared tags, because the phase-controlled 32-bit instruction/PC selection and stall-safe consume/cancel control burden decode and hazard paths more than hiding occasional instruction-memory backpressure repays.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:23:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Target-only replay-buffer tag\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The confirmed one-entry replay buffer is the only control-flow optimization\\\\nthat has improved the current core, but each hit compares both the owning\\\\nbranch PC and the cached target PC. The payload is simply the instruction at\\\\nthat target, so the branch-PC identity is redundant: any legal, aligned,\\\\ntaken backward branch resolving to the same target can safely reuse it.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The confirmed one-entry replay buffer is the only control-flow optimization\\\\nthat has improved the current core, but each hit compares both the owning\\\\nbranch PC and the cached target PC. The payload is simply the instruction at\\\\nthat target, so the branch-PC identity is redundant: any legal, aligned,\\\\ntaken backward branch resolving to the same target can safely reuse it.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Remove the stored branch-PC tag, its pending-fill copy, and the parallel\\\\n32-bit branch-PC equality from the replay-buffer hit path. Retain the exact\\\\n32-bit target comparison, captured target instruction, confirmed-EX lookup,\\\\naccepted-fetch fill protocol, and target-plus-four resume behavior unchanged.\\\\nThis is a strict hardware reduction with no new muxes or qualifiers, and\\\\nshould reduce comparator fabric, flops, and routing on the replay decision\\\\nwhile modestly broadening reuse when multiple backward branches share a loop\\\\nhead. Architectural execution, stalls, RVFI, traps, and nret remain unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Delete the entry and pending branch-PC tags and qualify confirmed replay hits solely with the existing exact target-PC tag and eligibility signals, preserving fill and replay timing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the now-unused resolved branch-PC output while retaining legal aligned taken-backward qualification and the exact resolved target.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant resolved-branch-PC wiring and update the replay-buffer connection without changing pipeline, bus-stall, or nret=1 behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks that same-target backward branches reuse one cached instruction, different targets miss and refill, and replay remains single-shot and stall-safe.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Delete the entry and pending branch-PC tags and qualify confirmed replay hits solely with the existing exact target-PC tag and eligibility signals, preserving fill and replay timing.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the now-unused resolved branch-PC output while retaining legal aligned taken-backward qualification and the exact resolved target.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the redundant resolved-branch-PC wiring and update the replay-buffer connection without changing pipeline, bus-stall, or nret=1 behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks that same-target backward branches reuse one cached instruction, different targets miss and refill, and replay remains single-shot and stall-safe.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 463.86, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 7.4, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 64.01, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 196.97, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4246355, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10217, \\\"ff\\\": 1956, \\\"seeds\\\": [186.25, 197.01, 196.97], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:40:47.746851Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (improvement, +7.40%): A confirmed backward-branch replay entry is safely keyed by its exact target alone, so dropping the redundant branch-PC tag removes 32-bit comparator, flop, and routing cost while allowing branches that share a loop head to reuse the cached instruction.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:24:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Completion-decoupled divide execute island\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Dispatch DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of ID/EX into a dedicated iterative\\\\nexecute island and immediately enter a registered divide mode. On dispatch,\\\\nallow ID/EX to capture exactly the single sequential successor already at\\\\ndecode, then hold that register and the fetch PC using the registered mode\\\\nbit until completion. Older EX/MEM work drains normally while the divider\\\\nruns; when both the result is ready and EX/MEM can accept it, inject one\\\\nsaved divide payload and result into EX/MEM, clear divide mode, and resume\\\\nthe held successor on the following cycle.\\\\n\\\\nThis completion-decoupled topology removes combinational div_busy/div_done\\\\nfeedback from hazard_unit and from the normal ALU-result path. Ordinary\\\\ninstructions see only the existing pipeline, while the divide island owns\\\\nits operands, RVFI metadata, corner-case state, and one completion-valid\\\\nbit. Keep the existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results on the normal\\\\npath so bounded formal depth is unchanged. The large divider-area reduction\\\\nobserved previously should then translate into routed-Fmax headroom without\\\\nthe high-fanout interlock that caused that experiment's regression.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/divider.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_divider.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/divider.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_divider.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 387.51, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -10.27, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 37.02, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 164.55, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4246317, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5903, \\\"ff\\\": 2385, \\\"seeds\\\": [160.57, 166.94, 164.55], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:37:13.351777Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.27%): Registering divide mode is not true completion decoupling when it still drives PC and ID/EX holds and gates branch redirect and EX/MEM control, because rare DIV/REM state then burdens ordinary-cycle clock-enable and execute timing cones.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"B-immediate tag for confirmed branch replay\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure that\\\\nhas improved fitness, but every hit currently requires both a full 32-bit branch-PC\\\\nequality and a second full 32-bit resolved-target equality. The target comparison\\\\nis wider than necessary because an RV32I conditional branch target is completely\\\\ndetermined by its 12 encoded B-immediate bits plus the already-compared branch PC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure that\\\\nhas improved fitness, but every hit currently requires both a full 32-bit branch-PC\\\\nequality and a second full 32-bit resolved-target equality. The target comparison\\\\nis wider than necessary because an RV32I conditional branch target is completely\\\\ndetermined by its 12 encoded B-immediate bits plus the already-compared branch PC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the replay entry's 32-bit target tag comparison with an exact comparison of\\\\nthe branch instruction's B-immediate bits [12:1]. EX will export these bits directly\\\\nfrom the already-latched decoded immediate, and the replay buffer will store them\\\\nwhen a confirmed miss begins a fill. A hit will require the unchanged full branch-PC\\\\nmatch plus the 12-bit displacement match, while the stored full target PC remains\\\\navailable solely to label the cached instruction and advance fetch to target+4.\\\\n\\\\nThis is an exact tag transformation for legal conditional branches: branch PC and\\\\nB-immediate uniquely determine the resolved target, including backward offsets, so\\\\nhit behavior, CoreMark cycles, redirects, stalls, RVFI state, and illegal/misaligned\\\\nhandling remain unchanged. Removing 20 comparator bits and their routed resolved-\\\\ntarget inputs from the replay-hit cone should raise FPGA Fmax without adding fetch-PC\\\\nfeedback logic, replay phases, range guards, or another entry.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Export the exact B-immediate displacement tag from the valid legal conditional branch in EX alongside the existing resolved branch PC and target signals.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the compact resolved displacement tag between EX and the confirmed replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface or any hazard and redirect priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Store and compare the 12-bit B-immediate tag instead of comparing the full 32-bit target tag, while retaining the full target PC for fill matching, replay PC, and target-plus-four resumption.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed alias checks showing that identical branch PCs with different positive and negative B-immediates miss, while exact displacement matches replay correctly across instruction and data bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Export the exact B-immediate displacement tag from the valid legal conditional branch in EX alongside the existing resolved branch PC and target signals.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the compact resolved displacement tag between EX and the confirmed replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface or any hazard and redirect priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Store and compare the 12-bit B-immediate tag instead of comparing the full 32-bit target tag, while retaining the full target PC for fill matching, replay PC, and target-plus-four resumption.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed alias checks showing that identical branch PCs with different positive and negative B-immediates miss, while exact displacement matches replay correctly across instruction and data bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 450.2, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 4.24, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 59.18, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 191.17, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4246355, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10248, \\\"ff\\\": 2052, \\\"seeds\\\": [195.96, 191.17, 189.5], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T17:34:13.579251Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 8, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/imm_gen.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\\ncores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\"),(.fmax_mhz//\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\"),(.cycles//\\\\\\\"n/a\\\\\\\"),(.implementation_notes//\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\njq -r 'select(.category==\\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\") | \\\\\\\"ID: \\\\\\\\(.id)\\\\\\\\nTITLE: \\\\\\\\(.title)\\\\\\\\nHYP: \\\\\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\\\\\nCHANGES: \\\\\\\\(.changes|map(.file)|join(\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"))\\\\\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\\\\\(.outcome) delta=\\\\\\\\(.delta_pct) fmax=\\\\\\\\(.fmax_mhz) cycles=\\\\\\\\(.cycles)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\\tDecouple fetch with a two-entry instruction FIFO\\tregression\\t8.99\\t133.83\\t4341607\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r2s1\\tIterative shared divider with pipeline interlock\\tregression\\t-26.08\\t134.39\\t4491817\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r3s1\\tFast aligned-LW MEM-to-EX bypass\\tregression\\t-25.45\\t131.93\\t4372553\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r4s1\\tOne-entry ordered store buffer\\tplacement_failed\\tn/a\\tn/a\\tn/a\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r5s1\\tLate store-data forwarding at the MEM boundary\\tregression\\t-23.48\\t140.33\\t4246318\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r6s1\\tHalf-cycle register-file writeback\\tbroken\\tn/a\\tn/a\\tn/a\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r7s1\\tRegistered instruction-response boundary\\tbroken\\tn/a\\tn/a\\tn/a\\t\\nhyp-20260711-002-r8s1\\tCompletion-decoupled divide execute island\\tregression\\t-10.27\\t164.55\\t4246317\\t\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\\nTITLE: Decouple fetch with a two-entry instruction FIFO\\nHYP: Replace the tightly coupled IF path with a two-entry fall-through instruction\\nFIFO carrying PC and instruction, and give fetch its own request PC. Enqueue\\nonly when io_imemReady is asserted, dequeue only when ID can advance, and keep\\nfetching during backend and load-use stalls while space remains. On an EX\\nredirect, invalidate all queued wrong-path entries and restart the request PC\\nat the redirect target. Hazard control should stall decode on FIFO-empty rather\\nthan directly on io_imemReady. This should overlap independent iStall and\\ndStall intervals, improving CoreMark iterations/cycle with only a small bank of\\nflops and shallow control muxes, so median Fmax should remain nearly unchanged.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/if_stage.sv, rtl/core.sv, rtl/hazard_unit.sv, test/test_fetch_fifo.py\\nOUTCOME: regression delta=8.99 fmax=133.83 cycles=4341607\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\\nTITLE: Iterative shared divider with pipeline interlock\\nHYP: Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU hardware with one shared\\niterative radix-2 quotient/remainder unit. A divide-class instruction starts\\nthe unit from EX, remains resident in ID/EX, and interlocks PC, ID/EX, and\\nEX/MEM until the result is ready; it then advances exactly once through the\\nexisting retirement path. Signed operations use operand magnitudes plus final\\nquotient/remainder sign correction, with explicit divide-by-zero and\\nINT_MIN/-1 handling to retain exact RV32M semantics.\\n\\nOrdinary ALU and multiply instructions must bypass the divider with no new\\nstate or muxing on the fetch PC feedback path. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep\\nthe existing one-cycle algebraic stand-ins so the bounded formal checks retain\\ntheir current liveness depth; normal simulation and FPGA synthesis exercise\\nthe iterative engine. Removing inferred combinational division and sharing\\none subtract/shift datapath should reduce LUT and routing pressure enough to\\nraise median Fmax, while the added cycles affect only divide-class dynamic\\ninstructions.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/divider.sv, rtl/alu.sv, rtl/ex_stage.sv, rtl/hazard_unit.sv, rtl/core.sv, test/test_divider.py\\nOUTCOME: regression delta=-26.08 fmax=134.39 cycles=4491817\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\\nTITLE: Fast aligned-LW MEM-to-EX bypass\\nHYP: Add a fast forwarding path from the current MEM-stage aligned LW data directly\\nto the following instruction's EX operand mux. Suppress the load-use bubble\\nonly for legal aligned word loads; retain the existing one-cycle interlock for\\nbyte and halfword loads so their shift/sign-extension network is not placed in\\nthe EX critical path. The existing dmem-stall freeze remains authoritative: a\\ndependent instruction is held in ID/EX while io_dmemReady is low and advances\\nexactly once using io_dmemRData when readiness returns.\\n\\nReuse the existing EX/MEM forwarding select and substitute raw dmem read data\\nonly when the producer in EX/MEM is an aligned LW; all ALU producers continue\\nto forward the registered ALU result. This removes a common pipeline bubble\\nwith a single local result mux and modest hazard qualification, while leaving\\nretirement, RVFI memory fields, misalignment traps, subword semantics, and the\\nnret=1 interface unchanged.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/mem_stage.sv, rtl/core.sv, rtl/hazard_unit.sv, rtl/ex_stage.sv, test/test_load_bypass.py\\nOUTCOME: regression delta=-25.45 fmax=131.93 cycles=4372553\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\\nTITLE: One-entry ordered store buffer\\nHYP: Add a one-entry write buffer for ordinary RAM stores. When a RAM store reaches\\nMEM while io_dmemReady is low, capture its address, replicated data, and byte\\nenables, allow that store to advance to retirement, and let following\\nnon-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains. If a younger load\\nor store reaches MEM before the entry drains, hold it behind the buffered\\nstore rather than adding load forwarding or permitting memory reordering.\\n\\nUART and timing-marker writes in the 0x10000000-0x10000200 window remain\\nunbuffered and retire only on an actual bus handshake. The buffered request\\nhas priority on the external dmem port, is issued exactly once, and may be\\nreplaced by a waiting RAM store only after the older entry is accepted. This\\nshould recover cycles from isolated store backpressure while leaving the ALU,\\noperand-forwarding, and instruction-address timing cones unchanged.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/store_buffer.sv, rtl/mem_stage.sv, rtl/hazard_unit.sv, rtl/core.sv, test/test_store_buffer.py\\nOUTCOME: placement_failed delta=null fmax=null cycles=null\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\\nTITLE: Late store-data forwarding at the MEM boundary\\nHYP: Move the architectural consumption point for store rs2 data from EX to MEM.\\nPermit a store whose address is independent of the preceding load to enter\\nEX without a load-use bubble, carry its rs2 register tag with the existing\\nEX/MEM payload, and select the registered MEM/WB result as store data in MEM\\nwhen the tags match. The normal EX forwarding path remains the source for\\nall other store dependencies, and a dependency through store rs1 still\\nstalls because the address is needed in EX.\\n\\nThis is a pipeline-topology change rather than a raw load bypass: load data\\ncrosses the existing MEM/WB register before it reaches the store-data mux,\\nso no io_dmemRData-to-EX combinational path is introduced. Only write data,\\nnot address or byte enables, gains a late registered bypass, preserving the\\nwrapper RAM's simple address/control inference. Qualifying the load-use\\ninterlock for store rs2 should recover bubbles with much less routed-Fmax\\nrisk than the prior aligned-LW EX bypass or ordered store buffer.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/hazard_unit.sv, rtl/core.sv, rtl/mem_stage.sv, test/test_pipeline.py\\nOUTCOME: regression delta=-23.48 fmax=140.33 cycles=4246318\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\\nTITLE: Half-cycle register-file writeback\\nHYP: Clock architectural register-file writes on the falling edge while retaining\\nID/EX operand capture on the rising edge. A value presented by MEM/WB after a\\nrising edge will then be committed half a cycle later and will already be in\\nthe array before the following rising-edge ID capture. This preserves the\\ncurrent same-cycle WB-to-ID dependency behavior while allowing both read\\nports to become direct x0-or-array selections with no WB tag comparison or\\nwrite-data bypass mux.\\n\\nThe MEM/WB-to-register-file write path is short and registered at its source,\\nso it should fit the half-cycle constraint more comfortably than the current\\nregister-read-to-ID/EX path fits the full-cycle critical cone. Pipeline\\nhazards, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, retirement order, RVFI data, bus-stall\\nbehavior, and the nret=1 interface remain unchanged. The implementation must\\nverify reset and held-MEM/WB cases explicitly so a retirement writes exactly\\nonce and x0 remains hardwired to zero.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/reg_file.sv, rtl/core.sv, test/test_reg_file.py\\nOUTCOME: broken delta=null fmax=null cycles=null\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r7s1\\nTITLE: Registered instruction-response boundary\\nHYP: Insert one explicit IF/ID response register carrying PC, instruction, and\\nvalid. Capture it only when io_imemReady is asserted and decode can advance;\\nhold both it and the fetch PC through load-use or dmem stalls, and invalidate\\nit synchronously on an EX redirect. Decode and register-file reads then start\\nfrom local flops instead of directly from io_imemData, splitting the current\\nmemory-to-ID/EX timing cone without an autonomous request PC, FIFO occupancy\\narithmetic, or a new mux on io_imemAddr.\\n\\nThe added stage preserves one-instruction-per-cycle steady-state throughput\\nbut adds one wrong-path slot to redirect recovery. Retain the confirmed\\nbackward-branch replay path as an ID/EX injection so hot loop hits still\\nbypass the newly registered frontend slot, and explicitly discard any held\\nwrong-path response when replay or redirect wins. The expected routed-Fmax\\ngain from this clean pipeline cut should outweigh the modest extra cycles\\nfor non-replayed taken branches and jumps while preserving nret=1 retirement,\\nRVFI ordering, precise traps, and all bus-stall semantics.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/core_pkg.sv, rtl/if_stage.sv, rtl/id_stage.sv, rtl/hazard_unit.sv, rtl/core.sv, test/test_if_id_register.py\\nOUTCOME: broken delta=null fmax=null cycles=null\\n\\nID: hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\\nTITLE: Completion-decoupled divide execute island\\nHYP: Dispatch DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of ID/EX into a dedicated iterative\\nexecute island and immediately enter a registered divide mode. On dispatch,\\nallow ID/EX to capture exactly the single sequential successor already at\\ndecode, then hold that register and the fetch PC using the registered mode\\nbit until completion. Older EX/MEM work drains normally while the divider\\nruns; when both the result is ready and EX/MEM can accept it, inject one\\nsaved divide payload and result into EX/MEM, clear divide mode, and resume\\nthe held successor on the following cycle.\\n\\nThis completion-decoupled topology removes combinational div_busy/div_done\\nfeedback from hazard_unit and from the normal ALU-result path. Ordinary\\ninstructions see only the existing pipeline, while the divide island owns\\nits operands, RVFI metadata, corner-case state, and one completion-valid\\nbit. Keep the existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results on the normal\\npath so bounded formal depth is unchanged. The large divider-area reduction\\nobserved previously should then translate into routed-Fmax headroom without\\nthe high-fanout interlock that caused that experiment's regression.\\n\\nCHANGES: rtl/divider.sv, rtl/alu.sv, rtl/core_pkg.sv, rtl/ex_stage.sv, rtl/id_stage.sv, rtl/if_stage.sv, rtl/core.sv, test/test_divider.py\\nOUTCOME: regression delta=-10.27 fmax=164.55 cycles=4246317\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n10:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -ni 'collapse|remove.*mem.?wb|four.stage|4-stage|retire.*mem|writeback stage|MEM/WB' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -100\\nsed -n '1,240p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\\nls -la cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail -20\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -ni 'collapse|remove.*mem.?wb|four.stage|4-stage|retire.*mem|writeback stage|MEM/WB' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -100\\nsed -n '1,240p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\\nls -la cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses | tail -20\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:8:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (improvement, +6.22%): Replacing variable subword shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and constant store-mask decoding reduces FPGA LUT depth on the dmem-to-MEM/WB path while preserving load latency and RVFI memory semantics.\\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:10:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.07%): Adding a second 64-bit branch-PC/target tag and parallel candidate equality to a one-entry replay buffer can collapse routed FPGA Fmax even off io_imemAddr, so narrow CoreMark replacement coverage cannot repay wide tag-comparison and routing cost.\\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:12:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -23.48%): Even a registered late load-to-store bypass puts a MEM/WB tag compare and writeback-data mux directly on the dmem write-data path, so removing CoreMark's rs2-only load-use bubbles can lose more FPGA Fmax than it recovers in IPC.\\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Falling-edge register-file writeback is half a cycle too late for an ID instruction that captures operands on the same rising edge its producer reaches MEM/WB, so removing the WB-to-ID write-first bypass requires an interlock or an earlier architectural write point.\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:6:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Iterative shared divider with pipeline interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU hardware with one shared\\\\niterative radix-2 quotient/remainder unit. A divide-class instruction starts\\\\nthe unit from EX, remains resident in ID/EX, and interlocks PC, ID/EX, and\\\\nEX/MEM until the result is ready; it then advances exactly once through the\\\\nexisting retirement path. Signed operations use operand magnitudes plus final\\\\nquotient/remainder sign correction, with explicit divide-by-zero and\\\\nINT_MIN/-1 handling to retain exact RV32M semantics.\\\\n\\\\nOrdinary ALU and multiply instructions must bypass the divider with no new\\\\nstate or muxing on the fetch PC feedback path. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep\\\\nthe existing one-cycle algebraic stand-ins so the bounded formal checks retain\\\\ntheir current liveness depth; normal simulation and FPGA synthesis exercise\\\\nthe iterative engine. Removing inferred combinational division and sharing\\\\none subtract/shift datapath should reduce LUT and routing pressure enough to\\\\nraise median Fmax, while the added cycles affect only divide-class dynamic\\\\ninstructions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/divider.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_divider.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/divider.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_divider.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 299.19, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -26.08, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 5.79, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 134.39, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491817, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 5688, \\\"ff\\\": 2111, \\\"seeds\\\": [140.57, 129.47, 134.39], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T13:25:06.711843Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 2, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (regression, -26.08%): Feeding an iterative divider's div_wait into centralized hazard control makes rare DIV/REM operations add a high-fanout PC/IF-ID/ID-EX stall path to every cycle, so multi-cycle-unit interlocks must be localized or completion-decoupled to preserve FPGA Fmax.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\\\\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\\\\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\\\\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\\\\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\\\\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\\\\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\\\\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\\\\n\\\\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\\\\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\\\\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\\\\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\\\\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\\\\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 221.94, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -45.16, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": -21.53, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 93.97, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4234052, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 13089, \\\"ff\\\": 2982, \\\"seeds\\\": [93.97, 105.74, 93.41], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T13:23:20.366277Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 2, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Fast aligned-LW MEM-to-EX bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a fast forwarding path from the current MEM-stage aligned LW data directly\\\\nto the following instruction's EX operand mux. Suppress the load-use bubble\\\\nonly for legal aligned word loads; retain the existing one-cycle interlock for\\\\nbyte and halfword loads so their shift/sign-extension network is not placed in\\\\nthe EX critical path. The existing dmem-stall freeze remains authoritative: a\\\\ndependent instruction is held in ID/EX while io_dmemReady is low and advances\\\\nexactly once using io_dmemRData when readiness returns.\\\\n\\\\nReuse the existing EX/MEM forwarding select and substitute raw dmem read data\\\\nonly when the producer in EX/MEM is an aligned LW; all ALU producers continue\\\\nto forward the registered ALU result. This removes a common pipeline bubble\\\\nwith a single local result mux and modest hazard qualification, while leaving\\\\nretirement, RVFI memory fields, misalignment traps, subword semantics, and the\\\\nnret=1 interface unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose a combinational fast-forward value that selects raw dmem_rdata for a legal aligned word load and the registered ALU result otherwise, without changing load extraction or RVFI capture.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Route the MEM-stage fast-forward value into the existing EX/MEM operand-forwarding input and provide the hazard unit with the load width needed to distinguish fast LW dependencies from subword loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Suppress the load-use bubble for aligned word-load producers while retaining the current interlock for byte and halfword loads and preserving all redirect and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Document and use the existing EX/MEM forwarding input as a producer result that may come from same-cycle MEM-stage LW data rather than assuming it is always the registered ALU result.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_load_bypass.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks that dependent LW consumers execute without a bubble, subword consumers still interlock, and random dmem stalls hold the dependency until ready without stale data, duplicate writes, or duplicate retirement.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose a combinational fast-forward value that selects raw dmem_rdata for a legal aligned word load and the registered ALU result otherwise, without changing load extraction or RVFI capture.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Route the MEM-stage fast-forward value into the existing EX/MEM operand-forwarding input and provide the hazard unit with the load width needed to distinguish fast LW dependencies from subword loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Suppress the load-use bubble for aligned word-load producers while retaining the current interlock for byte and halfword loads and preserving all redirect and bus-stall priorities.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Document and use the existing EX/MEM forwarding input as a producer result that may come from same-cycle MEM-stage LW data rather than assuming it is always the registered ALU result.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_load_bypass.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed checks that dependent LW consumers execute without a bubble, subword consumers still interlock, and random dmem stalls hold the dependency until ready without stale data, duplicate writes, or duplicate retirement.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 301.72, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -25.45, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 6.68, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 131.93, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4372553, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10286, \\\"ff\\\": 1869, \\\"seeds\\\": [131.93, 123.33, 135.37], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T13:40:55.193247Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 3, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -25.45%): Forwarding raw io_dmemRData through the EX/MEM operand mux into the following instruction's ALU trades a load-use bubble for a dmem-read-to-EX combinational path whose FPGA timing cost outweighs the CoreMark CPI gain.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:11:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Explicit memory byte-lane selection\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The MEM stage currently extracts subword loads with a variable 32-bit right\\\\nshift and generates store masks with another variable shift. On a small FPGA,\\\\nthese expressions can infer wider mux networks than the four fixed byte lanes\\\\nrequire, adding LUT depth and routing to the dmem-to-MEM/WB timing path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The MEM stage currently extracts subword loads with a variable 32-bit right\\\\nshift and generates store masks with another variable shift. On a small FPGA,\\\\nthese expressions can infer wider mux networks than the four fixed byte lanes\\\\nrequire, adding LUT depth and routing to the dmem-to-MEM/WB timing path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the variable shifts with explicit address-lane case selection: a\\\\nfour-way 8-bit mux for byte loads, a two-way 16-bit mux for aligned halfword\\\\nloads, and directly decoded constant write masks. This preserves pipeline\\\\nlatency and all RVFI memory semantics while reducing the width and depth of\\\\nthe lane-selection logic. Because it does not forward raw dmem data into EX,\\\\nit avoids the long dmem-to-ALU path that regressed the prior load bypass.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace dynamic subword extraction and byte-mask shifts with explicit fixed-lane muxes and constant mask decoding while preserving alignment traps, sign extension, and RVFI data reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_mem_stage.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed byte and halfword load/store lane tests covering every legal offset, signed and unsigned extension, masks, and misalignment suppression.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace dynamic subword extraction and byte-mask shifts with explicit fixed-lane muxes and constant mask decoding while preserving alignment traps, sign extension, and RVFI data reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_mem_stage.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed byte and halfword load/store lane tests covering every legal offset, signed and unsigned extension, masks, and misalignment suppression.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 431.88, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 6.22, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 52.7, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 183.39, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4246355, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10435, \\\"ff\\\": 2037, \\\"seeds\\\": [184.03, 159.87, 183.39], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T14:07:45.613751Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 0, \\\"round_id\\\": 4, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (improvement, +6.22%): Replacing variable subword shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and constant store-mask decoding reduces FPGA LUT depth on the dmem-to-MEM/WB path while preserving load latency and RVFI memory semantics.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:12:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry ordered store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"A data-bus stall currently freezes every pipeline stage even when the blocked\\\\noperation is a store whose address and data are already complete. With the\\\\nevaluation's roughly 22% random dmem backpressure, those store stalls waste\\\\nexecution bandwidth and can combine with later instruction stalls. Earlier\\\\nstructural attempts added timing-sensitive paths to fetch or EX; buffering a\\\\ncompleted store confines the new state and arbitration to the MEM boundary.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"A data-bus stall currently freezes every pipeline stage even when the blocked\\\\noperation is a store whose address and data are already complete. With the\\\\nevaluation's roughly 22% random dmem backpressure, those store stalls waste\\\\nexecution bandwidth and can combine with later instruction stalls. Earlier\\\\nstructural attempts added timing-sensitive paths to fetch or EX; buffering a\\\\ncompleted store confines the new state and arbitration to the MEM boundary.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry write buffer for ordinary RAM stores. When a RAM store reaches\\\\nMEM while io_dmemReady is low, capture its address, replicated data, and byte\\\\nenables, allow that store to advance to retirement, and let following\\\\nnon-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains. If a younger load\\\\nor store reaches MEM before the entry drains, hold it behind the buffered\\\\nstore rather than adding load forwarding or permitting memory reordering.\\\\n\\\\nUART and timing-marker writes in the 0x10000000-0x10000200 window remain\\\\nunbuffered and retire only on an actual bus handshake. The buffered request\\\\nhas priority on the external dmem port, is issued exactly once, and may be\\\\nreplaced by a waiting RAM store only after the older entry is accepted. This\\\\nshould recover cycles from isolated store backpressure while leaving the ALU,\\\\noperand-forwarding, and instruction-address timing cones unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry RAM-store queue with capture, stable drain request, single-handshake completion, and explicit empty/full state.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Arbitrate buffered stores onto dmem, enqueue stalled ordinary-RAM stores, keep MMIO stores strongly ordered, and expose whether the current memory operation may advance.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace raw store backpressure freezing with a MEM-stage blocked indication so non-memory instructions can proceed while a queued store drains, while younger memory operations remain ordered.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the store-buffer advance/block controls into the existing pipeline hold network without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement ordering.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_store_buffer.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Check stalled-store capture and single issue, progress of following non-memory instructions, blocking of younger memory operations, random-stall ordering, and unbuffered UART/marker behavior.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-entry RAM-store queue with capture, stable drain request, single-handshake completion, and explicit empty/full state.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Arbitrate buffered stores onto dmem, enqueue stalled ordinary-RAM stores, keep MMIO stores strongly ordered, and expose whether the current memory operation may advance.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace raw store backpressure freezing with a MEM-stage blocked indication so non-memory instructions can proceed while a queued store drains, while younger memory operations remain ordered.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the store-buffer advance/block controls into the existing pipeline hold network without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement ordering.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_store_buffer.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Check stalled-store capture and single issue, progress of following non-memory instructions, blocking of younger memory operations, random-stall ordering, and unbuffered UART/marker behavior.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"placement_failed\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": \\\"placement_failed\\\", \\\"seeds\\\": [null, null, null], \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 4, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Muxing buffered versus live address, data, and byte enables onto the dmem port can break block-RAM inference for the 8 KiB wrapper memory, expanding it into 1,024 RAM16SDP4 cells and exceeding the FPGA's 648-cell placement capacity.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:13:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Hysteretic replacement for the loop replay entry\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry taken-target replay buffer improved fitness without placing a\\\\npredictor on the instruction-address timing cone, but every taken backward\\\\nbranch miss currently starts replacement of the resident entry. Interleaved\\\\nloop-closing branches can therefore evict the hottest replay entry before it\\\\nis reused, sharply limiting coverage despite the useful confirmed-replay\\\\nmechanism already being present.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry taken-target replay buffer improved fitness without placing a\\\\npredictor on the instruction-address timing cone, but every taken backward\\\\nbranch miss currently starts replacement of the resident entry. Interleaved\\\\nloop-closing branches can therefore evict the hottest replay entry before it\\\\nis reused, sharply limiting coverage despite the useful confirmed-replay\\\\nmechanism already being present.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Protect the valid replay entry with a one-candidate, two-strike replacement\\\\nfilter. A miss by a new backward branch records only its branch PC and target;\\\\na second miss by that same challenger authorizes the existing accepted-target\\\\nfill and replaces the resident instruction. A hit clears the challenger so a\\\\nfrequently reused resident entry is not displaced by isolated or alternating\\\\nmisses. Initial fill remains immediate when no valid resident exists.\\\\n\\\\nThis adds only a candidate tag and a small equality test in the EX-confirmed\\\\nupdate path. It neither widens the replay payload nor changes io_imemAddr,\\\\nsequential PC feedback, branch resolution, or RVFI behavior. By eliminating\\\\ndestructive one-miss thrashing, the same single cached target instruction\\\\nshould cover more CoreMark loop iterations with negligible routed-Fmax cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-candidate two-strike replacement filter: fill an invalid entry immediately, retain a valid hot entry after an isolated miss, and authorize replacement only when the same branch-PC/target challenger misses again; keep all fill and replay updates qualified by existing accepted-fetch and EX-advance conditions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed coverage for initial fill, isolated-miss protection, repeated-challenger replacement, challenger cancellation on resident hits, alternating branch sites, and accepted-fetch behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 2, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a one-candidate two-strike replacement filter: fill an invalid entry immediately, retain a valid hot entry after an isolated miss, and authorize replacement only when the same branch-PC/target challenger misses again; keep all fill and replay updates qualified by existing accepted-fetch and EX-advance conditions.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_predictor.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed coverage for initial fill, isolated-miss protection, repeated-challenger replacement, challenger cancellation on resident hits, alternating branch sites, and accepted-fetch behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 316.84, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -22.07, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 12.03, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 134.28, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4238065, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10427, \\\"ff\\\": 2088, \\\"seeds\\\": [134.28, 132.21, 150.08], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T14:03:49.758560Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 4, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.07%): Adding a second 64-bit branch-PC/target tag and parallel candidate equality to a one-entry replay buffer can collapse routed FPGA Fmax even off io_imemAddr, so narrow CoreMark replacement coverage cannot repay wide tag-comparison and routing cost.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Late store-data forwarding at the MEM boundary\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The pipeline currently treats both encoded source fields of every following\\\\ninstruction as EX-stage consumers. In particular, a store immediately after\\\\na load stalls when the load destination is only the store's rs2 data, even\\\\nthough that value is not required until the store reaches MEM one cycle\\\\nlater. CoreMark performs frequent memory copies and structure updates, so\\\\nthese conservative load-to-store bubbles reduce iterations per cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The pipeline currently treats both encoded source fields of every following\\\\ninstruction as EX-stage consumers. In particular, a store immediately after\\\\na load stalls when the load destination is only the store's rs2 data, even\\\\nthough that value is not required until the store reaches MEM one cycle\\\\nlater. CoreMark performs frequent memory copies and structure updates, so\\\\nthese conservative load-to-store bubbles reduce iterations per cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move the architectural consumption point for store rs2 data from EX to MEM.\\\\nPermit a store whose address is independent of the preceding load to enter\\\\nEX without a load-use bubble, carry its rs2 register tag with the existing\\\\nEX/MEM payload, and select the registered MEM/WB result as store data in MEM\\\\nwhen the tags match. The normal EX forwarding path remains the source for\\\\nall other store dependencies, and a dependency through store rs1 still\\\\nstalls because the address is needed in EX.\\\\n\\\\nThis is a pipeline-topology change rather than a raw load bypass: load data\\\\ncrosses the existing MEM/WB register before it reaches the store-data mux,\\\\nso no io_dmemRData-to-EX combinational path is introduced. Only write data,\\\\nnot address or byte enables, gains a late registered bypass, preserving the\\\\nwrapper RAM's simple address/control inference. Qualifying the load-use\\\\ninterlock for store rs2 should recover bubbles with much less routed-Fmax\\\\nrisk than the prior aligned-LW EX bypass or ordered store buffer.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Recognize a following STORE and suppress a load-use stall only when the match is exclusively on store rs2; retain the stall for store-address rs1 dependencies and all ordinary EX consumers.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Provide the next-instruction store classification to hazard control and route the registered MEM/WB destination and result into the MEM-stage late store-data bypass without changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Select store write data and RVFI rs2/mem_wdata from the registered MEM/WB result on a matching nonzero rs2 tag, retaining the selected value and tag-correct behavior across dmem backpressure while leaving dmem address and byte-enable generation unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed load-to-store tests proving that an rs2-only dependency has no bubble, an rs1 address dependency still stalls, byte and halfword stores use the forwarded value, and random dmem stalls neither lose nor duplicate the store or retirement.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Recognize a following STORE and suppress a load-use stall only when the match is exclusively on store rs2; retain the stall for store-address rs1 dependencies and all ordinary EX consumers.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Provide the next-instruction store classification to hazard control and route the registered MEM/WB destination and result into the MEM-stage late store-data bypass without changing the nret=1 interface.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Select store write data and RVFI rs2/mem_wdata from the registered MEM/WB result on a matching nonzero rs2 tag, retaining the selected value and tag-correct behavior across dmem backpressure while leaving dmem address and byte-enable generation unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add directed load-to-store tests proving that an rs2-only dependency has no bubble, an rs1 address dependency still stalls, byte and halfword stores use the forwarded value, and random dmem stalls neither lose nor duplicate the store or retirement.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"regression\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 330.47, \\\"delta_pct\\\": -23.48, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 16.85, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 140.33, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4246318, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 10660, \\\"ff\\\": 2024, \\\"seeds\\\": [143.2, 140.33, 137.21], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-07-11T14:38:20.977237Z\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 1, \\\"round_id\\\": 5, \\\"lesson\\\": \\\"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -23.48%): Even a registered late load-to-store bypass puts a MEM/WB tag compare and writeback-data mux directly on the dmem write-data path, so removing CoreMark's rs2-only load-use bubbles can lose more FPGA Fmax than it recovers in IPC.\\\"}\\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:18:{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Half-cycle register-file writeback\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The asynchronous register-file read path currently includes, on both ports,\\\\na write-enable gate, a five-bit WB address comparison, and a 32-bit bypass\\\\nmux. The reset-valid-mask experiment showed that even a small extra select on\\\\nthese read paths substantially damages routed Fmax, identifying them as a\\\\nsensitive structural boundary. Removing the existing bypass logic could\\\\ntherefore raise frequency without changing CoreMark's instruction count or\\\\nplacing new logic on the fetch, execute, or memory interfaces.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The asynchronous register-file read path currently includes, on both ports,\\\\na write-enable gate, a five-bit WB address comparison, and a 32-bit bypass\\\\nmux. The reset-valid-mask experiment showed that even a small extra select on\\\\nthese read paths substantially damages routed Fmax, identifying them as a\\\\nsensitive structural boundary. Removing the existing bypass logic could\\\\ntherefore raise frequency without changing CoreMark's instruction count or\\\\nplacing new logic on the fetch, execute, or memory interfaces.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Clock architectural register-file writes on the falling edge while retaining\\\\nID/EX operand capture on the rising edge. A value presented by MEM/WB after a\\\\nrising edge will then be committed half a cycle later and will already be in\\\\nthe array before the following rising-edge ID capture. This preserves the\\\\ncurrent same-cycle WB-to-ID dependency behavior while allowing both read\\\\nports to become direct x0-or-array selections with no WB tag comparison or\\\\nwrite-data bypass mux.\\\\n\\\\nThe MEM/WB-to-register-file write path is short and registered at its source,\\\\nso it should fit the half-cycle constraint more comfortably than the current\\\\nregister-read-to-ID/EX path fits the full-cycle critical cone. Pipeline\\\\nhazards, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, retirement order, RVFI data, bus-stall\\\\nbehavior, and the nret=1 interface remain unchanged. The implementation must\\\\nverify reset and held-MEM/WB cases explicitly so a retirement writes exactly\\\\nonce and x0 remains hardwired to zero.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Move the single architectural write port to the falling clock edge and remove same-cycle WB address comparisons and data bypass muxes from both combinational read ports, while preserving reset clearing and x0 semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Document the half-cycle WB-to-register-file timing contract and keep the existing MEM/WB write controls stable across the falling-edge commit point without changing pipeline or top-level interfaces.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-accurate checks for falling-edge writes, direct reads, x0 suppression, reset behavior, back-to-back writes, and WB-to-ID dependencies including instruction- and data-bus stalls.\\\"}]}, \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Move the single architectural write port to the falling clock edge and remove same-cycle WB address comparisons and data bypass muxes from both combinational read ports, while preserving reset clearing and x0 semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Document the half-cycle WB-to-register-file timing contract and keep the existing MEM/WB write controls stable across the falling-edge commit point without changing pipeline or top-level interfaces.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add cycle-accurate checks for falling-edge writes, direct reads, x0 suppression, reset behavior, back-to-back writes, and WB-to-ID dependencies including instruction- and data-bus stalls.\\\"}], \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\\\n52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:05  Status: failed\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]