{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,280p' cores/v1/rtl/if_stage.sv\nsed -n '60,125p' cores/v1/rtl/core_pkg.sv\nsed -n '105,205p' cores/v1/rtl/ex_stage.sv\nfind cores -path '*/experiments/log.jsonl' -type f -maxdepth 4 -print\"","aggregated_output":"// rtl/if_stage.sv\n//\n// Instruction fetch stage. Holds the PC register; the IF/ID payload\n// (pc + instr + valid) is *combinational* — there is no separate IF/ID\n// flop in this microarchitecture, the next-stage's ID/EX register\n// captures everything one cycle later.\n//\n// On flush or EX redirect, the instruction emitted to ID is forced to NOP\n// (`0x00000013` = ADDI x0,x0,0). This prevents the hazard unit from\n// observing a real rs1/rs2 from a wrong-path instruction and inserting\n// a spurious load-use stall after mispredict recovery.\n//\n// A decode-light static predictor recognizes legal B-type conditional\n// branches with a negative immediate and direct JALs, provided the predicted\n// target is word-aligned. JALR remains unpredicted and resolves in EX.\n// A two-bank direct-mapped replay table hides external imem stalls only\n// through a registered lookahead candidate for the current PC; the IF\n// decision path never reads or compares the table combinationally. Each\n// bank has 128 entries addressed by pc[8:2]. Entries store pc[31:9], a\n// valid bit, and the instruction word; a per-index MRU bit chooses which\n// bank to replace when neither bank already contains the fetched tag.\n//\n// Latency:        PC-reg update is synchronous; output is combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_rdata (via ID/EX/MEM/WB) and pc_wdata (via\n//                 EX-stage redirect).\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic              clock,\n  input  logic              reset,\n  input  logic              stall,            // hold PC (load-use)\n  input  logic              flush,            // emit NOP into ID this cycle\n  input  logic              redirect,         // EX has resolved a branch/jump\n  input  logic [31:0]       redirect_target,\n  output logic [31:0]       imem_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0]       imem_data,\n  input  logic              imem_ready,\n  output logic              fetch_ready,\n  output if_id_t  out\n);\n\n  localparam logic [31:0] RESET_PC = 32'h0000_0000;\n  localparam logic [31:0] NOP      = 32'h0000_0013;\n  localparam int unsigned REPLAY_INDEX_BITS = 7;\n  localparam int unsigned REPLAY_ENTRIES    = 1 << REPLAY_INDEX_BITS;\n  localparam int unsigned REPLAY_TAG_HI_LSB = REPLAY_INDEX_BITS + 2;\n  localparam int unsigned REPLAY_TAG_HI_BITS = 32 - REPLAY_TAG_HI_LSB;\n\n  logic [31:0] pc;\n  logic [31:0] next_pc;\n  logic [31:0] pc_plus4;\n  logic [31:0] fetch_instr;\n  logic [31:0] branch_imm;\n  logic [31:0] jal_imm;\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\n  logic [31:0] jal_target;\n  logic [31:0] predicted_target;\n  logic        fetch_kill;\n  logic        predict_enable;\n  logic        branch_opcode;\n  logic        branch_funct_legal;\n  logic        branch_predict_taken;\n  logic        jal_opcode;\n  logic        jal_predict_taken;\n  logic        predicted_taken;\n\n  logic                              replay_valid_b0 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic                              replay_valid_b1 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic [REPLAY_TAG_HI_BITS-1:0]     replay_tag_hi_b0 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic [REPLAY_TAG_HI_BITS-1:0]     replay_tag_hi_b1 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_instr_b0 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_instr_b1 [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic                              replay_mru [0:REPLAY_ENTRIES-1];\n  logic [REPLAY_INDEX_BITS-1:0]      replay_fill_idx;\n  logic [REPLAY_INDEX_BITS-1:0]      replay_lookup_idx;\n  logic [REPLAY_TAG_HI_BITS-1:0]     replay_fill_tag_hi;\n  logic [REPLAY_TAG_HI_BITS-1:0]     replay_lookup_tag_hi;\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_lookup_pc;\n  logic                              replay_fill_bank0_match;\n  logic                              replay_fill_bank1_match;\n  logic                              replay_fill_bank;\n  logic                              replay_lookup_bank0_hit;\n  logic                              replay_lookup_bank1_hit;\n  logic                              replay_lookup_hit;\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_lookup_instr;\n  logic                              replay_cand_valid_q;\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_cand_pc_q;\n  logic [31:0]                       replay_cand_instr_q;\n  logic                              replay_current_hit;\n  logic                              replay_fill_bypass;\n\n  assign replay_fill_idx    = pc[REPLAY_INDEX_BITS+1:2];\n  assign replay_lookup_idx  = replay_lookup_pc[REPLAY_INDEX_BITS+1:2];\n  assign replay_fill_tag_hi = pc[31:REPLAY_TAG_HI_LSB];\n  assign replay_lookup_tag_hi = replay_lookup_pc[31:REPLAY_TAG_HI_LSB];\n  assign replay_current_hit = replay_cand_valid_q && (replay_cand_pc_q == pc);\n  assign fetch_ready        = imem_ready || replay_current_hit;\n  assign fetch_instr        = imem_ready         ? imem_data :\n                              replay_current_hit ? replay_cand_instr_q :\n                                                   NOP;\n  assign replay_fill_bank0_match = replay_valid_b0[replay_fill_idx]\n                                && (replay_tag_hi_b0[replay_fill_idx] == replay_fill_tag_hi);\n  assign replay_fill_bank1_match = replay_valid_b1[replay_fill_idx]\n                                && (replay_tag_hi_b1[replay_fill_idx] == replay_fill_tag_hi);\n  assign replay_fill_bank = replay_fill_bank0_match ? 1'b0 :\n                            replay_fill_bank1_match ? 1'b1 :\n                                                       ~replay_mru[replay_fill_idx];\n  assign replay_lookup_bank0_hit = replay_valid_b0[replay_lookup_idx]\n                                && (replay_tag_hi_b0[replay_lookup_idx] == replay_lookup_tag_hi);\n  assign replay_lookup_bank1_hit = replay_valid_b1[replay_lookup_idx]\n                                && (replay_tag_hi_b1[replay_lookup_idx] == replay_lookup_tag_hi);\n  assign replay_lookup_hit = replay_lookup_bank0_hit || replay_lookup_bank1_hit;\n  assign replay_lookup_instr = replay_lookup_bank0_hit ? replay_instr_b0[replay_lookup_idx]\n                                                       : replay_instr_b1[replay_lookup_idx];\n\n  always_comb begin\n    pc_plus4           = pc + 32'd4;\n    branch_imm         = {{19{fetch_instr[31]}}, fetch_instr[31], fetch_instr[7],\n                          fetch_instr[30:25], fetch_instr[11:8], 1'b0};\n    jal_imm            = {{11{fetch_instr[31]}}, fetch_instr[31], fetch_instr[19:12],\n                          fetch_instr[20], fetch_instr[30:21], 1'b0};\n    branch_target      = pc + branch_imm;\n    jal_target         = pc + jal_imm;\n    fetch_kill         = reset || flush || redirect;\n    predict_enable     = !fetch_kill && !stall;\n    branch_opcode      = (fetch_instr[6:0] == 7'b1100011);\n    branch_funct_legal = (fetch_instr[14:12] != 3'd2) && (fetch_instr[14:12] != 3'd3);\n    branch_predict_taken = predict_enable\n                       && branch_opcode\n                       && branch_funct_legal\n                       && fetch_instr[31]\n                       && (branch_target[1:0] == 2'b00);\n    jal_opcode         = (fetch_instr[6:0] == 7'b1101111);\n    jal_predict_taken  = predict_enable\n                       && jal_opcode\n                       && (jal_target[1:0] == 2'b00);\n    predicted_taken    = branch_predict_taken || jal_predict_taken;\n    predicted_target   = jal_predict_taken ? jal_target : branch_target;\n\n    next_pc = redirect        ? redirect_target :\n              predicted_taken ? predicted_target :\n                                pc_plus4;\n    replay_lookup_pc = redirect ? redirect_target :\n                       !stall   ? next_pc :\n                                  pc;\n    replay_fill_bypass = imem_ready\n                       && (replay_fill_idx == replay_lookup_idx)\n                       && (replay_fill_tag_hi == replay_lookup_tag_hi);\n  end\n\n  // Redirect must override stall: a BRANCH/JAL/JALR in EX may fire\n  // redirect on the same cycle as imem_stall or dmem_stall — without\n  // this priority the redirect target would be silently dropped, the\n  // PC would hold its old (wrong-path) value, and execution would\n  // resume on the wrong path once the bus unstalls. Verified by the\n  // VexRiscv-binary CoreMark sweep with --istall enabled.\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if      (reset)    pc <= RESET_PC;\n    else if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\n    else if (!stall)   pc <= next_pc;\n  end\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      replay_cand_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n      for (int i = 0; i < REPLAY_ENTRIES; i++) begin\n        replay_valid_b0[i] <= 1'b0;\n        replay_valid_b1[i] <= 1'b0;\n        replay_mru[i]      <= 1'b0;\n      end\n    end else begin\n      if (imem_ready) begin\n        if (replay_fill_bank) begin\n          replay_valid_b1[replay_fill_idx] <= 1'b1;\n          replay_tag_hi_b1[replay_fill_idx] <= replay_fill_tag_hi;\n          replay_instr_b1[replay_fill_idx] <= imem_data;\n        end else begin\n          replay_valid_b0[replay_fill_idx] <= 1'b1;\n          replay_tag_hi_b0[replay_fill_idx] <= replay_fill_tag_hi;\n          replay_instr_b0[replay_fill_idx] <= imem_data;\n        end\n        replay_mru[replay_fill_idx] <= replay_fill_bank;\n      end\n\n      replay_cand_pc_q <= replay_lookup_pc;\n      if (replay_fill_bypass) begin\n        replay_cand_valid_q <= 1'b1;\n        replay_cand_instr_q <= imem_data;\n      end else begin\n        replay_cand_valid_q <= replay_lookup_hit;\n        replay_cand_instr_q <= replay_lookup_instr;\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  assign imem_addr = pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    out.pc               = pc;\n    out.instr            = fetch_kill ? NOP : fetch_instr;\n    out.predicted_taken  = predicted_taken;\n    out.predicted_target = predicted_taken ? predicted_target : pc_plus4;\n    out.valid            = !fetch_kill;\n  end\n\nendmodule\n    logic [2:0] branch_op;\n    logic       is_branch;\n    logic       is_jump;\n    logic       is_jalr;\n    logic       is_lui;\n    logic       is_auipc;\n    logic       mem_read;\n    logic       mem_write;\n    logic [1:0] mem_width;   // 0 = byte, 1 = half, 2 = word\n    logic       mem_sext;    // sign-extend load result\n    logic       reg_write;\n    logic       mem_to_reg;  // 1 = write loaded data, 0 = write ALU result\n    logic       is_illegal;  // default-true in decoder; cleared inside\n                              // validated opcode/funct arms only.\n  } ctrl_t;\n\n  // IF -> ID combinational bundle (no register; PC reg sits in if_stage).\n  // instr may be the live imem word or a word supplied by IF's registered\n  // lookahead replay predictor during an external imem stall. The replay\n  // predictor uses two direct-mapped 128-entry banks indexed by pc[8:2],\n  // tagged by pc[31:9], and replaced with a per-index MRU bit. Tag and\n  // instruction payload arrays are resetless behind bank valid bits.\n  // predicted_* is generic next-PC prediction metadata for any IF-resolved\n  // control-flow instruction, not branch-only state.\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic [31:0] pc;\n    logic [31:0] instr;\n    logic        predicted_taken;\n    logic [31:0] predicted_target;\n    logic        valid;\n  } if_id_t;\n\n  // ID/EX register payload.\n  // predicted_* carries the IF next-PC prediction, computed from the selected\n  // live/replayed instruction word, into EX for validation.\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic [31:0] pc;\n    logic [31:0] rs1_val;\n    logic [31:0] rs2_val;\n    logic [31:0] imm;\n    logic [4:0]  rd;\n    logic [4:0]  rs1_addr;\n    logic [4:0]  rs2_addr;\n    ctrl_t       ctrl;\n    logic [31:0] instr;\n    logic        predicted_taken;\n    logic [31:0] predicted_target;\n    logic        valid;\n  } id_ex_t;\n\n  // EX/MEM register payload.\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic [31:0] pc;\n    logic [31:0] alu_result;\n    logic [31:0] write_data;     // raw rs2 (post-forward), pre byte replication\n    logic [4:0]  rd;\n    logic [4:0]  rs1_addr;\n    logic [4:0]  rs2_addr;\n    logic [31:0] rs1_val;        // post-forward rs1 used by EX\n    logic [31:0] rs2_val;        // post-forward rs2 used by EX\n    logic [31:0] pc_next;        // resolved next-PC (target / pc+4)\n    logic        branch_taken;\n    logic [31:0] branch_target;\n    ctrl_t       ctrl;\n    logic [31:0] instr;\n    logic        valid;\n    .done   (div_done),\n    .result (div_result_w)\n  );\n\n  // ── Branch resolve ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  logic        branch_cond;\n  logic        branch_taken;\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\n  logic [31:0] branch_next_pc;\n  logic [31:0] predicted_next_pc;\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  logic [31:0] jalr_sum;  // bit 0 deliberately dropped per RV JALR spec\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n\n  always_comb begin\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\n      default: branch_cond = 1'b0;\n    endcase\n    branch_taken  = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_cond;\n    branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;\n    branch_next_pc = branch_taken ? branch_target : (in.pc + 32'd4);\n    predicted_next_pc = in.predicted_taken ? in.predicted_target\n                                           : (in.pc + 32'd4);\n    // JALR clears bit 0 (RV spec); JAL uses imm directly.\n    jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;\n    jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}\n                                  : (in.pc + in.imm);\n  end\n\n  // ── Misaligned branch / jump target trap ──────────────────────────────\n  // riscv-formal's spec demands rvfi_trap=1 when next_pc is misaligned\n  // (without C extension that means [1:0] != 0). We trap the offending\n  // instruction, suppress the redirect (PC stays linear), and clear\n  // reg_write so JAL/JALR don't write the return address on trap.\n  logic misalign_branch;\n  logic misalign_jump;\n  logic misalign_fault;\n  logic branch_mispredict;\n  logic jump_redirect;\n  logic jal_mispredict;\n  ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    misalign_branch = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_taken\n                      && (branch_target[1:0] != 2'b00);\n    misalign_jump   = in.ctrl.is_jump && (jump_target[1:0] != 2'b00);\n    misalign_fault  = misalign_branch || misalign_jump;\n\n    ctrl_with_trap = in.ctrl;\n    if (misalign_fault) begin\n      ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal = 1'b1;\n      ctrl_with_trap.reg_write  = 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    branch_mispredict = in.valid\n                     && in.ctrl.is_branch\n                     && !misalign_fault\n                     && (branch_next_pc != predicted_next_pc);\n    jal_mispredict    = in.ctrl.is_jump\n                     && !in.ctrl.is_jalr\n                     && (jump_target != predicted_next_pc);\n    jump_redirect     = in.valid\n                     && in.ctrl.is_jump\n                     && !misalign_fault\n                     && (in.ctrl.is_jalr || jal_mispredict);\n  end\n\n  assign redirect        = branch_mispredict || jump_redirect;\n  assign redirect_target = in.ctrl.is_jump ? jump_target : branch_next_pc;\n\n  // ── EX/MEM register ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  ex_mem_t reg_q;\n\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      div_active_q       <= 1'b0;\n      div_result_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n      div_result_q       <= 32'b0;\n      div_in_q           <= '0;\n      div_rs1_q          <= 32'b0;\n      div_rs2_q          <= 32'b0;\n    end else begin\n      if (div_start) begin\n        div_active_q       <= 1'b1;\n        div_result_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n        div_result_q       <= 32'b0;\n        div_in_q           <= in;\n        div_rs1_q          <= rs1;\n        div_rs2_q          <= rs2;\n      end else if (div_complete && !stall) begin\n        div_active_q       <= 1'b0;\n        div_result_valid_q <= 1'b0;\n      end else if (div_done && div_active_q) begin\n        div_result_q       <= div_result_w;\ncores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"if [ -f cores/v1/LESSONS.md ]; then sed -n '1,240p' cores/v1/LESSONS.md; fi\nrg -n -i 'predict|branch|btb|bht|replay|ras' cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -40\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"if [ -f cores/v1/LESSONS.md ]; then sed -n '1,240p' cores/v1/LESSONS.md; fi\nrg -n -i 'predict|branch|btb|bht|replay|ras' cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -40\"","aggregated_output":"35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-028-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Byte-Segmented RVFI Order Counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from a pure timing micro-optimization: replacing\\nthe monolithic 64-bit RVFI order increment with four 16-bit counter chunks.\\nThat change did not alter CoreMark cycles, memory behavior, branch\\nprediction, divider latency, or retirement semantics, but it raised median\\nFmax enough to become the new best fitness. This strongly suggests the\\nRVFI-order feedback path is still visible to synthesis and placement because\\nthe FPGA wrapper keeps RVFI outputs alive.\\n\\nThe current implementation still has a 16-bit carry-chain self-loop in the\\nlow order chunk, and each retirement toggles that chunk. A narrower exact\\nchunk size is the most direct follow-up in the micro_opt slot: it targets the\\nsame measured timing structure that just paid off, avoids another datapath or\\npredictor rewrite, and should leave CoreMark iterations/cycle unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the RVFI order counter in `rtl/core.sv` from four 16-bit chunks to\\neight 8-bit chunks. The lowest byte increments on every `mem_wb_w.valid`\\nretirement. Each higher byte increments only when every lower byte is\\n`8'hff` on that same retirement cycle. Concatenating the bytes still drives\\nthe exact 64-bit `io_rvfi_order_0` value, reset still produces zero, and the\\nvisible sequence still advances by exactly one per channel-0 retirement.\\n\\nThis preserves CLAUDE.md invariant 4 exactly: no skipped order values, no\\nduplicate order values, and no change to the single-retire channel-0 /\\nchannel-1-tied-off RVFI contract. It also leaves the top-level IO, MEM/WB\\nretirement point, store slot, divider interlock, branch predictor, memory\\nhandshake behavior, and CoreMark cycle count unchanged. The expected gain is\\na small additional Fmax improvement by shortening the always-active low-order\\ncounter carry path from 16 bits to 8 bits while keeping the upper-byte carry\\nenables narrow and exact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four 16-bit `rvfi_order_*_q` registers and carry signals\\nwith eight 8-bit order counter registers. Increment byte 0 on each\\nretirement and conditionally increment bytes 1 through 7 only on exact\\nlower-byte overflow. Drive `io_rvfi_order_0` from the concatenated bytes\\nin the same 64-bit order as today, preserve reset-to-zero behavior, and\\ndo not change any RVFI field names, widths, channel ordering, channel-1\\ntie-off, MEM/WB retirement logic, or pipeline control.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four 16-bit `rvfi_order_*_q` registers and carry signals\\nwith eight 8-bit order counter registers. Increment byte 0 on each\\nretirement and conditionally increment bytes 1 through 7 only on exact\\nlower-byte overflow. Drive `io_rvfi_order_0` from the concatenated bytes\\nin the same 64-bit order as today, preserve reset-to-zero behavior, and\\ndo not change any RVFI field names, widths, channel ordering, channel-1\\ntie-off, MEM/WB retirement logic, or pipeline control.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 373.27, \"delta_pct\": -11.71, \"vs_baseline\": 23.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.75, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4226169, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5926, \"ff\": 2221, \"seeds\": [163.29, 149.5, 157.75], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Replaced the four 16-bit RVFI order counter registers in `rtl/core.sv`\\n  with eight explicit 8-bit byte registers.\\n- Byte 0 increments on every `mem_wb_w.valid` retirement.\\n- Bytes 1 through 7 increment only when `mem_wb_w.valid` is asserted and\\n  every lower byte is `8'hff` in that same cycle.\\n- `io_rvfi_order_0` is still driven by the concatenated 64-bit counter value,\\n  now ordered `{b7, b6, b5, b4, b3, b2, b1, b0}`.\\n- Reset behavior remains all-zero. No RVFI field names, widths, channel\\n  ordering, channel-1 tie-off, pipeline control, memory, branch, divider, or\\n  writeback logic changed.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No functional deviations from the hypothesis. I kept the byte counter logic\\n  inline in `rtl/core.sv` to match the existing segmented-counter style.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The direct in-place `bash formal/run_all.sh` command could not stage files in\\n  this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared checkout\\n  outside the writable worktree; the script failed before running any proof\\n  checks with `Operation not permitted` while removing staged `.sv` files.\\n- To verify the RTL anyway, I ran the same script from a temporary copy of the\\n  worktree with the `formal/riscv-formal` symlink dereferenced into `/tmp`.\\n\\n## Local verification status\\n\\n- Lint: pass\\n  - `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`\\n- Formal: pass in writable temporary worktree\\n  - `bash formal/run_all.sh`\\n  - Final tally: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`\\n  - No formal counterexample was observed.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:01:51.815239\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12}\n36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-029-r12s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already captured the two strongest proven wins:\\ncold DIV/REM is off the universal EX path, and the one-deep pending-store\\nslot hides store-only dmem backpressure while preserving the ready-store fast\\npath. The next structural bottleneck is therefore load-side dmem stalls. A\\nload whose word was recently read still freezes the whole pipeline whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even though CoreMark repeatedly touches a compact 6 KB\\nworking set across list, matrix, state, stack, and CRC code.\\n\\nInstruction replay experiments showed that serving repeated hot accesses\\nduring random bus stalls can cut many cycles, but putting a wide replay table\\nin IF cost too much Fmax. A smaller load replay structure in MEM attacks the\\nsame random-stall problem on the data side, where the lookup is off the\\nbranch/JAL predictor path and does not add load data to the EX operand muxes.\\nThis is a structural stall-hiding change, not a broader predictor or\\narithmetic rewrite.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay buffer in the MEM stage, for example a\\n16-entry direct-mapped table indexed by word address. Each entry holds a\\nvalid bit, word tag, and the raw 32-bit memory word returned by a previous\\nlegal aligned or byte/halfword load. On `io_dmemReady=1`, loads keep using\\nthe live `io_dmemRData` exactly as today and refresh the replay entry for the\\naligned word address. On `io_dmemReady=0`, a legal normal-RAM load may\\ncomplete from the replay buffer on a valid tag hit: suppress the external\\ndmem read, use the cached raw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI\\n`mem_rdata`, and do not assert `mem_wait`.\\n\\nReplay is deliberately conservative. It is disabled while the pending-store\\nslot is valid, for MMIO/marker/UART addresses, for trapping or misaligned\\naccesses, and for stores. Normal-RAM stores invalidate or update the matching\\nreplay entry before they retire or enqueue into the pending-store slot, so a\\nlater replayed load cannot observe stale data. Replay misses preserve the\\ncurrent dmem wait behavior exactly. The top-level IO, single-retire RVFI\\nchannel-0 convention, tied-off channel 1, divider interlock, branch\\nprediction, and pending-store ordering all remain unchanged. The expected win\\nis lower CoreMark cycle count under the 22% random dStall model with much\\nless Fmax risk than MEM-to-EX load forwarding or IF replay caches.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide lookup-hit/data outputs for legal load word\\naddresses, refresh entries from live dmem load responses, and invalidate\\nor byte-merge-update matching entries on legal normal-RAM stores. Reset\\nclears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the access is a legal non-trapping\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the replay tag\\nhits. On a replay hit, gate off `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, drive\\nload-data extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached word, and keep\\nreplay misses plus all nonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire any replay-buffer module instance or MEM-stage sideband signals\\nneeded for store invalidation/update and dmem request gating. Preserve\\nthe existing pending-store-slot arbitration, top-level core IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port layout, strict channel-0 order sequence, and tied-off\\nchannel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if needed to document\\nnormal-RAM replay eligibility and raw-word cache metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide lookup-hit/data outputs for legal load word\\naddresses, refresh entries from live dmem load responses, and invalidate\\nor byte-merge-update matching entries on legal normal-RAM stores. Reset\\nclears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the access is a legal non-trapping\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the replay tag\\nhits. On a replay hit, gate off `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, drive\\nload-data extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached word, and keep\\nreplay misses plus all nonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire any replay-buffer module instance or MEM-stage sideband signals\\nneeded for store invalidation/update and dmem request gating. Preserve\\nthe existing pending-store-slot arbitration, top-level core IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port layout, strict channel-0 order sequence, and tied-off\\nchannel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if needed to document\\nnormal-RAM replay eligibility and raw-word cache metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 442.54, \"delta_pct\": 4.68, \"vs_baseline\": 47.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 184.6, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4171413, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5925, \"ff\": 2229, \"seeds\": [169.66, 193.35, 184.6], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What Changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/load_replay.sv`, a 16-entry direct-mapped load replay table keyed by aligned word address. Each entry stores a valid bit, word tag, and raw 32-bit memory word. Reset clears valid bits.\\n- Updated `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to refresh the table from accepted normal-RAM loads when `dmem_ready` is high.\\n- Updated `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to replay legal normal-RAM loads only when `dmem_ready` is low, no pending store owns the port, and the replay tag hits. Replay hits suppress `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, and use the cached raw word for load extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata`.\\n- Updated normal-RAM store handling to byte-merge stores into an existing matching replay entry before the store retires or enqueues into the pending-store slot.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No `rtl/core.sv` wiring was needed; the replay table is instantiated inside `mem_stage` because all eligibility, load data, and store-update sideband signals are local there.\\n- Stores update existing matching entries but do not allocate new entries. This avoids fabricating a full cached word for sub-word stores when no previous load populated the word.\\n- The checked-in `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not stage files because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a read-only location outside this writable sandbox. I mirrored the riscv-formal framework into `/tmp` and ran the same generated checks from there.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The replay table is intentionally conservative and only helps words already populated by accepted loads. It will not create hits from store-only hot data.\\n- No top-level IO, RVFI channel layout, branch path, divider interlock, or pending-store arbitration contract was changed.\\n\\n## Local Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before checks by read-only staging cleanup under `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher`.\\n- Mirrored formal run from `/tmp/riscv-formal-codex.itXm55` using the same `formal/checks.cfg` and generated file list: 105 passed, 0 failed.\\n- Formal fix attempts used: 0.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:07:18.650889\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12}\n37:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-030-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is still the simple static frontend:\\nbackward conditional branches and direct JAL targets are predicted in IF.\\nBroader learned branch tables and return predictors reduced a few control\\nbubbles, but their IF reads, compares, and EX update routing lost more Fmax\\nthan the cycle savings recovered.\\n\\nThe only predictor-family experiment with a large cycle signal was the\\nstall-only instruction replay table. The 256-entry replay version reduced\\nCoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314 by serving hot instructions\\nduring random imem stalls, but its combinational tag/data lookup sat directly\\non the current fetch-ready and predictor path and pulled median Fmax below\\nthe current best. Smaller combinational replay tables kept much of the same\\ntiming problem while giving up hit rate.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore not another direction table\\nor return predictor, but a timing-clean replay predictor: keep the large\\niStall-hiding benefit, while moving the table lookup off the same-cycle IF\\ndecision path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a registered lookahead instruction replay predictor in `if_stage`. Keep a\\ndirect-mapped replay table, preferably 128 or 256 entries indexed by fetch PC,\\nwith valid bits, full PC tags, and 32-bit instruction data. On every live\\n`io_imemReady=1` fetch, use `io_imemData` exactly as the current best RTL\\ndoes and refresh that PC's replay entry.\\n\\nSeparately, precompute and register the replay lookup for the PC expected to\\nbe fetched on the next cycle. The current cycle should consume only the\\nregistered `{hit, pc_tag, instr}` candidate for the current PC when\\n`io_imemReady=0`; it should not perform a fresh table read and tag compare in\\nthe same combinational path that drives `fetch_ready`, branch/JAL prediction,\\nand the PC update. On a registered replay hit during an external imem stall,\\ntreat fetch as effectively ready and feed the cached instruction into the\\nexisting backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor metadata path. On a replay\\nmiss, preserve the current PC-hold and IF-bubble behavior.\\n\\nRedirects and reset still flush only the in-flight IF payload and restart the\\nPC at the resolved target; replay contents are immutable copies of prior live\\nimem words and only need reset invalidation. The registered candidate must be\\ninvalidated or recomputed on redirect, reset, and held-PC cases so a stale\\nword can never enter the pipeline. This keeps correctness guarded by full PC\\ntags and the existing EX resolved-next-PC mismatch recovery, while targeting\\nthe 256-entry replay cache's cycle gain with substantially less Fmax damage.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the direct-mapped replay table plus a registered lookahead result for\\nthe current fetch PC. Select live `io_imemData` on ready cycles; select\\ncached data only when `io_imemReady` is low and the registered candidate\\nhas a full-tag hit for the current PC. Fill entries from live ready\\nfetches, clear valid bits on reset, invalidate/recompute the candidate on\\nredirects and held-PC cycles, and run the existing static backward-branch\\nplus direct-JAL prediction from the selected instruction word.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume an effective fetch-ready signal from IF instead of raw\\n`io_imemReady`. A registered replay hit during an external imem stall\\nshould not hold the PC or inject an IF bubble; a miss should behave like\\nthe current imem stall. Preserve load-use, divide-long, redirect, and MEM\\nwait interlocks exactly as in the current best RTL.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire `io_imemReady` into IF and wire IF's effective fetch-ready output to\\nthe hazard unit. Do not change the top-level core IO, pending store-slot\\narbitration, RVFI NRET=2 port set, strict channel-0 order sequence, or\\ntied-off channel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID prediction metadata comments to note that the\\ninstruction word may come from live imem or from a registered lookahead\\nreplay predictor. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the direct-mapped replay table plus a registered lookahead result for\\nthe current fetch PC. Select live `io_imemData` on ready cycles; select\\ncached data only when `io_imemReady` is low and the registered candidate\\nhas a full-tag hit for the current PC. Fill entries from live ready\\nfetches, clear valid bits on reset, invalidate/recompute the candidate on\\nredirects and held-PC cycles, and run the existing static backward-branch\\nplus direct-JAL prediction from the selected instruction word.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume an effective fetch-ready signal from IF instead of raw\\n`io_imemReady`. A registered replay hit during an external imem stall\\nshould not hold the PC or inject an IF bubble; a miss should behave like\\nthe current imem stall. Preserve load-use, divide-long, redirect, and MEM\\nwait interlocks exactly as in the current best RTL.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire `io_imemReady` into IF and wire IF's effective fetch-ready output to\\nthe hazard unit. Do not change the top-level core IO, pending store-slot\\narbitration, RVFI NRET=2 port set, strict channel-0 order sequence, or\\ntied-off channel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID prediction metadata comments to note that the\\ninstruction word may come from live imem or from a registered lookahead\\nreplay predictor. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 472.96, \"delta_pct\": 11.87, \"vs_baseline\": 57.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 163.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5916, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [167.5, 163.61, 158.3], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changed\\n\\n- Added a 256-entry direct-mapped instruction replay table inside `rtl/if_stage.sv`, with valid bits, full 32-bit PC tags, and 32-bit instruction words.\\n- Added a registered lookahead replay candidate for the expected next fetch PC. Current-cycle IF selection only chooses between live `imem_data` and the previously registered candidate; it does not read or tag-compare the replay table combinationally.\\n- Live `imem_ready` fetches always refresh the replay entry for the current PC. A same-PC fill bypass updates the candidate on held-PC cycles so a just-fetched held instruction can be replayed if the external imem stalls next cycle.\\n- Static backward-branch and direct-JAL prediction now runs from the selected instruction word, whether that word came from live imem or from the registered replay candidate.\\n- Added IF `fetch_ready`, high for either live imem readiness or a registered replay hit, and wired it through `rtl/core.sv` into `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`.\\n- Updated IF/ID prediction metadata comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to note that the instruction word can come from live imem or replay.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- The replay table size is 256 entries, matching the high-hit-rate prior experiment described in the hypothesis.\\n- The candidate is recomputed every cycle for the post-clock expected PC, including redirects and held-PC cases, rather than only invalidating on those events. Reset still invalidates the table and candidate.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The replay table contents assume immutable instruction memory after a live fetch, matching the previous replay-cache hypothesis and the current benchmark contract.\\n- The in-place `bash formal/run_all.sh` command could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a protected directory outside the writable root; it failed before running any property check with `Operation not permitted` on the staging cleanup.\\n\\n## Local Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked in-place by sandbox staging permissions before checks ran.\\n- Same `formal/run_all.sh` flow from a writable `/tmp/aa-formal-run` copy with the edited `rtl/`: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:03:52.957639\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 12}\n38:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-031-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Compressed Resetless I-Fetch Replay Tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best fitness comes from the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor: it preserves most of the 256-entry replay table's large\\nrandom-iStall cycle reduction while moving the same-cycle tag lookup off the\\nIF decision path. That win also makes the replay table one of the remaining\\nhigh-leverage timing structures. The table still stores a full 32-bit PC tag\\nfor every entry even though the direct-mapped index already encodes\\n`pc[9:2]` and all reachable fetch PCs are word-aligned. It also resets the\\ntag and instruction arrays even though the valid bits alone determine\\nwhether those payloads can be consumed.\\n\\nRecent micro-opt results show that exact timing-only cleanups can matter:\\nthe 16-bit segmented RVFI order counter improved Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, while broader datapath rewrites and smaller replay tables\\nlost either hit rate or placement quality. Compressing and de-resetting the\\nexisting replay metadata is the same kind of narrow micro-optimization: keep\\nthe current replay behavior and cycle count, but reduce IF table width,\\nreset fanout, and registered-candidate comparison logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the 256-entry registered lookahead replay predictor and its live-imem\\nfirst policy unchanged, but store only the non-index PC tag bits in each\\nentry. With `REPLAY_INDEX_BITS=8`, replace the full 32-bit `replay_tag`\\narray with a `replay_tag_hi` array holding `pc[31:10]`; `pc[9:2]` is already\\nthe array index, and `pc[1:0]` is always zero for architecturally reachable\\nfetches because misaligned branch/JAL/JALR targets trap and suppress the\\nredirect. The lookahead fill, lookup, and fill-bypass paths should compare\\nonly these high tag bits while retaining the existing guard that the\\nregistered candidate corresponds to the current fetch PC.\\n\\nAlso remove the reset writes to replay tag and instruction payload arrays.\\nReset clears only `replay_valid` plus the registered candidate state; invalid\\nentries may hold arbitrary tag/data bits because they are never selected.\\nLive `io_imemReady=1` cycles still use `io_imemData` and refresh the replay\\nentry. `io_imemReady=0` cycles still replay only on a valid matching\\nregistered candidate, otherwise preserving the current PC-hold and IF-bubble\\nbehavior. Branch/JAL prediction, redirect priority, hazard-unit fetch-ready\\nsemantics, RVFI instruction reporting, top-level IO, pending-store behavior,\\ndivider interlock, and channel-0-only retirement remain unchanged.\\n\\nThe expected gain is a modest Fmax improvement from shortening the replay\\ntag read/compare path, reducing table storage width, and removing reset\\npressure from the replay payload memories, with no intended change to\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compress the replay table tag from a full 32-bit PC to the high\\nnon-index bits `pc[31:10]`, and update the registered lookahead lookup,\\nfill, and fill-bypass comparisons accordingly. Clear only replay valid\\nbits and registered candidate state on reset; leave replay tag and\\ninstruction payload arrays resetless because invalid entries cannot be\\nselected. Preserve the 256-entry table size, live-imem priority,\\nregistered replay-hit behavior, static backward-branch/direct-JAL\\nprediction, redirect priority, and `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify only the IF/ID prediction comments to note that replay entries\\nare tagged by the high PC bits plus the direct-mapped index, and that\\nreplay tag/instruction payload arrays are resetless behind valid bits.\\nDo not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compress the replay table tag from a full 32-bit PC to the high\\nnon-index bits `pc[31:10]`, and update the registered lookahead lookup,\\nfill, and fill-bypass comparisons accordingly. Clear only replay valid\\nbits and registered candidate state on reset; leave replay tag and\\ninstruction payload arrays resetless because invalid entries cannot be\\nselected. Preserve the 256-entry table size, live-imem priority,\\nregistered replay-hit behavior, static backward-branch/direct-JAL\\nprediction, redirect priority, and `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify only the IF/ID prediction comments to note that replay entries\\nare tagged by the high PC bits plus the direct-mapped index, and that\\nreplay tag/instruction payload arrays are resetless behind valid bits.\\nDo not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 505.65, \"delta_pct\": 6.91, \"vs_baseline\": 67.97, \"fmax_mhz\": 174.92, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5938, \"ff\": 2226, \"seeds\": [165.07, 188.82, 174.92], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Compressed the IF replay table tag storage in `rtl/if_stage.sv` from full\\n  32-bit PCs to the high non-index bits `pc[31:10]`.\\n- Kept the 256-entry direct-mapped replay table and the registered lookahead\\n  replay behavior unchanged.\\n- Updated replay fill, registered lookup, and fill-bypass matching to use the\\n  direct-mapped index plus the compressed high tag bits.\\n- Preserved the registered current-PC guard with `replay_cand_pc_q == pc`\\n  before a replayed instruction can be selected.\\n- Removed reset writes to replay tag and instruction payload arrays. Reset now\\n  clears only `replay_valid` and the registered replay candidate state.\\n- Updated the IF/ID bundle comment in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document compressed\\n  replay tags and resetless replay payload arrays.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No architectural or interface deviations from the hypothesis.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not stage files directly in this worktree\\n  because the sandbox blocks writes under the protected `formal/riscv-formal`\\n  staging path. I ran the same script in a dereferenced `/tmp` copy of the\\n  worktree so the formal staging writes occurred outside the protected tree.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- None known. Invalid replay entries remain unselectable because reset clears\\n  all valid bits, and replay payload/tag bits are only consumed after a valid\\n  indexed high-tag match and the registered current-PC guard.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Verilator lint: pass\\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`\\n- Formal: pass in `/tmp/auto-arch-formal.TSXDCE`\\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh`\\n  Result: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:19:26.695091\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 13}\n39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-032-r13s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Stage Load Replay Behind I-Fetch Replay\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combining the two targets\\nindependent random-stall sources: IF replay hides instruction stalls, while\\nMEM replay can retire repeated loads during data-memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its large gain from the registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table: random iStall bubbles are often converted into\\nuseful fetches without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF critical\\npath. That leaves the data side as the cleanest remaining structural stall\\nsource. A load still freezes the whole in-order backend whenever\\n`io_dmemReady` is low, even when the same normal-RAM word was recently read\\nin CoreMark's compact 6 KB working set.\\n\\nThe earlier standalone MEM-stage load replay experiment was the best\\nstructural signal after the store slot: it reduced CoreMark cycles with a\\nsmall, conservative table located away from the IF predictor path and did\\nnot require load data to feed the EX operand mux. It was not combined with\\nthe current registered instruction replay best. Combining the two targets\\nindependent random-stall sources: IF replay hides instruction stalls, while\\nMEM replay can retire repeated loads during data-memory stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay buffer in the MEM stage, integrated with\\nthe current pending-store slot and registered IF replay design. Use a\\ndirect-mapped table, for example 16 entries indexed by aligned word address,\\nwith valid bits, word tags, and raw 32-bit memory words returned by previous\\naccepted loads. On `io_dmemReady=1`, loads keep using live `io_dmemRData`\\nexactly as today and refresh the replay entry for their aligned word. On\\n`io_dmemReady=0`, a legal non-trapping normal-RAM load may complete from the\\nreplay table on a valid tag hit: suppress the live dmem read, use the cached\\nraw word for sign/zero extension and RVFI `mem_rdata`, and do not assert\\n`mem_wait`.\\n\\nReplay must remain conservative. It is disabled while the pending store slot\\nis valid, for MMIO/marker/UART addresses, for misaligned or trapping memory\\noperations, and for stores. Normal-RAM stores update or invalidate any\\nmatching replay entry before they retire or enqueue into the pending store\\nslot, so later replayed loads cannot observe stale data. Replay misses and\\nall nonbufferable accesses preserve the current dmem wait behavior. This\\nshould reduce data-stall cycles on top of the current instruction-stall win\\nwithout adding IF routing, predictor state, extra retirement width, or a\\nMEM-to-EX load-data bypass.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide lookup-hit/data outputs for aligned load\\nword addresses, refresh entries from accepted live dmem load responses,\\nand invalidate or byte-merge-update matching entries on legal normal-RAM\\nstores. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the replay tag\\nhits. On a replay hit, gate off `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, drive load\\nextraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached word, and keep replay\\nmisses plus all nonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire any MEM-stage replay sideband needed for dmem request gating while\\npreserving the current registered IF replay, pending-store-slot\\narbitration, top-level core IO, RVFI NRET=2 port layout, strict\\nchannel-0 order sequence, and tied-off channel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document\\nnormal-RAM replay eligibility and raw-word replay metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide lookup-hit/data outputs for aligned load\\nword addresses, refresh entries from accepted live dmem load responses,\\nand invalidate or byte-merge-update matching entries on legal normal-RAM\\nstores. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the replay tag\\nhits. On a replay hit, gate off `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, drive load\\nextraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached word, and keep replay\\nmisses plus all nonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire any MEM-stage replay sideband needed for dmem request gating while\\npreserving the current registered IF replay, pending-store-slot\\narbitration, top-level core IO, RVFI NRET=2 port layout, strict\\nchannel-0 order sequence, and tied-off channel 1 behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document\\nnormal-RAM replay eligibility and raw-word replay metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 488.87, \"delta_pct\": 3.36, \"vs_baseline\": 62.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 166.36, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3402954, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5896, \"ff\": 2232, \"seeds\": [166.36, 171.44, 162.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"## What changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/load_replay.sv`, a 16-entry direct-mapped replay table for aligned normal-RAM words. It stores valid bits, aligned word tags, and raw 32-bit load words.\\n- Integrated the table in `rtl/mem_stage.sv`.\\n- Accepted live normal-RAM loads refresh the replay entry when `dmem_ready` is high.\\n- Legal aligned normal-RAM loads can complete from replay only when `dmem_ready` is low, no pending store slot is valid, and the replay tag hits.\\n- Replay hits suppress `dmem_ren`, clear the MEM-stage wait path, and drive both load extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached raw word.\\n- Legal normal-RAM stores that retire normally or enqueue into the pending-store slot byte-merge any matching replay entry so later replayed loads cannot observe stale bytes.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Kept the replay table instantiated inside `mem_stage.sv`; no `core.sv` sideband was needed because the existing live dmem request wires already carry the gated `dmem_ren`.\\n- Used byte-merge store updates instead of invalidation. This preserves replay opportunities after stores while remaining coherent for cached words.\\n- Did not change `core_pkg.sv`; the eligibility logic stayed compact enough to keep local to MEM.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- Exact `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not run to completion in this sandbox because its staging cleanup tries to remove files under the protected `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher` tree and fails with `Operation not permitted` before checks are generated.\\n- To still self-check the RTL, I ran the same genchecks/make/tally flow against a dereferenced temporary copy of `formal/riscv-formal` under `/tmp`. This avoids modifying the protected tree while using the same RTL, wrapper, checks config, and ch1 PREUNSAT pass rule.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: environment blocked during staging cleanup; no proof counterexample was produced.\\n- Temporary-copy riscv-formal run: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:24:52.102769\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13}\n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-033-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Two-Word Line I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nfull-tag word table risks growing the registered lookup mux and tag storage\\nin the IF stage. A better predictor-shaped next step is to pack adjacent\\ninstruction words under one line tag: it increases effective replay\\ncapacity for sequential CoreMark code while amortizing the full PC tag over\\ntwo instructions and preserving the current registered-candidate timing\\nmodel.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor result is the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. It kept the large cycle win of the earlier stall-only\\nreplay cache, reducing CoreMark cycles from 4,226,169 to 3,459,314, while\\nmoving the tag/data lookup off the same-cycle IF decision path enough to\\nreach 472.96 fitness. The predictor experiments after BTFNT and direct JAL\\nshow the useful signal clearly: hiding random imem stalls pays much more\\nthan adding another branch-direction table or return predictor.\\n\\nThe remaining replay loss is likely capacity conflict, not replay timing.\\nThe 64-entry replay attempts gave up many cycles versus the 256-entry\\nversion, so hot text footprint and aliasing matter. Simply doubling a\\nfull-tag word table risks growing the registered lookup mux and tag storage\\nin the IF stage. A better predictor-shaped next step is to pack adjacent\\ninstruction words under one line tag: it increases effective replay\\ncapacity for sequential CoreMark code while amortizing the full PC tag over\\ntwo instructions and preserving the current registered-candidate timing\\nmodel.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current one-word-per-entry replay table in `if_stage` with a\\ntwo-word direct-mapped replay-line predictor. Use a line index such as\\n`pc[10:3]`, a full line tag for the upper PC bits, two per-word valid bits,\\nand two 32-bit instruction slots selected by `pc[2]`. On every live\\n`io_imemReady=1` fetch, keep using `io_imemData` exactly as today and\\nrefresh only the selected word within that line. On `io_imemReady=0`, the\\ncurrent cycle still consumes only the registered lookahead candidate; a hit\\nrequires the registered line tag to match the current PC line and the\\nselected word valid bit to be set.\\n\\nThis keeps the zero-wait and current-cycle IF path structurally equivalent\\nto the present best design: live imem wins whenever ready, replay data is\\nused only from a previously registered candidate, and the existing static\\nbackward-branch plus direct-JAL predictor runs from the selected instruction\\nword. Redirects and reset still flush only the in-flight IF payload and\\nrecompute the registered candidate for the next fetch PC; immutable replay\\ncontents only need reset invalidation. Full line tags and per-word valid\\nbits preserve correctness under arbitrary PC values, so an alias can only\\nmiss, never inject the wrong instruction. The expected gain is a modest\\ncycle reduction from fewer replay conflicts with lower Fmax risk than a\\nlarger full-tag word table or any new branch-direction update path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the registered lookahead replay table from 256 independent\\nword entries to a direct-mapped two-word line organization. Store one\\nfull line tag, two valid bits, and two 32-bit instruction words per\\nline; fill only the live-fetched word on `imem_ready`, use live imem\\ndata on ready cycles, and use the selected registered replay word only\\non an external imem stall with matching line tag and valid word bit.\\nPreserve the existing `fetch_ready` semantics, PC redirect priority,\\nsame-PC fill bypass, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, and\\nno combinational table read on the current IF decision path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to describe that replayed\\ninstruction metadata may come from a registered two-word line replay\\npredictor. Do not change bundle widths, RVFI fields, decoder legality\\ndefaults, architectural state, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the registered lookahead replay table from 256 independent\\nword entries to a direct-mapped two-word line organization. Store one\\nfull line tag, two valid bits, and two 32-bit instruction words per\\nline; fill only the live-fetched word on `imem_ready`, use live imem\\ndata on ready cycles, and use the selected registered replay word only\\non an external imem stall with matching line tag and valid word bit.\\nPreserve the existing `fetch_ready` semantics, PC redirect priority,\\nsame-PC fill bypass, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, and\\nno combinational table read on the current IF decision path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to describe that replayed\\ninstruction metadata may come from a registered two-word line replay\\npredictor. Do not change bundle widths, RVFI fields, decoder legality\\ndefaults, architectural state, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 475.9, \"delta_pct\": 0.62, \"vs_baseline\": 58.09, \"fmax_mhz\": 163.56, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3436867, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5948, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [172.56, 141.16, 163.56], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"## Two-Word Line I-Fetch Replay Predictor\\n\\n### What changed\\n\\n- Replaced `if_stage`'s 256-entry one-word replay table with a 256-line direct-mapped replay-line table.\\n- Each replay line now uses index `pc[10:3]`, upper-PC tag `pc[31:11]`, two per-word valid bits, and two 32-bit instruction slots selected by `pc[2]`.\\n- Live `imem_ready` fetches still feed IF immediately and update only the selected word in the replay line. If the line tag changes, the other word is invalidated; if the tag matches, the other word's valid/data state is preserved.\\n- The IF stall replay path still uses only a registered lookahead candidate. The registered candidate now holds the full line address `pc[31:3]`, both valid bits, and both instruction words; a replay hit requires the candidate line to match the current PC line and the selected word valid bit to be set.\\n- Replay fills and hits are gated to word-aligned PCs so the two-word line offset never aliases a hypothetical misaligned fetch onto an aligned instruction word.\\n- Preserved live-imem priority, redirect priority over stalls, same-line/same-PC fill bypass into the next registered candidate, and static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction from the selected live or replayed instruction word.\\n- Updated `core_pkg.sv` IF/ID prediction comments to describe the registered two-word line replay predictor. No bundle widths, RVFI fields, architectural state, or top-level IO changed.\\n\\n### Deviations\\n\\n- The implementation keeps 256 direct-mapped entries, now interpreted as 256 two-word lines. This follows the hypothesis' `pc[10:3]` line-index suggestion and increases effective replay data capacity to 512 instruction words while amortizing each line tag across two words.\\n- The registered fill bypass merges a live fetched word into the candidate whenever the next lookup is for the same two-word line, not only when the exact PC matches. This is a conservative extension of the existing same-PC bypass and does not add a current-cycle table lookup to the IF decision path.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- No known functional concerns. The current-cycle IF decision still depends only on `imem_ready`, the registered candidate line, the current PC alignment, and the current PC word select.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` from the worktree was blocked by sandbox permissions because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared non-writable tree and the script refreshes `cores/auto-arch-researcher`.\\n\\n### Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass when run from a `/tmp` mirror with a writable copied `formal/riscv-formal` framework area; final tally was `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:26:15.756125\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13}\n41:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-034-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Elide Replay Candidate PC Compare\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay candidate\\nis computed for exactly the PC that will be held or loaded into the PC\\nregister on the next cycle. Redirect loads both PC and candidate from the\\nredirect target, a non-stalled fetch loads both from `next_pc`, and a stalled\\nfetch recomputes the candidate for the held PC.\\n\\nRemoving this full-width equality check is a narrow micro-optimization in the\\nsame proven area as the current best. It should reduce IF-side compare\\nfanout, remove 32 candidate-PC flops, and shorten the `fetch_ready` decision\\npath without changing replay hit rate, branch/JAL prediction policy,\\npipeline timing, memory behavior, RVFI reporting, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is dominated by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. Compressing the replay table tags improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which shows the replay-hit/fetch-ready path is now\\none of the most timing-sensitive structures left in the design.\\n\\nThe IF stage still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and\\ncompares it against the current `pc` every cycle before a replay candidate\\ncan satisfy an external imem stall. That guard is logically redundant with\\nthe current lookahead update discipline: on every clock, the replay candidate\\nis computed for exactly the PC that will be held or loaded into the PC\\nregister on the next cycle. Redirect loads both PC and candidate from the\\nredirect target, a non-stalled fetch loads both from `next_pc`, and a stalled\\nfetch recomputes the candidate for the held PC.\\n\\nRemoving this full-width equality check is a narrow micro-optimization in the\\nsame proven area as the current best. It should reduce IF-side compare\\nfanout, remove 32 candidate-PC flops, and shorten the `fetch_ready` decision\\npath without changing replay hit rate, branch/JAL prediction policy,\\npipeline timing, memory behavior, RVFI reporting, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace `replay_current_hit = replay_cand_valid_q && (replay_cand_pc_q == pc)`\\nwith a candidate-valid-only hit signal, and remove `replay_cand_pc_q`\\nentirely. The invariant to preserve is that `replay_cand_valid_q` and\\n`replay_cand_instr_q` always describe the current fetch PC at the start of a\\ncycle:\\n\\n- reset clears the candidate valid bit;\\n- EX redirect updates both `pc` and the next registered candidate from\\n  `redirect_target`;\\n- normal non-stalled fetch updates both from the same `next_pc`;\\n- stalled fetch holds `pc` and recomputes the candidate for that held `pc`;\\n- live `io_imemReady` still takes priority over replay data, and fill bypass\\n  still supplies a just-fetched word when the next candidate lookup aliases\\n  the just-filled replay entry.\\n\\nOn an external imem stall, `fetch_ready` therefore becomes\\n`replay_cand_valid_q` instead of a full 32-bit PC equality plus valid check.\\nReplay misses keep the existing PC-hold and IF-bubble behavior. The visible\\ninstruction stream, predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata, EX\\nresolved-next-PC recovery, strict RVFI order, channel-0-only retirement, and\\ntied-off channel 1 remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the `replay_cand_pc_q` register and the current-PC equality from\\n`replay_current_hit`. Treat the registered replay candidate valid bit as\\nthe sole replay-hit guard because the candidate is already recomputed for\\nthe next-cycle PC on every redirect, stall, and normal PC advance. Keep\\nlive-imem priority, replay fill/update behavior, fill bypass, reset valid\\nclearing, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, and the existing\\n`fetch_ready` contract otherwise unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID replay comments to state that the registered lookahead\\ncandidate is maintained in lockstep with the fetch PC, so its valid bit\\nis sufficient to indicate a replay hit during external imem stalls. Do\\nnot change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults,\\nRVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the `replay_cand_pc_q` register and the current-PC equality from\\n`replay_current_hit`. Treat the registered replay candidate valid bit as\\nthe sole replay-hit guard because the candidate is already recomputed for\\nthe next-cycle PC on every redirect, stall, and normal PC advance. Keep\\nlive-imem priority, replay fill/update behavior, fill bypass, reset valid\\nclearing, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, and the existing\\n`fetch_ready` contract otherwise unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID replay comments to state that the registered lookahead\\ncandidate is maintained in lockstep with the fetch PC, so its valid bit\\nis sufficient to indicate a replay hit during external imem stalls. Do\\nnot change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults,\\nRVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 502.12, \"delta_pct\": -0.7, \"vs_baseline\": 66.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 173.7, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5909, \"ff\": 2226, \"seeds\": [181.55, 172.77, 173.7], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Removed `replay_cand_pc_q` from `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- Changed `replay_current_hit` to use `replay_cand_valid_q` directly, so `fetch_ready` is `imem_ready || replay_cand_valid_q`.\\n- Kept live imem priority over replay data and kept the replay fill bypass path unchanged.\\n- Narrowed the internal replay lookup PC signal to `[31:2]` because the low fetch-address bits are no longer consumed after removing the candidate-PC equality check.\\n- Updated IF replay comments in `rtl/if_stage.sv` and `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document that the registered candidate is maintained in lockstep with the fetch PC.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No architectural or interface deviations from the hypothesis.\\n- The only implementation detail beyond the hypothesis text is narrowing the replay lookup source from a full 32-bit signal to word-address bits to avoid an unused-signal lint warning.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known functional concerns. The optimization relies on the existing IF update discipline: reset clears candidate valid, redirects load the target candidate, normal fetch advances candidate with `next_pc`, and stalls recompute for the held PC.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` from the repository tree could not stage files because this sandbox rejects writes through the symlinked/protected `formal/riscv-formal` tree (`rm ... Operation not permitted` before checks were generated).\\n- Ran the same formal script from a temporary copy with the `riscv-formal` symlink dereferenced into `/tmp` and renamed to a writable `formal/rf` path. Result: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:40:44.254148\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 14}\n42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-035-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Registered MEM Load-Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest IPC gain from registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay: repeated hot instructions can be supplied during random\\nimem stalls without putting a same-cycle replay-table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. The analogous remaining structural loss is on the data side.\\nLoads still freeze the whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low,\\neven when the same normal-RAM word was recently returned by dmem.\\n\\nThe earlier MEM-stage load replay experiment showed the opportunity: it cut\\nCoreMark cycles, but the combinational replay-table lookup, tag compare, and\\nstore merge logic in MEM lowered Fmax enough to miss the current best. The\\npromising structural angle is to keep the replay behavior while moving the\\nlookup off the MEM critical path, just as the successful IF replay predictor\\ndid for instruction stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small normal-RAM load replay table with a registered candidate for the\\ninstruction entering MEM. When EX computes an effective address for a legal\\nload, use that address to look up the replay table one cycle early and latch a\\ncandidate {hit, raw_word, tag/address} alongside the EX/MEM payload. In the\\nfollowing MEM cycle, if io_dmemReady is low and the candidate still matches a\\nvalid non-trapping normal-RAM load, complete the load from the registered raw\\nword: suppress the live dmem read, use the cached word for sign/zero\\nextension and RVFI mem_rdata, and do not assert mem_wait. If the candidate\\nmisses, or if dmem is ready, preserve the current live-memory behavior.\\n\\nReplay remains conservative. It is disabled while the pending store slot is\\nvalid, for MMIO/marker/UART addresses, for misaligned or trapping memory\\noperations, and for stores. Accepted normal-RAM loads refresh the replay\\ntable from live io_dmemRData. Normal-RAM stores either byte-merge-update or\\ninvalidate a matching replay entry before the store retires or enqueues into\\nthe pending store slot, so a replayed load cannot observe stale bytes.\\n\\nThis is a structural latency-hiding change, not a load-to-EX bypass: cached\\ndata is used only at the MEM retirement boundary, and dependent younger\\ninstructions still rely on the existing in-order load-use behavior. The\\ntop-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 contract, channel-0-only retirement convention,\\nstrict rvfi_order sequence, registered IF replay predictor, divider\\ninterlock, branch prediction, and pending-store ordering all remain\\nunchanged. The expected win is to recover much of the prior load-replay cycle\\nreduction with less Fmax loss because MEM sees only a registered hit/data\\ncandidate instead of a same-cycle table read.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide a one-cycle-early lookup interface that\\nlatches a replay candidate for the load entering MEM, refresh entries\\nfrom accepted live dmem load responses, and invalidate or byte-merge\\nmatching entries on legal normal-RAM stores. Reset clears only valid and\\ncandidate-valid state.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the effective address and replay-eligible metadata for the\\ninstruction being launched into EX/MEM so the load replay table can\\nregister its lookup candidate one cycle before MEM uses it. Do not change\\nALU results, forwarding, branch/JAL prediction repair, divider launch,\\nor RVFI operand metadata.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered replay candidate during load handling. Select\\ncached raw word data only when dmem_ready is low, the current access is a\\nvalid legal normal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the\\nregistered candidate matches. On a hit, gate off dmem_ren, clear\\nmem_wait, and drive load extraction plus RVFI mem_rdata from the cached\\nword. Keep replay misses and all nonbufferable accesses on the existing\\nwait path, and update or invalidate replay entries for stores.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the one-cycle-early replay lookup sideband from EX to the replay\\ntable and the registered replay candidate into MEM, while preserving the\\nexisting registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot dmem arbitration,\\ntop-level core IO shape, RVFI channel layout, and channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only compact helper typedefs or comments if useful for carrying the\\nregistered load-replay candidate and eligibility metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped normal-RAM replay table with valid, tag, and\\nraw-word data state. Provide a one-cycle-early lookup interface that\\nlatches a replay candidate for the load entering MEM, refresh entries\\nfrom accepted live dmem load responses, and invalidate or byte-merge\\nmatching entries on legal normal-RAM stores. Reset clears only valid and\\ncandidate-valid state.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the effective address and replay-eligible metadata for the\\ninstruction being launched into EX/MEM so the load replay table can\\nregister its lookup candidate one cycle before MEM uses it. Do not change\\nALU results, forwarding, branch/JAL prediction repair, divider launch,\\nor RVFI operand metadata.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the registered replay candidate during load handling. Select\\ncached raw word data only when dmem_ready is low, the current access is a\\nvalid legal normal-RAM load, no pending store slot owns the port, and the\\nregistered candidate matches. On a hit, gate off dmem_ren, clear\\nmem_wait, and drive load extraction plus RVFI mem_rdata from the cached\\nword. Keep replay misses and all nonbufferable accesses on the existing\\nwait path, and update or invalidate replay entries for stores.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the one-cycle-early replay lookup sideband from EX to the replay\\ntable and the registered replay candidate into MEM, while preserving the\\nexisting registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot dmem arbitration,\\ntop-level core IO shape, RVFI channel layout, and channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only compact helper typedefs or comments if useful for carrying the\\nregistered load-replay candidate and eligibility metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 380.74, \"delta_pct\": -24.7, \"vs_baseline\": 26.47, \"fmax_mhz\": 130.14, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3418106, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5928, \"ff\": 2237, \"seeds\": [140.23, 129.57, 130.14], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What Changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/load_replay.sv`, a 16-entry direct-mapped normal-RAM load replay table with reset-cleared valid state and a registered MEM-stage replay candidate.\\n- Added `load_replay_cand_t` in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to carry `{valid, word-aligned address, raw word}` from the replay table to MEM.\\n- Extended `rtl/ex_stage.sv` to expose a one-cycle-early effective-address lookup sideband for valid, legal, aligned normal-RAM loads.\\n- Extended `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to consume the registered candidate only when `io_dmemReady` is low, the current instruction is still a valid legal normal-RAM load, and the pending store slot is not valid. Replay hits suppress the live dmem read, clear `mem_wait`, and drive both load extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` from the cached raw word.\\n- Wired the replay table through `rtl/core.sv` while leaving top-level IO, RVFI channel layout, channel-1 tie-off, IF replay, divider interlock, branch prediction, and store-slot dmem arbitration unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Store handling invalidates a matching replay entry instead of byte-merge-updating it. This is conservative and prevents stale replayed bytes while keeping the store-side replay-table update path smaller.\\n- The exact in-tree `bash formal/run_all.sh` command could not stage files because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a sandbox-protected directory; it failed immediately with `Operation not permitted` while removing staged `.sv` files. I mirrored `rtl/` and `formal/` into `/tmp/codex-formal-load-replay-36775` with a writable `riscv-formal/cores` directory and ran the same script there.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The replay table is intentionally small and direct-mapped; it may recover less IPC than a larger or merge-updating table.\\n- The lookup is off the MEM wait path, but it still adds an EX effective-address-to-candidate-register setup path. The FPGA fitness run will decide whether that is cheaper than the old same-cycle MEM replay lookup.\\n\\n## Local Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: in-place run blocked by sandbox permissions before proofs began.\\n- `/tmp` mirror `bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:42:55.474038\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 14}\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-036-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Phase-Aligned Replay Candidate\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` and gates\\nreplay hits with `replay_cand_pc_q == pc` before asserting `fetch_ready`.\\nThat compare is on the current IF stall/PC-enable path even though the\\ncandidate is already generated from the exact same redirect/stall/next-PC\\nselection that updates the PC register. After a clock edge, the registered\\nreplay candidate is phase-aligned with the current PC by construction.\\n\\nRemoving the redundant current-PC equality check is a narrower predictor\\nimprovement than changing table capacity or direction policy. It preserves\\nthe current replay hit rate and CoreMark cycle count while shortening the\\nfetch-ready path and dropping the 32-bit candidate-PC register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result comes from the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay predictor. It keeps the large random-iStall\\ncycle win of the 256-entry replay table while avoiding a same-cycle table\\nread in IF. The predictor experiments since then show that adding more\\nbranch-direction or return-target state is not attractive: those changes\\nsaved a few cycles but lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining predictor timing cost is inside the replay fast decision\\nitself. `if_stage` still carries a full 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` and gates\\nreplay hits with `replay_cand_pc_q == pc` before asserting `fetch_ready`.\\nThat compare is on the current IF stall/PC-enable path even though the\\ncandidate is already generated from the exact same redirect/stall/next-PC\\nselection that updates the PC register. After a clock edge, the registered\\nreplay candidate is phase-aligned with the current PC by construction.\\n\\nRemoving the redundant current-PC equality check is a narrower predictor\\nimprovement than changing table capacity or direction policy. It preserves\\nthe current replay hit rate and CoreMark cycle count while shortening the\\nfetch-ready path and dropping the 32-bit candidate-PC register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the full-PC-guarded replay candidate with a phase-aligned valid\\ncandidate. In `if_stage`, compute `replay_lookup_pc` from the same priority\\nmux that determines the next value of `pc`: EX redirect target first, then\\npredicted/fallthrough next PC when fetch is not stalled, otherwise the held\\ncurrent PC. Use that lookup to register only `{valid, instr}` for the next\\ncycle. Because the PC register loads the same selected address on the same\\nclock edge, `replay_cand_valid_q` means the cached instruction candidate is\\nfor the current fetch PC on the following cycle.\\n\\nWith that invariant, drive `fetch_ready = io_imemReady || replay_cand_valid_q`\\nand select `replay_cand_instr_q` on an external imem stall whenever the\\ncandidate valid bit is set. Remove `replay_cand_pc_q` and the 32-bit\\nequality check from the current-cycle IF path. The replay table itself keeps\\nthe current 256-entry organization, compressed high-PC tags, resetless\\ntag/instruction payloads, live-imem-first fill policy, and same-index fill\\nbypass. Reset still clears the replay valid bits and candidate valid state;\\nredirects and held-PC cycles recompute the candidate from the same address\\nthe PC will hold or load.\\n\\nThis should be bit-equivalent for all architecturally reachable traces:\\nreplay contents are still selected only after a valid table lookup for the\\nselected next PC, live imem data still wins whenever ready, replay misses\\nstill hold the PC and inject the existing IF bubble, and EX still repairs\\nbranch/JAL prediction mistakes through resolved-next-PC mismatch recovery.\\nThe top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 contract, channel-0 retirement order, pending\\nstore slot, divider interlock, decoder legality rules, and static\\nbackward-branch/direct-JAL prediction policy remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove `replay_cand_pc_q` and the full 32-bit\\n`replay_cand_pc_q == pc` replay-hit guard. Register the replay\\ncandidate from the same selected next-PC value that the PC register will\\nhold after the clock edge, so the candidate valid bit is phase-aligned\\nwith the current fetch PC. Drive effective `fetch_ready` from live imem\\nreadiness or this phase-aligned candidate valid bit, and preserve the\\nexisting replay table size, compressed high tags, resetless payload\\narrays, fill bypass, live-imem priority, redirect priority, and static\\nbranch/JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to document that replay candidates\\nare registered for the same next-PC value loaded by the IF PC register,\\nso candidate validity is phase-aligned with the current fetch PC. Do not\\nchange bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults,\\nRVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove `replay_cand_pc_q` and the full 32-bit\\n`replay_cand_pc_q == pc` replay-hit guard. Register the replay\\ncandidate from the same selected next-PC value that the PC register will\\nhold after the clock edge, so the candidate valid bit is phase-aligned\\nwith the current fetch PC. Drive effective `fetch_ready` from live imem\\nreadiness or this phase-aligned candidate valid bit, and preserve the\\nexisting replay table size, compressed high tags, resetless payload\\narrays, fill bypass, live-imem priority, redirect priority, and static\\nbranch/JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to document that replay candidates\\nare registered for the same next-PC value loaded by the IF PC register,\\nso candidate validity is phase-aligned with the current fetch PC. Do not\\nchange bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults,\\nRVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 300.23, \"delta_pct\": -40.62, \"vs_baseline\": -0.27, \"fmax_mhz\": 103.86, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5944, \"ff\": 2223, \"seeds\": [103.86, 112.33, 100.73], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What Changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/if_stage.sv` to remove the 32-bit `replay_cand_pc_q` register and the `replay_cand_pc_q == pc` replay-hit guard.\\n- `fetch_ready` is now driven by `imem_ready || replay_cand_valid_q`.\\n- During an external imem stall, IF now selects `replay_cand_instr_q` whenever `replay_cand_valid_q` is set.\\n- Kept the existing replay table organization: 256 direct-mapped entries, high-PC tags, resetless tag/instruction payloads, valid-bit reset, live-imem-first fill, and same-index fill bypass.\\n- Narrowed the replay lookup address storage used inside IF to `replay_lookup_pc_word[31:2]`, since the low two PC bits are architecturally zero for fetch and no longer feed a full-PC equality check.\\n- Updated the IF/ID comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document that replay candidates are registered for the same next-PC value loaded by IF's PC register.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No architectural or bundle-width changes were made.\\n- No changes were made outside `rtl/` except this notes file.\\n- The normal `bash formal/run_all.sh` invocation could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared tree outside the writable worktree. I ran the same script from a dereferenced `/tmp/auto-arch-formal-r14s2` copy containing the current `rtl/` instead.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The change relies on the existing invariant that `replay_lookup_pc_word` is selected from the same redirect/stall/next-PC priority used by the PC register update. That invariant is now directly reflected in the remaining IF logic.\\n- No known functional concerns from lint or formal.\\n\\n## Local Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked in-place by sandbox permissions on the symlinked shared `formal/riscv-formal` staging directory before checks ran.\\n- Equivalent `/tmp` formal run using a copied `formal/` tree and current `rtl/`: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:38:29.236296\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 14}\n44:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-037-r15s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-Only Pipeline Payload Reset\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields include PCs, instructions, operands,\\nimmediates, memory words, and RVFI metadata; only a small subset of valid\\nbits and side-effect controls must be known zero to prevent writes,\\nmemory requests, redirects, or incorrect forwarding. A valid-gated payload\\ncleanup is a narrow micro-optimization in the same class as the measured\\nreplay tag reset elision: preserve the exact instruction stream and\\nretirement behavior, but remove needless reset/clear logic from hot\\npipeline boundaries.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by timing-only wins that do not change\\nCoreMark cycles: the segmented RVFI order counter and the compressed\\nresetless IF replay metadata both improved Fmax by removing unnecessary\\ncarry, compare, and reset pressure from always-present logic. The remaining\\npipeline registers still use broad `<= '0` clears on reset, flush, and\\ninjected bubbles, even though most payload bits are architecturally\\nirrelevant whenever the stage valid bit is zero.\\n\\nThose full-bundle clears add synchronous reset/clear muxes and reset fanout\\nto wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divide-side metadata, and replay-candidate\\npayload registers. The data fields include PCs, instructions, operands,\\nimmediates, memory words, and RVFI metadata; only a small subset of valid\\nbits and side-effect controls must be known zero to prevent writes,\\nmemory requests, redirects, or incorrect forwarding. A valid-gated payload\\ncleanup is a narrow micro-optimization in the same class as the measured\\nreplay tag reset elision: preserve the exact instruction stream and\\nretirement behavior, but remove needless reset/clear logic from hot\\npipeline boundaries.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the main pipeline and sidecar payload registers to valid-only or\\nside-effect-only reset discipline. Reset, redirect flushes, and long-op\\nbubbles should clear the stage `valid` bit and any control enables that can\\ncause an architectural side effect (`reg_write`, `mem_read`, `mem_write`,\\nbranch/jump redirect enables, and forwarding-visible write enables). The\\nwide payload fields behind those valid bits may remain resetless and do not\\nneed to be overwritten with zero on bubbles.\\n\\nConsumers must be made explicitly valid-aware wherever they are not already:\\nload-use detection should see `id_ex.valid && id_ex.ctrl.mem_read`;\\nEX/MEM forwarding should require a valid producer; MEM/WB forwarding must\\npreserve the existing held-load forwarding behavior during `hold_wb` while\\nstill being safe immediately after reset. MEM-stage dmem requests, WB\\nwrites, redirects, and RVFI retirement already key off valid and should keep\\nthat discipline. Channel 1 remains tied off, and channel 0 RVFI fields are\\nonly meaningful when `io_rvfi_valid_0` is asserted.\\n\\nThe visible 64-bit RVFI order sequence, `pc_wdata`, trap behavior, divider\\ninterlock, IF replay predictor, pending-store slot, memory ordering, and\\nCoreMark cycle count should remain bit-identical. The expected gain is a\\nmodest Fmax improvement from removing reset/clear muxes and reset routing on\\nseveral hundred nonarchitectural payload flops without adding new datapath\\nwork or predictor state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change reset and flush handling for the ID/EX register so only\\n`valid` and side-effecting control bits are forced to a safe bubble.\\nLeave operand, PC, instruction, immediate, address, and prediction\\npayload fields resetless behind the cleared valid bit. Preserve decoder\\nillegal-default behavior and the existing capture path when the stage is\\nnot stalled.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply the same valid-only clear discipline to the EX/MEM register and\\ndivide metadata. Reset or bubble cycles should clear `valid` and any\\nforwarding-visible or memory/writeback side-effect controls, but should\\nnot zero wide payload fields that are ignored while invalid. Keep the\\ndivider launch/done protocol, branch/JAL/JALR repair logic, and RVFI\\noperand metadata semantics unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Convert MEM/WB reset to clear only `valid` and the minimal control\\nfields needed for safe post-reset forwarding/writeback behavior. Keep\\nthe existing `hold_wb` behavior that clears valid while retaining load\\nmetadata for forwarding during a dmem stall. Do not alter load\\nextraction, store-slot enqueue, dmem request gating, misalignment traps,\\nor RVFI memory fields for valid retirements.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make forwarding producer enables explicitly valid-aware for EX/MEM, and\\npreserve the current MEM/WB held-load forwarding case with a safe\\npost-reset enable. This prevents resetless invalid payloads from being\\nselected while keeping all existing true bypass behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate hazard and forwarding inputs with the appropriate stage-valid or\\nheld-forwarding predicates so resetless payload fields cannot influence\\nstalls or operand selection. Preserve the top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 port\\nlayout, strict channel-0 order counter, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\nIF replay wiring, and channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update pipeline-bundle comments to document that many payload fields are\\nresetless and are only architecturally meaningful when the associated\\nvalid bit and side-effect controls are asserted. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, RVFI fields, decoder legality defaults, architectural state, or\\nthe NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change reset and flush handling for the ID/EX register so only\\n`valid` and side-effecting control bits are forced to a safe bubble.\\nLeave operand, PC, instruction, immediate, address, and prediction\\npayload fields resetless behind the cleared valid bit. Preserve decoder\\nillegal-default behavior and the existing capture path when the stage is\\nnot stalled.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply the same valid-only clear discipline to the EX/MEM register and\\ndivide metadata. Reset or bubble cycles should clear `valid` and any\\nforwarding-visible or memory/writeback side-effect controls, but should\\nnot zero wide payload fields that are ignored while invalid. Keep the\\ndivider launch/done protocol, branch/JAL/JALR repair logic, and RVFI\\noperand metadata semantics unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Convert MEM/WB reset to clear only `valid` and the minimal control\\nfields needed for safe post-reset forwarding/writeback behavior. Keep\\nthe existing `hold_wb` behavior that clears valid while retaining load\\nmetadata for forwarding during a dmem stall. Do not alter load\\nextraction, store-slot enqueue, dmem request gating, misalignment traps,\\nor RVFI memory fields for valid retirements.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make forwarding producer enables explicitly valid-aware for EX/MEM, and\\npreserve the current MEM/WB held-load forwarding case with a safe\\npost-reset enable. This prevents resetless invalid payloads from being\\nselected while keeping all existing true bypass behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate hazard and forwarding inputs with the appropriate stage-valid or\\nheld-forwarding predicates so resetless payload fields cannot influence\\nstalls or operand selection. Preserve the top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 port\\nlayout, strict channel-0 order counter, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\nIF replay wiring, and channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update pipeline-bundle comments to document that many payload fields are\\nresetless and are only architecturally meaningful when the associated\\nvalid bit and side-effect controls are asserted. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, RVFI fields, decoder legality defaults, architectural state, or\\nthe NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.76, \"delta_pct\": -38.94, \"vs_baseline\": 2.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 106.81, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5949, \"ff\": 2255, \"seeds\": [95.9, 107.67, 106.81], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Converted ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, divider side metadata, IF replay-candidate payload, and pending-store-slot payload reset handling toward valid-only discipline.\\n- Reset, redirect/bubble, and invalid-capture paths now clear `valid` plus side-effecting controls (`reg_write`, `mem_read`, `mem_write`, branch/jump redirect controls) while leaving wide payload fields resetless.\\n- Gated load-use detection with `id_ex.valid && id_ex.ctrl.mem_read`.\\n- Made EX/MEM forwarding valid-aware. MEM/WB forwarding keeps the existing held-writeback behavior by treating `mem_wb.ctrl.reg_write` as a forwarding-visible enable; reset and invalid captures now clear it, while `hold_wb` intentionally retains it.\\n- Updated bundle comments to document resetless payload validity rules.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Also applied the same payload reset elision to `div_unit.sv`, `if_stage.sv` replay-candidate payload fields, and `store_slot.sv` payload fields. These are behind explicit valid/state bits and match the hypothesis intent even though they were not all in the advisory file list.\\n- Did not add any new architectural state, change bundle widths, alter top-level IO, or change the RVFI NRET=2 channel contract.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- Channel-0 RVFI payload fields may carry stale/resetless values when `io_rvfi_valid_0` is low. This is intentional; visible RVFI retirements remain valid-gated.\\n- The direct in-tree `bash formal/run_all.sh` invocation is blocked in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a protected path and the script cannot refresh `cores/auto-arch-researcher/*.sv` there.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass when run from a disposable `/tmp` copy of the same project inputs; final tally was `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n- No RTL formal counterexamples were observed, so no formal-driven fix attempts were needed.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:02:23.850439\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 15}\n45:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-038-r15s1\", \"title\": \"Two-Entry Pending Store Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much larger design. The current RTL is\\nsubstantially smaller and already has the timing-local one-entry direct fast\\npath, so revisiting only a second pending-store slot is the cleanest\\nstructural bet: it targets store bursts under the 22% random dStall model\\nwithout adding load data to EX, changing the branch predictor, widening\\nretirement, or touching the IF replay timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core combines the proven structural pieces: cold DIV/REM is\\nout of the universal EX path, the one-deep pending-store slot hides some\\nnormal-RAM store backpressure, and the registered instruction replay table\\nremoves most random iStall loss. The remaining structural stalls are now\\nconcentrated on the data side. A single pending-store entry lets one stalled\\nRAM store retire, but the next store still freezes the whole pipeline while\\nthe older slot drains, even though both stores already have their address,\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory metadata available in MEM.\\n\\nA two-entry store buffer was attempted before the large post-DIV and replay\\ntiming wins and failed placement on a much larger design. The current RTL is\\nsubstantially smaller and already has the timing-local one-entry direct fast\\npath, so revisiting only a second pending-store slot is the cleanest\\nstructural bet: it targets store bursts under the 22% random dStall model\\nwithout adding load data to EX, changing the branch predictor, widening\\nretirement, or touching the IF replay timing path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the pending-store retirement slot into a two-entry oldest-first FIFO\\nfor legal, aligned normal-RAM stores only. When the FIFO is empty and\\n`io_dmemReady` is high, keep the existing direct ready-store path unchanged:\\nthe live MEM-stage store drives the external dmem port and retires normally.\\nWhen a bufferable store cannot use the port because `io_dmemReady` is low or\\nbecause an older pending store already owns the port, enqueue the store into\\nany free FIFO entry, retire it once through MEM/WB with its RVFI memory\\nfields, and allow younger non-memory instructions to continue.\\n\\nThe FIFO drains oldest-first onto the single external dmem port. While any\\npending store is valid, younger loads, younger stores that cannot enqueue,\\nand all MMIO/marker/UART stores must wait behind the buffered store stream.\\nThis preserves in-order memory visibility and keeps start/stop marker timing\\nexact. Misaligned, illegal, trapping, or non-normal-RAM stores never enter the\\nFIFO and keep the current wait-for-handshake behavior. If both entries are\\nfull, the MEM stage asserts the existing precise `mem_wait` interlock.\\n\\nThis should recover store-burst stall cycles that the one-entry slot cannot\\nabsorb while keeping the hot zero-wait memory path close to the current best\\nRTL. The top-level core IO, RVFI NRET=2 port set, channel-0-only single\\nretirement convention, strict `rvfi_order` sequence, divider interlock,\\nregistered IF replay predictor, and decoder legality invariants remain\\nunchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the one-entry pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\\nFIFO. Expose valid/nonempty, full, enqueue-ready, and oldest drain\\nsignals. Support simultaneous drain and enqueue so a ready drain can\\nfree capacity for a younger store in the same cycle. Each entry holds\\nonly the byte address, replicated write data, and byte mask; RVFI\\nmetadata still retires through the normal MEM/WB path on enqueue.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat legal aligned normal-RAM stores as bufferable whenever the FIFO has\\nspace and the live dmem port is unavailable because `io_dmemReady` is low\\nor an older pending store is draining. Keep the direct ready-store path\\nunchanged when the FIFO is empty and `io_dmemReady` is high. Assert\\n`mem_wait` for loads and nonbufferable/MMIO stores behind any valid\\npending store, and for bufferable stores only when the FIFO is full.\\nPreserve misalignment traps, dmem gating, RVFI `mem_addr`, `mem_wmask`,\\nand `mem_wdata` semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the two-entry store FIFO into the existing top-level dmem\\narbitration. Give the FIFO's oldest entry priority on the external port\\nwhile valid, route live MEM-stage requests directly only when the FIFO is\\nempty, and pass FIFO fullness/capacity back to MEM. Preserve the core\\nmodule IO shape, registered IF replay wiring, RVFI channel layout, strict\\nchannel-0 order counter behavior, and tied-off channel 1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep consuming the MEM-stage `mem_wait` interlock, but update comments\\nand any naming if needed to reflect that a store can retire into either\\npending-store FIFO entry while younger non-memory work continues. Do not\\nchange load-use, imem replay readiness, EX long-operation, redirect, or\\nMEM/WB hold semantics.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the one-entry pending-store slot with a two-entry oldest-first\\nFIFO. Expose valid/nonempty, full, enqueue-ready, and oldest drain\\nsignals. Support simultaneous drain and enqueue so a ready drain can\\nfree capacity for a younger store in the same cycle. Each entry holds\\nonly the byte address, replicated write data, and byte mask; RVFI\\nmetadata still retires through the normal MEM/WB path on enqueue.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat legal aligned normal-RAM stores as bufferable whenever the FIFO has\\nspace and the live dmem port is unavailable because `io_dmemReady` is low\\nor an older pending store is draining. Keep the direct ready-store path\\nunchanged when the FIFO is empty and `io_dmemReady` is high. Assert\\n`mem_wait` for loads and nonbufferable/MMIO stores behind any valid\\npending store, and for bufferable stores only when the FIFO is full.\\nPreserve misalignment traps, dmem gating, RVFI `mem_addr`, `mem_wmask`,\\nand `mem_wdata` semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the two-entry store FIFO into the existing top-level dmem\\narbitration. Give the FIFO's oldest entry priority on the external port\\nwhile valid, route live MEM-stage requests directly only when the FIFO is\\nempty, and pass FIFO fullness/capacity back to MEM. Preserve the core\\nmodule IO shape, registered IF replay wiring, RVFI channel layout, strict\\nchannel-0 order counter behavior, and tied-off channel 1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep consuming the MEM-stage `mem_wait` interlock, but update comments\\nand any naming if needed to reflect that a store can retire into either\\npending-store FIFO entry while younger non-memory work continues. Do not\\nchange load-use, imem replay readiness, EX long-operation, redirect, or\\nMEM/WB hold semantics.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 15}\n46:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-039-r15s2\", \"title\": \"Registered I-Fetch Replay Victim Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is driven by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor with compressed, resetless tags. That predictor keeps the\\nlarge random-iStall cycle reduction of the 256-entry replay table while\\navoiding a same-cycle table read on the IF decision path. The experiment log\\nalso shows the boundary clearly: smaller replay tables gave up too much hit\\nrate, two-word replay lines recovered only a little cycle count while losing\\nFmax, and branch-direction or return predictors have consistently lost more\\nclock than their control-flow savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without widening the main table, adding a new\\nbranch update path, or putting a combinational lookup back onto current-cycle\\nfetch_ready.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is driven by the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor with compressed, resetless tags. That predictor keeps the\\nlarge random-iStall cycle reduction of the 256-entry replay table while\\navoiding a same-cycle table read on the IF decision path. The experiment log\\nalso shows the boundary clearly: smaller replay tables gave up too much hit\\nrate, two-word replay lines recovered only a little cycle count while losing\\nFmax, and branch-direction or return predictors have consistently lost more\\nclock than their control-flow savings recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are therefore most likely direct-mapped conflict\\nmisses in CoreMark's hot text footprint, not a lack of branch-direction\\nsophistication. A tiny victim predictor behind the existing 256-entry replay\\ntable targets those conflicts without widening the main table, adding a new\\nbranch update path, or putting a combinational lookup back onto current-cycle\\nfetch_ready.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current 256-entry registered lookahead replay table, compressed\\nhigh-PC tags, resetless replay payload arrays, live-imem-first policy, and\\nregistered replay candidate semantics. Add a small fully-associative victim\\nreplay buffer, for example four entries, that stores recently evicted replay\\nwords as `{valid, word_pc_tag, instr}`. The victim entries are looked up only\\nwhile computing the registered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC; the\\ncurrent IF cycle still selects only between live `io_imemData` and the\\npreviously registered replay candidate.\\n\\nOn a live `io_imemReady=1` fetch, refresh the direct-mapped replay entry for\\nthe current PC exactly as today. If that direct entry is valid for a different\\nhigh tag, move the evicted word into the victim buffer using a simple\\nround-robin or age pointer. If the live fetch PC already exists in the victim\\nbuffer, invalidate that victim copy after refreshing the direct entry so the\\ndirect table remains authoritative. On lookahead lookup, register a replay\\ncandidate on either a direct-table hit or a victim-buffer hit, with direct hit\\npriority. Replay misses still hold the PC and inject the existing IF bubble\\nwhen external imem is not ready.\\n\\nThis should recover hot-loop replay misses caused by a few PCs aliasing in\\nthe direct-mapped table while keeping the current-cycle IF path nearly\\nunchanged: `fetch_ready` is still driven from live imem readiness or the\\nregistered candidate hit, branch/JAL prediction still runs from the selected\\nlive or registered replay word, and EX still repairs any control prediction\\nmistake through resolved-next-PC mismatch recovery. Reset clears only valid\\nbits and candidate state; victim tags/instruction payloads can remain\\nresetless behind valid bits. Top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 ports, strict\\nchannel-0 order, tied-off channel 1, pending-store behavior, divider\\ninterlock, and decoder legality rules remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry fully-associative victim replay buffer behind the\\nexisting 256-entry registered lookahead replay table. Store evicted\\nreplay words with a valid bit, full word-PC tag, and instruction data;\\nclear only valid bits on reset. During registered lookahead generation,\\nchoose a direct-table hit first and a victim hit second, then register\\nthe selected candidate exactly as the current replay path does. On live\\nimem fills, move displaced valid direct entries into the victim buffer,\\ninvalidate duplicate victim entries for the just-refreshed PC, and keep\\nlive imem data as the selected instruction whenever `io_imemReady` is\\nhigh. Preserve compressed direct tags, resetless payload arrays,\\nsame-index fill bypass, redirect priority, static backward-branch and\\ndirect-JAL prediction, and the existing `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that a replayed instruction\\nmay come from the direct-mapped table or from a small registered-lookahead\\nvictim predictor. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2\\ncontract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a four-entry fully-associative victim replay buffer behind the\\nexisting 256-entry registered lookahead replay table. Store evicted\\nreplay words with a valid bit, full word-PC tag, and instruction data;\\nclear only valid bits on reset. During registered lookahead generation,\\nchoose a direct-table hit first and a victim hit second, then register\\nthe selected candidate exactly as the current replay path does. On live\\nimem fills, move displaced valid direct entries into the victim buffer,\\ninvalidate duplicate victim entries for the just-refreshed PC, and keep\\nlive imem data as the selected instruction whenever `io_imemReady` is\\nhigh. Preserve compressed direct tags, resetless payload arrays,\\nsame-index fill bypass, redirect priority, static backward-branch and\\ndirect-JAL prediction, and the existing `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that a replayed instruction\\nmay come from the direct-mapped table or from a small registered-lookahead\\nvictim predictor. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2\\ncontract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 465.7, \"delta_pct\": -7.9, \"vs_baseline\": 54.7, \"fmax_mhz\": 161.06, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3458440, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5921, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [161.06, 172.15, 144.68], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes Made\\n\\n- Added a four-entry fully-associative victim replay buffer in `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- Victim entries store `{valid, pc[31:2], instr}` and are searched only for the registered lookahead replay candidate.\\n- Kept live imem priority and the existing current-cycle IF contract: `fetch_ready` is still driven only by `imem_ready` or a previously registered replay candidate hit.\\n- Preserved direct-table priority over victim hits, including same-index live fill bypass priority.\\n- On live imem fills, the direct-mapped replay table is refreshed as before. A valid displaced direct entry with a different compressed high tag is inserted into the victim buffer through a round-robin replacement pointer.\\n- On live imem fills, matching victim entries for the just-refreshed PC are invalidated so the direct table remains authoritative.\\n- Reset clears replay valid bits, victim valid bits, replacement pointer, and candidate state only; direct/victim tag and instruction payload arrays remain resetless behind valid bits.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document that replayed words may come from either the direct table or the victim buffer.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No functional deviations from the hypothesis. The victim size is four entries and uses simple round-robin replacement.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not run directly in the worktree because the sandbox blocked the script's staging cleanup under `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher`. I ran the same unmodified script from a writable `/tmp/aa-formal-run-r15s2` mirror containing the current `rtl/` files and symlinked read-only riscv-formal resources.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- Yosys reports that the four-entry victim arrays are replaced with lists of registers during formal elaboration. That is expected for the small fully-associative structure and is not a functional issue.\\n- The victim lookup adds four tag compares to the registered lookahead path, not the current-cycle `fetch_ready` path. Fmax impact should be limited, but FPGA P&R is still the real arbiter.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass via writable temp mirror, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T06:55:12.038574\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 15}\n47:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-040-r16s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay Candidate Payloads\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only the candidate valid bit should preserve the\\ncurrent replay hit rate and all visible behavior while trimming a small but\\ntiming-relevant reset structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is built around the registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay predictor. The strongest recent micro-opt win came from compressing\\nreplay tags and making replay table payload arrays resetless behind valid\\nbits; broader attempts to remove the replay candidate PC guard or apply\\nvalid-only reset discipline across the whole pipeline regressed. That points\\nto a narrower follow-up in the same proven area.\\n\\n`if_stage` still synchronously resets the registered replay candidate PC and\\ninstruction payload to `RESET_PC` and `NOP`, even though those fields are\\nconsumed only when `replay_cand_valid_q` is asserted and the existing full-PC\\nguard matches the current fetch PC. Reset muxes and reset fanout on these\\n64 payload bits sit next to the IF replay/fetch-ready path that now matters\\nmost for Fmax. Clearing only the candidate valid bit should preserve the\\ncurrent replay hit rate and all visible behavior while trimming a small but\\ntiming-relevant reset structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the registered replay candidate payload resetless while keeping the\\ncurrent correctness guard intact. On reset, clear `replay_cand_valid_q` and\\nthe replay table valid bits exactly as today, but do not assign reset values\\nto `replay_cand_pc_q` or `replay_cand_instr_q`. The replay candidate remains\\nselectable only when `replay_cand_valid_q && (replay_cand_pc_q == pc)`, so\\nresetless PC/instruction payload bits cannot affect fetch, decode, RVFI, or\\nbranch/JAL prediction until they have been written by a real lookahead\\nlookup or fill-bypass update.\\n\\nThis intentionally does not repeat the prior candidate-PC-compare removal:\\nthe full current-PC equality check stays in place, live `io_imemData` still\\nhas priority whenever `io_imemReady` is high, replay misses still create the\\nsame PC-hold and IF bubble, and the 256-entry compressed-tag replay table,\\nstatic backward-branch/direct-JAL predictor, redirect priority, divider\\ninterlock, pending-store slot, RVFI NRET=2 ports, channel-0-only retirement,\\nand strict `rvfi_order` sequence remain unchanged. CoreMark cycles should be\\nbit-identical; the expected gain is a modest median Fmax improvement from\\nremoving reset muxing and reset routing on the replay candidate payload\\nregisters in the timing-sensitive IF replay block.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the replay-candidate reset block so reset clears only\\n`replay_cand_valid_q` and the replay table valid bits. Leave\\n`replay_cand_pc_q` and `replay_cand_instr_q` resetless behind the\\nexisting candidate-valid and full-PC match guard. Preserve live-imem\\npriority, replay fill/update behavior, fill bypass, compressed replay\\ntags, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, redirect priority,\\nand the current `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that the registered replay\\ncandidate payload is resetless behind its valid bit and current-PC guard.\\nDo not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the replay-candidate reset block so reset clears only\\n`replay_cand_valid_q` and the replay table valid bits. Leave\\n`replay_cand_pc_q` and `replay_cand_instr_q` resetless behind the\\nexisting candidate-valid and full-PC match guard. Preserve live-imem\\npriority, replay fill/update behavior, fill bypass, compressed replay\\ntags, static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction, redirect priority,\\nand the current `fetch_ready` contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that the registered replay\\ncandidate payload is resetless behind its valid bit and current-PC guard.\\nDo not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 363.19, \"delta_pct\": -28.17, \"vs_baseline\": 20.65, \"fmax_mhz\": 125.64, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5929, \"ff\": 2229, \"seeds\": [125.64, 139.88, 124.46], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/if_stage.sv` so reset clears only `replay_cand_valid_q` and the `replay_valid` table bits.\\n- Moved replay payload updates into a separate resetless clocked block. This leaves `replay_cand_pc_q`, `replay_cand_instr_q`, `replay_tag_hi`, and `replay_instr` behind their existing valid-bit protection.\\n- Preserved the existing live-imem priority, fill-bypass behavior, compressed replay tags, and full current-PC replay candidate guard.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay comment in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document the resetless replay-candidate payload.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No functional deviations from the hypothesis. The implementation uses a separate resetless payload block so synthesis does not need reset fanout or reset-driven hold muxing on the candidate PC/instruction payload flops.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known correctness concerns. During reset, replay payload contents may be arbitrary or continue updating, but replay selection remains blocked by `replay_cand_valid_q == 0` and the replay table valid bits are cleared.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` from the worktree was blocked before checks ran because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink outside the writable sandbox and the script's staging cleanup uses `rm -f` there.\\n- To run the same gate without modifying the protected formal tree, I copied the formal harness to `/tmp/formal-run.8xxSkF`, symlinked its `rtl/` back to this worktree, and ran the unmodified copied `run_all.sh` there.\\n- Temporary-harness formal result: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:15:06.858912\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 16}\n48:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-041-r16s1\", \"title\": \"Same-Cycle Store Slot Refill\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory fields\\navailable in MEM and could refill the just-drained slot. A previous\\ntwo-entry FIFO targeted the same store-burst pressure but failed placement.\\nThe existing `store_slot` state machine already supports simultaneous drain\\nand enqueue, so the lower-risk structural angle is to expose that fast refill\\ncase without adding another entry or touching the IF replay path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the two structural wins that matter most:\\nregistered instruction replay hides many random iStall cycles, and the\\none-deep pending-store slot lets a stalled normal-RAM store retire before\\nthe external dmem write is accepted. The remaining store-side bottleneck is\\nnarrower than a full second buffer entry. When the pending slot is valid, the\\nMEM stage currently treats every younger memory operation as blocked even on\\nthe exact cycle where `io_dmemReady` will drain the older buffered store.\\n\\nThat leaves a small but avoidable bubble for bursts of normal-RAM stores:\\nthe older store uses the physical dmem port, while the younger store already\\nhas its address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible memory fields\\navailable in MEM and could refill the just-drained slot. A previous\\ntwo-entry FIFO targeted the same store-burst pressure but failed placement.\\nThe existing `store_slot` state machine already supports simultaneous drain\\nand enqueue, so the lower-risk structural angle is to expose that fast refill\\ncase without adding another entry or touching the IF replay path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Allow a legal, aligned normal-RAM store in MEM to enqueue into the pending\\nstore slot on the same cycle that an older slot entry drains\\n(`store_slot_valid && io_dmemReady`). The top-level dmem port still gives the\\nolder slot entry priority for that cycle. The younger store retires once\\nthrough MEM/WB with its normal RVFI `mem_addr`, `mem_wmask`, and `mem_wdata`,\\nand the slot remains valid with the younger store's physical drain fields for\\na later external write.\\n\\nKeep all existing ordering constraints. If the slot is valid and the port is\\nnot ready, younger memory operations still wait. Loads, MMIO/marker/UART\\nstores, misaligned stores, illegal/trapping accesses, and any non-normal-RAM\\nstore still wait behind the slot and never enter it. If the slot is empty,\\nthe current direct ready-store path and low-ready enqueue path remain\\nunchanged. Younger non-memory instructions continue to pass while a buffered\\nstore drains, as they do today.\\n\\nThis should recover part of the store-burst stall cycles that motivated the\\nfailed two-entry FIFO, but with almost no additional placement pressure:\\nonly the MEM-stage enqueue/wait predicate changes, and the existing one-entry\\nslot already implements the simultaneous drain-plus-enqueue update. The\\ntop-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 channel layout, strict channel-0 order sequence,\\ntied-off channel 1, registered IF replay predictor, divider interlock, branch\\nprediction, and decoder legality invariants remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine pending-store backpressure so a bufferable normal-RAM store can\\nenqueue when the existing slot is valid and `dmem_ready` is high, because\\nthe older slot entry is draining on that same cycle. Preserve the direct\\nready-store path when the slot is empty, the existing low-ready enqueue\\npath when the slot is empty and the port stalls, and full waits for\\nloads, nonbufferable stores, MMIO/marker/UART stores, traps, and memory\\noperations behind a non-draining valid slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep the one-entry slot state machine, but document and, if needed,\\ntighten the simultaneous drain-plus-enqueue behavior so a draining older\\nstore can be replaced by a younger bufferable store in the same clock\\nedge. The slot still holds only physical drain fields; RVFI metadata\\nremains retired through MEM/WB on the enqueue cycle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current top-level dmem arbitration with pending-slot\\npriority while valid. Update comments or local naming only if needed to\\nmake clear that `store_slot_valid && io_dmemReady` represents available\\nenqueue capacity after the older store drains, without changing the core\\nmodule IO, RVFI ports, IF replay wiring, or channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refine pending-store backpressure so a bufferable normal-RAM store can\\nenqueue when the existing slot is valid and `dmem_ready` is high, because\\nthe older slot entry is draining on that same cycle. Preserve the direct\\nready-store path when the slot is empty, the existing low-ready enqueue\\npath when the slot is empty and the port stalls, and full waits for\\nloads, nonbufferable stores, MMIO/marker/UART stores, traps, and memory\\noperations behind a non-draining valid slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep the one-entry slot state machine, but document and, if needed,\\ntighten the simultaneous drain-plus-enqueue behavior so a draining older\\nstore can be replaced by a younger bufferable store in the same clock\\nedge. The slot still holds only physical drain fields; RVFI metadata\\nremains retired through MEM/WB on the enqueue cycle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current top-level dmem arbitration with pending-slot\\npriority while valid. Update comments or local naming only if needed to\\nmake clear that `store_slot_valid && io_dmemReady` represents available\\nenqueue capacity after the older store drains, without changing the core\\nmodule IO, RVFI ports, IF replay wiring, or channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 16}\n49:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-042-r16s2\", \"title\": \"Replay-Cached Next-PC Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That means an external imem stall served by\\nreplay still has a flop-to-flop path from `replay_cand_instr_q` through\\nbranch/JAL immediate reconstruction, target adders, the next-PC mux, and the\\nPC register. The replay table already knows the instruction for that PC from\\nan earlier live fetch, so its static next-PC prediction can be cached at fill\\ntime and replayed as metadata instead of recomputed in the hot stall path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It keeps the large random-iStall cycle\\nwin of the 256-entry replay cache while avoiding a same-cycle table lookup\\non the IF decision path. Later predictor experiments show that adding new\\nbranch-direction tables, return predictors, victim entries, or wider replay\\nlines either saves very few cycles or loses more Fmax than it recovers.\\n\\nThe remaining promising predictor work is therefore inside the replay path\\nthat already wins. On a replay hit, the IF stage currently feeds the cached\\ninstruction word back through the same static branch/JAL decode and target\\nadders used for live imem data. That means an external imem stall served by\\nreplay still has a flop-to-flop path from `replay_cand_instr_q` through\\nbranch/JAL immediate reconstruction, target adders, the next-PC mux, and the\\nPC register. The replay table already knows the instruction for that PC from\\nan earlier live fetch, so its static next-PC prediction can be cached at fill\\ntime and replayed as metadata instead of recomputed in the hot stall path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend each instruction replay-table entry with compact static next-PC\\nprediction metadata: a predicted-taken bit and a predicted target or\\npredicted next-PC value computed from the live `io_imemData` and current PC\\nwhen the entry is filled. The existing instruction word, compressed high-PC\\ntag, valid bit, live-imem-first policy, resetless payload arrays, registered\\nlookahead candidate, and same-index fill bypass remain in place.\\n\\nWhen `io_imemReady=1`, IF still uses the live instruction word and the\\ncurrent static backward-branch/direct-JAL predictor exactly as it does\\ntoday; this preserves the formal-ready path and keeps live imem authoritative.\\nThe replay entry is refreshed with the live instruction plus the computed\\nmetadata. When `io_imemReady=0` and the registered replay candidate hits the\\ncurrent PC, IF should use the registered metadata to drive `predicted_taken`,\\n`predicted_target`, and the PC update, while still passing the cached\\ninstruction word into decode. Replay misses keep the current PC-hold and\\nIF-bubble behavior.\\n\\nThis should be bit-equivalent for all replayed instructions because the\\ncached metadata is just the current static predictor result computed at the\\nprevious live fetch of the same immutable instruction word. EX still checks\\nthe carried prediction against the resolved next PC, so any branch outcome\\nmismatch is repaired exactly as today. The expected gain is a modest Fmax\\nimprovement by removing branch/JAL immediate decode and target-add work from\\nthe replay-hit PC update path, with no intended CoreMark cycle-count change\\nand no top-level IO, RVFI, divider, memory, or retirement-contract changes.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add replay-table metadata arrays and registered candidate fields for the\\nstatic next-PC prediction associated with each cached instruction. Fill\\nthem on live `io_imemReady` fetches using the same backward-branch and\\ndirect-JAL prediction rule currently computed from `fetch_instr`. On a\\nreplay hit during an external imem stall, bypass the instruction decode\\nand target adders for PC prediction by using the registered metadata to\\ndrive `predicted_taken`, `predicted_target`, and `next_pc`. Keep live\\nimem cycles on the existing live decode path, preserve compressed replay\\ntags, resetless payload arrays, valid-bit reset, fill bypass, redirect\\npriority, `fetch_ready` semantics, and replay-miss stalls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID prediction comments to state that replayed\\ninstructions may carry precomputed static next-PC metadata from the\\nregistered replay predictor, while live imem instructions still compute\\nprediction metadata directly from the live word. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields,\\ntop-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add replay-table metadata arrays and registered candidate fields for the\\nstatic next-PC prediction associated with each cached instruction. Fill\\nthem on live `io_imemReady` fetches using the same backward-branch and\\ndirect-JAL prediction rule currently computed from `fetch_instr`. On a\\nreplay hit during an external imem stall, bypass the instruction decode\\nand target adders for PC prediction by using the registered metadata to\\ndrive `predicted_taken`, `predicted_target`, and `next_pc`. Keep live\\nimem cycles on the existing live decode path, preserve compressed replay\\ntags, resetless payload arrays, valid-bit reset, fill bypass, redirect\\npriority, `fetch_ready` semantics, and replay-miss stalls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID prediction comments to state that replayed\\ninstructions may carry precomputed static next-PC metadata from the\\nregistered replay predictor, while live imem instructions still compute\\nprediction metadata directly from the live word. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, architectural state, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields,\\ntop-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 476.16, \"delta_pct\": -5.83, \"vs_baseline\": 58.17, \"fmax_mhz\": 164.72, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5955, \"ff\": 2228, \"seeds\": [164.72, 162.5, 165.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- Extended the IF replay table with resetless static prediction metadata arrays:\\n  `replay_pred_taken` and `replay_pred_next_pc`.\\n- Added registered lookahead candidate fields for the replay metadata, including\\n  same-index fill bypass alongside the existing instruction bypass.\\n- Split IF static prediction so live imem cycles compute prediction from\\n  `imem_data` and `pc`, while replay-hit cycles select the registered metadata\\n  instead of decoding `replay_cand_instr_q` for branch/JAL immediates.\\n- Kept live imem authoritative: when `imem_ready` is high, IF uses the live\\n  instruction word and live-computed prediction metadata.\\n- Updated `core_pkg.sv` comments to describe live-computed versus replay-cached\\n  IF prediction metadata without changing bundle widths.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- The replay table stores predicted next PC rather than a separate target value.\\n  This matches the hypothesis allowance for either target or next-PC metadata and\\n  avoids recomputing the not-taken `pc+4` value on replay hits.\\n- The metadata is computed as the ungated static prediction at fill time. Runtime\\n  `flush`, `redirect`, and `stall` gating is still applied when metadata is used,\\n  preserving the previous IF behavior while avoiding stale false predictions from\\n  entries filled during a pipeline stall.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The change adds 33 metadata bits per replay entry. This is intentional for the\\n  requested Fmax tradeoff and does not alter top-level IO, RVFI, decode legality,\\n  memory, divider, or retirement contracts.\\n- No known behavioral concerns. Replay misses still hold PC and bubble IF through\\n  the existing `fetch_ready` path.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` in the worktree could not stage files because\\n  `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink outside the writable sandbox; the script\\n  failed before generating checks with `Operation not permitted` on the stale\\n  staged `.sv` cleanup.\\n- Equivalent local formal run from a writable `/tmp` copy of the project and\\n  riscv-formal framework: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:19:06.776737\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 16}\n50:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-043-r17s0\", \"title\": \"RTL-Only Hot/Cold ALU Opcode Split\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opcode/mux shape, so this remains the\\ncleanest unevaluated micro-opt angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already removed the cold DIV/REM arithmetic from\\nthe universal EX path and gained another large timing win from trimming the\\nregistered instruction replay metadata. The remaining micro-opt opportunity\\nshould therefore avoid new queues, predictors, reset rewrites, or\\nmulti-cycle units, and instead reshape logic that is already on the hot\\none-cycle EX datapath.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still uses a dense ALU operation encoding and a single broad\\nresult case for the common RV32I operations, the single-cycle multiply\\nresults, and dummy DIV/REM results that are now supplied by `div_unit.sv`.\\nThe two previous hot/cold ALU hypotheses never produced architectural data\\nbecause their implementations were rejected for touching an off-limits test\\nhelper, not because the RTL idea failed formal, cosim, or FPGA placement.\\nThe source still has the same dense opcode/mux shape, so this remains the\\ncleanest unevaluated micro-opt angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the ALU operation constants so `alu_op[4]` is an explicit\\nM-extension discriminator. Keep every RV32I/simple operation at\\n`op[4]=0`, and move `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHSU`, `MULHU`, `DIV`, `DIVU`,\\n`REM`, and `REMU` to `op[4]=1`, preferably `5'b10000` through\\n`5'b10111` in RV32M funct3 order.\\n\\nRefactor `rtl/alu.sv` into two local result muxes. The hot simple mux covers\\nADD/SUB, logic, compare, shift, and LUI only. The cold M mux covers the\\nexisting multiply results and the existing dummy zero result for DIV/REM\\nopcodes handled by the iterative divider. Drive the final ALU output with a\\nsingle `op[4] ? m_out : simple_out` select.\\n\\nThis does not change any arithmetic primitive, instruction latency,\\nforwarding path, branch predictor, memory behavior, divider protocol,\\ntop-level IO, RVFI field, retirement order, or CoreMark cycle count. The\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS multiply substitutions stay in the cold M mux under the\\nnew constants, while divide/remainder ALTOPS behavior remains in\\n`div_unit.sv`. The expected benefit is a modest median Fmax improvement from\\nreducing decode and result-mux depth on the common RV32I EX result path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU to an `op[4]=1` encoding, preferably\\ncontiguous values `5'b10000` through `5'b10111` matching RV32M funct3\\norder. Keep all ADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI operations at\\n`op[4]=0`. Do not change bundle widths, RVFI fields, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the single broad ALU result case into `simple_out` and `m_out`\\nsub-muxes. `simple_out` should cover only the RV32I hot path. `m_out`\\nshould reuse the existing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU arithmetic and ALTOPS\\nformulas, while DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU continue to return zero because the\\niterative divider supplies their real result. Select the final output\\nonly with `op[4]`.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign ALU_MUL through ALU_REMU to an `op[4]=1` encoding, preferably\\ncontiguous values `5'b10000` through `5'b10111` matching RV32M funct3\\norder. Keep all ADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI operations at\\n`op[4]=0`. Do not change bundle widths, RVFI fields, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Split the single broad ALU result case into `simple_out` and `m_out`\\nsub-muxes. `simple_out` should cover only the RV32I hot path. `m_out`\\nshould reuse the existing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU arithmetic and ALTOPS\\nformulas, while DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU continue to return zero because the\\niterative divider supplies their real result. Select the final output\\nonly with `op[4]`.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 529.35, \"delta_pct\": 4.69, \"vs_baseline\": 75.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 183.12, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5930, \"ff\": 2235, \"seeds\": [192.01, 171.94, 183.12], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes made\\n\\n- Re-encoded the RV32M ALU constants in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` so all M-extension\\n  operations have `alu_op[4] = 1`.\\n- Assigned the M-extension constants contiguously as `5'b10000` through\\n  `5'b10111`, matching RV32M `funct3` order:\\n  `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHSU`, `MULHU`, `DIV`, `DIVU`, `REM`, `REMU`.\\n- Refactored `rtl/alu.sv` from one broad result `case` into two local result\\n  muxes:\\n  - `simple_out` covers ADD/SUB, logic, compare, shifts, and LUI.\\n  - `m_out` covers multiply results plus the existing zero result for\\n    DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, whose real result still comes from `div_unit.sv`.\\n- The final ALU result is selected only by `op[4] ? m_out : simple_out`.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No architectural or pipeline behavior was changed.\\n- No bundle widths, RVFI fields, decoder legality defaults, divider protocol,\\n  forwarding behavior, retirement order, top-level IO, or test/eval files were\\n  changed.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not run directly in this sandbox because\\n  `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared tree outside the writable\\n  worktree, and the script's staging cleanup hit `Operation not permitted`.\\n  I ran the unmodified script from a temporary writable copy of the project\\n  instead.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- None known. The change is intentionally limited to ALU op encoding and ALU\\n  result mux shape.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked in-place by sandbox permissions before any\\n  proof ran.\\n- Equivalent local formal self-check from temporary writable staging copy:\\n  pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:32:03.632312\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 17}\n51:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-044-r17s1\", \"title\": \"Tiny MRU Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already uses the two structural ideas that have paid\\nclearly: a one-deep pending-store slot for store-only dmem backpressure and\\na registered instruction replay table for random iStall. The remaining\\nstructural stalls are concentrated on normal-RAM loads. A load still freezes\\nthe whole in-order pipeline whenever io_dmemReady is low, even if the same\\naligned word was returned recently.\\n\\nThe prior 16-entry MEM-stage load replay buffer showed that this stall source\\nis real: it reduced CoreMark cycles on top of the instruction replay design.\\nIt missed the current best because the same-cycle table lookup, tag compare,\\nand store-update logic dropped Fmax too much. A much smaller MRU replay latch\\ntargets the same dmem-stall opportunity while keeping the MEM path close to\\nthe current best design.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry most-recently-used normal-RAM load replay latch in MEM. Each\\nentry stores a valid bit, an aligned word address tag, and the raw 32-bit word\\nreturned by a previously accepted legal normal-RAM load. On io_dmemReady=1,\\nloads keep using live io_dmemRData exactly as today and update the MRU latch\\nwith the aligned word and raw data. On io_dmemReady=0, a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load may complete from the latch if either entry matches the\\naligned word address: suppress dmem_ren, use the cached raw word for\\nsign/zero extension and RVFI mem_rdata, and do not assert mem_wait.\\n\\nReplay remains conservative. It is disabled while the pending store slot is\\nvalid, for MMIO/marker/UART addresses, for misaligned or trapping accesses,\\nand for stores. Normal-RAM stores byte-merge any matching replay entry before\\nthey retire or enqueue into the pending-store slot; if a simple merge is not\\navailable for a corner case, invalidating the matching entry is acceptable.\\nReplay misses preserve the current dmem wait behavior exactly.\\n\\nThis is a structural latency-hiding change, not a MEM-to-EX bypass. Cached\\ndata is consumed only at the MEM retirement boundary, dependent younger\\ninstructions still use the existing load-use discipline, and the top-level IO,\\nRVFI NRET=2 channel layout, channel-0-only retirement order, divider\\ninterlock, registered IF replay predictor, branch prediction, pending-store\\nordering, and decoder legality invariants remain unchanged. The expected win\\nis a small cycle reduction from repeated normal-RAM loads during random\\ndmem stalls with much less Fmax cost than the earlier 16-entry load replay\\ntable.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_mru_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny two-entry MRU replay latch for normal-RAM load words. Entries\\nhold valid, aligned word address tag, and raw 32-bit data. Provide lookup\\nhit/data for the current MEM load, update from accepted live load\\nresponses, and byte-merge or invalidate matching entries on legal\\nnormal-RAM stores. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the MRU replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when dmem_ready is low, the current access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot is valid, and the latch hits. On a\\nhit, gate off dmem_ren, clear mem_wait, and drive load extraction plus\\nRVFI mem_rdata from the cached word. Keep replay misses and all\\nnonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document the\\nMRU load replay eligibility and raw-word metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_mru_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny two-entry MRU replay latch for normal-RAM load words. Entries\\nhold valid, aligned word address tag, and raw 32-bit data. Provide lookup\\nhit/data for the current MEM load, update from accepted live load\\nresponses, and byte-merge or invalidate matching entries on legal\\nnormal-RAM stores. Reset clears only valid bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the MRU replay lookup into load handling. Select cached raw word\\ndata only when dmem_ready is low, the current access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot is valid, and the latch hits. On a\\nhit, gate off dmem_ren, clear mem_wait, and drive load extraction plus\\nRVFI mem_rdata from the cached word. Keep replay misses and all\\nnonbufferable accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document the\\nMRU load replay eligibility and raw-word metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI field\\nwidths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 404.58, \"delta_pct\": -19.99, \"vs_baseline\": 34.39, \"fmax_mhz\": 139.0, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3435657, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5909, \"ff\": 2224, \"seeds\": [139.0, 142.94, 131.86], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/load_mru_replay.sv`, a two-entry MRU latch for legal normal-RAM load words. Each entry tracks a valid bit, aligned word address tag, and raw 32-bit word data.\\n- Integrated the latch in `rtl/mem_stage.sv`.\\n  - Ready normal-RAM loads still use live `dmem_rdata` and fill the latch.\\n  - Stalled legal normal-RAM load hits suppress `dmem_ren`, clear `mem_wait`, and use cached raw data for load extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata`.\\n  - Replay is disabled while the pending-store slot is valid, and for stores, MMIO/marker/UART/OOB addresses, illegal accesses, and misaligned accesses.\\n  - Legal normal-RAM stores byte-merge matching latch entries when they retire or enqueue into the pending-store slot.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No `core_pkg.sv` changes were needed; the replay eligibility stayed local to MEM.\\n- The implementation promotes an entry-1 replay hit to entry 0 so the two entries behave as a true MRU pair. This is a small sequential state update and does not add another table.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known functional concerns from lint or formal. The latch intentionally resets only valid bits; tags and data are ignored until valid.\\n- This still adds two 32-bit tag compares in the MEM decision path, so the final FPGA result will determine whether the small cycle win outweighs timing cost.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before solver startup in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared checkout outside the writable worktree, and the script's staging cleanup could not remove stale files there (`Operation not permitted`).\\n- Equivalent local formal flow from a temporary writable mirror under `/tmp`, using the same `checks.cfg`, `wrapper.sv`, current `rtl/*.sv`, and riscv-formal framework files: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:35:07.182987\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 17}\n52:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-045-r17s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead Agree Branch Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned direction-table lookup into a\\nregistered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC. That preserves the\\ncurrent IF replay structure and avoids adding a current-cycle counter-array\\nread to the branch/JAL target and fetch-ready path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor win is the compressed registered lookahead\\ninstruction replay table. It attacks random iStall cycles without putting a\\nsame-cycle replay lookup on the IF decision path. Other predictor attempts\\nshow the remaining tradeoff clearly: learned conditional-branch tables did\\nreduce CoreMark cycles, but their combinational IF table reads and EX update\\nrouting cost more Fmax than the saved redirects recovered. Return predictors\\nand replay capacity variants had the same problem or saved too few cycles.\\n\\nThe most promising predictor angle is therefore to revisit dynamic branch\\ndirection with the timing discipline that made instruction replay work.\\nKeep the existing static BTFNT plus direct-JAL predictor as the cold and\\ntarget-generation path, but move the learned direction-table lookup into a\\nregistered lookahead candidate for the next fetch PC. That preserves the\\ncurrent IF replay structure and avoids adding a current-cycle counter-array\\nread to the branch/JAL target and fetch-ready path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small PC-indexed two-bit agree predictor for conditional branch\\ndirection, but read it one cycle ahead in IF. The table is indexed by the\\nsame selected next fetch PC used to maintain the registered instruction\\nreplay candidate. Each cycle registers the selected counter prediction for\\nthe PC expected to be current after the next clock edge. When IF later sees a\\nlegal aligned B-type branch at that PC, it combines the registered\\nagree/disagree bit with the existing static direction rule: cold counters\\nreset to strongly agree, so cold behavior is exactly the current backward\\ntaken / forward not-taken policy. Direct JAL prediction remains unchanged,\\nand JALR remains unpredicted.\\n\\nConditional branches update the agree table from EX when the branch advances\\ninto EX/MEM. The update trains toward whether the resolved branch outcome\\nagreed with the static direction for that branch PC, and is gated so dmem\\nholds cannot update the same branch repeatedly. Redirect recovery remains\\nthe existing resolved-next-PC mismatch check, so aliasing or a stale\\nregistered prediction can only cost redirect bubbles and cannot affect RVFI\\ncorrectness.\\n\\nThis targets the cycle-count signal seen in the earlier agree-predictor\\nexperiment while avoiding its biggest timing cost: the current-cycle IF\\ntable lookup. The design adds no BTB, RAS, replay capacity change, pipeline\\nstage, load-data bypass, top-level IO change, or retirement-width change.\\nThe existing registered IF replay predictor, pending-store slot, divider\\ninterlock, strict channel-0 RVFI order sequence, and tied-off channel 1\\nremain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small two-bit agree-direction table, such as 64 entries indexed by\\nfetch PC bits, with counters reset to strongly agree. Read the selected\\ncounter into a registered lookahead prediction using the same next-PC\\npriority as the replay candidate: EX redirect target first, then the\\npredicted/fallthrough next PC when fetch advances, otherwise the held PC.\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, combine the registered agree bit with\\nthe existing static direction rule to choose predicted_taken. Preserve\\ndirect-JAL prediction, JALR unpredicted behavior, compressed instruction\\nreplay tags, resetless replay payload arrays, live-imem priority,\\nreplay fill bypass, redirect priority, and fetch_ready semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit one-cycle branch predictor update sideband signals for valid,\\nnon-misaligned conditional branches when EX/MEM is allowed to advance:\\nupdate_valid, update_pc, update_static_taken, and update_actual_taken.\\nKeep redirect generation based on resolved next PC versus the carried\\npredicted next PC, and do not change branch target calculation, trap\\nhandling, divider launch/completion, forwarding, or RVFI metadata.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX-stage conditional-branch update signals back to IF without\\nchanging the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0-only\\nsingle retirement convention, strict rvfi_order behavior, pending-store\\nslot arbitration, registered instruction replay wiring, or channel-1\\ntie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the prediction metadata comments to document that conditional\\nbranch direction may come from a registered lookahead agree predictor,\\nwhile direct JAL and replayed instruction handling remain otherwise\\nunchanged. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder\\nlegality defaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small two-bit agree-direction table, such as 64 entries indexed by\\nfetch PC bits, with counters reset to strongly agree. Read the selected\\ncounter into a registered lookahead prediction using the same next-PC\\npriority as the replay candidate: EX redirect target first, then the\\npredicted/fallthrough next PC when fetch advances, otherwise the held PC.\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, combine the registered agree bit with\\nthe existing static direction rule to choose predicted_taken. Preserve\\ndirect-JAL prediction, JALR unpredicted behavior, compressed instruction\\nreplay tags, resetless replay payload arrays, live-imem priority,\\nreplay fill bypass, redirect priority, and fetch_ready semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit one-cycle branch predictor update sideband signals for valid,\\nnon-misaligned conditional branches when EX/MEM is allowed to advance:\\nupdate_valid, update_pc, update_static_taken, and update_actual_taken.\\nKeep redirect generation based on resolved next PC versus the carried\\npredicted next PC, and do not change branch target calculation, trap\\nhandling, divider launch/completion, forwarding, or RVFI metadata.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX-stage conditional-branch update signals back to IF without\\nchanging the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0-only\\nsingle retirement convention, strict rvfi_order behavior, pending-store\\nslot arbitration, registered instruction replay wiring, or channel-1\\ntie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the prediction metadata comments to document that conditional\\nbranch direction may come from a registered lookahead agree predictor,\\nwhile direct JAL and replayed instruction handling remain otherwise\\nunchanged. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder\\nlegality defaults, RVFI fields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.84, \"delta_pct\": -32.59, \"vs_baseline\": 13.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 115.73, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3395390, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6327, \"ff\": 2368, \"seeds\": [115.73, 111.22, 121.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Registered Lookahead Agree Branch Predictor\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added a 64-entry PC-indexed two-bit agree predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- The agree table resets counters to strongly agree (`2'b11`), so cold conditional-branch behavior remains the existing static backward-taken / forward-not-taken rule.\\n- IF reads the agree counter into a registered lookahead candidate using the same selected next-PC priority as the instruction replay candidate: EX redirect target, then predicted/fallthrough next PC when fetch advances, otherwise the held PC.\\n- Legal aligned B-type branches now combine the registered agree/disagree bit with the static direction rule. Direct JAL prediction and JALR-unpredicted behavior are unchanged.\\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv` now emits one-cycle conditional-branch predictor update sideband signals when a valid non-misaligned branch is allowed to advance into EX/MEM.\\n- `rtl/core.sv` wires the EX update sideband back to IF without changing the top-level IO.\\n- `rtl/core_pkg.sv` comments now document that conditional branch direction may come from the registered lookahead agree predictor.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- The agree table uses 64 entries as suggested. It is direct-mapped and untagged; aliasing is intentionally handled by normal EX redirect recovery.\\n- Simultaneous update and lookahead read of the same agree-table entry uses the pre-update counter value for the registered candidate. This keeps the IF path simple and still only affects prediction quality.\\n- A Verilator `UNUSEDSIGNAL` pragma marks the unused high and low bits of `bp_update_pc` in IF, because the table intentionally indexes only `pc[7:2]`.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- Aliasing or a stale registered agree bit can train/predict the wrong direction, but any bad prediction is corrected by the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch redirect.\\n- The in-tree `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a protected path outside the writable worktree, causing `rm -f \\\"$CORE_DIR\\\"/*.sv` to fail with `Operation not permitted` before any checks ran.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before checks by the sandbox staging issue described above.\\n- Equivalent formal run from a throwaway `/tmp` mirror of `formal/` and `rtl/`, using the same `run_all.sh`: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:37:08.616973\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 17}\n53:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-046-r18s0\", \"title\": \"Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best RTL has already harvested the high-leverage Fmax wins:\\nDIV/REM is cold and iterative, the IF replay predictor hides most random\\niStall loss, replay tags are compressed/resetless, the RVFI order counter is\\nsegmented, and the ALU result mux has a hot/cold split. The next micro-opt\\nshould therefore avoid new memories, predictors, queues, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining always-present cycle loss in the control path is the\\nconservative load-use detector. It currently stalls whenever the load in\\nID/EX has a nonzero rd matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield of the\\nIF/ID instruction. That is necessary for true consumers, but false for many\\nlegal RV32I encodings: OP-IMM, LOAD, and JALR do not read rs2; LUI, AUIPC,\\nJAL, FENCE, and EBREAK do not read either source. CoreMark has many pointer\\nupdates, immediate arithmetic, loads, and control instructions after loads,\\nso these false matches can still inject bubbles even after the instruction\\nreplay predictor improves frontend availability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the load-use interlock so it only compares the ID/EX load destination\\nagainst source operands that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Add a\\nsmall source-use predecode in the hazard path with conservative defaults:\\nrs1 is used by OP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used by OP,\\nSTORE, and BRANCH; LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and the exact EBREAK encoding are\\nsource-free. Unknown opcodes and reserved/unclear SYSTEM encodings remain\\nconservative and are treated as using both raw source fields so illegal-trap\\nRVFI behavior is not weakened.\\n\\nGate the predicate with both stage valid bits: a bubble in ID/EX is not a\\nload producer, and an invalid IF/ID payload has no consumer sources. True\\nload-use dependencies keep the exact existing one-cycle bubble, including\\nload-to-store address dependencies and load-to-branch comparisons. The change\\ndoes not add a MEM-to-EX data path, alter forwarding, change memory ordering,\\ntouch the IF replay predictor, change the divider interlock, or modify any\\nRVFI field or top-level IO. The expected benefit is a small CoreMark cycle\\nreduction with limited Fmax risk because the added logic is only a compact\\nopcode/source-use filter in the existing hazard predicate.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1-or-rs2 load-use comparison with a source-qualified\\npredicate. Add inputs for IF/ID valid, ID/EX valid, and the IF/ID\\ninstruction or opcode/funct fields. Compute uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from\\nthe opcode with conservative handling for unknown or unclear encodings,\\nand stall only when the load rd matches a source that is actually read.\\nPreserve the existing imem replay readiness, redirect flush, dmem wait,\\nEX long-operation, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire IF/ID valid, ID/EX valid, and the IF/ID instruction or needed\\nopcode/funct fields into the hazard unit. Keep the core module IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port set, channel-0-only retirement behavior, strict rvfi_order\\nsequence, registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\nand tied-off channel 1 unchanged.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw rs1-or-rs2 load-use comparison with a source-qualified\\npredicate. Add inputs for IF/ID valid, ID/EX valid, and the IF/ID\\ninstruction or opcode/funct fields. Compute uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 from\\nthe opcode with conservative handling for unknown or unclear encodings,\\nand stall only when the load rd matches a source that is actually read.\\nPreserve the existing imem replay readiness, redirect flush, dmem wait,\\nEX long-operation, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire IF/ID valid, ID/EX valid, and the IF/ID instruction or needed\\nopcode/funct fields into the hazard unit. Keep the core module IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port set, channel-0-only retirement behavior, strict rvfi_order\\nsequence, registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\nand tied-off channel 1 unchanged.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 365.3, \"delta_pct\": -30.99, \"vs_baseline\": 21.35, \"fmax_mhz\": 126.37, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5997, \"ff\": 2240, \"seeds\": [126.37, 131.89, 117.63], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Source-Qualified Load-Use Interlock\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/source_use_decode.sv`, a compact shared predecode for architectural source usage.\\n- Updated `rtl/hazard_unit.sv` so load-use stalls require:\\n  - a valid ID/EX load producer,\\n  - a valid IF/ID consumer,\\n  - a nonzero matching load destination,\\n  - and a match only against source operands the IF/ID instruction actually reads.\\n- Updated `rtl/core.sv` to wire IF/ID and ID/EX valid bits plus the IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit.\\n- Added a MEM-stage RVFI source-data repair path from the current WB value. This keeps the core's existing raw RVFI source-address convention intact for cosim while ensuring a false raw source match behind a load still reports the current architectural source data.\\n- Qualified EX/MEM forwarding so loads are not forwarded from EX/MEM. True load consumers still wait for the existing load-use bubble and receive data from MEM/WB; this prevents false consumers from recording a load address as raw RVFI source data.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- I added a small `source_use_decode` module instead of open-coding the opcode mask directly inside `hazard_unit`.\\n- I kept raw RVFI source addresses because the existing cosim reference compares them directly for every instruction. Instead of masking unused sources to x0/0, `mem_stage` repairs raw source data from the immediately older WB instruction when needed. This affects RVFI metadata only; it does not feed EX operands or add a load-data bypass for execution.\\n- EX/MEM forwarding for loads is now explicitly disabled. The old forwarding select was harmless only because the conservative load-use interlock kept true consumers away from that path; after source qualification, false consumers can reach EX while a load is in MEM, so forwarding the load address would corrupt raw RVFI source metadata.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known functional concerns after lint, formal, and cosim.\\n- The added source-use decode is combinational in the existing hazard path. The MEM-stage RVFI repair adds small compare/mux logic on source metadata; FPGA timing will determine the actual Fmax impact.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before solver startup because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a protected checkout outside the writable sandbox, and the script's staging cleanup failed with `Operation not permitted`.\\n- Equivalent formal run from `/tmp/codex-formal-hyp-20260428-046-r18s0`, using the same `run_all.sh`, `checks.cfg`, `wrapper.sv`, current `rtl/*.sv`, and riscv-formal framework files: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n- `make cosim`: pass, `elfs_tested: 2`.\\n- Intermediate formal attempts failed `reg_ch0` on false raw source dependencies. The first counterexample showed stale raw source data behind a load; the second showed an AUIPC recording a misaligned load's address through EX/MEM forwarding. The WB-to-MEM metadata repair plus load-qualified EX/MEM forwarding fixed those failures while preserving raw RVFI source addresses.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:58:39.012355\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 18}\n54:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-047-r18s1\", \"title\": \"Decode-Exact Load-Use Interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has strong timing wins from the registered instruction\\nreplay predictor, segmented RVFI order counter, pending-store slot, and\\nhot/cold ALU split. The remaining structural improvement should therefore\\navoid another cache, queue, predictor table, or load-data forwarding path that\\ncould disturb Fmax.\\n\\nThe load-use interlock is still structurally conservative. It stalls whenever\\na load in ID/EX has a nonzero destination matching either raw rs1 or raw rs2\\nbitfield of the IF/ID instruction. That is correct for true consumers, but it\\ncreates false bubbles for instructions where one or both fields are not\\nsource operands: OP-IMM and LOAD do not read rs2, JAL/LUI/AUIPC read neither,\\nFENCE/EBREAK read neither, and JALR reads only rs1. CoreMark's optimized code\\nhas dense immediate arithmetic, address-generation loads, calls, and loop\\ncontrol, so these false one-cycle bubbles are a plausible remaining IPC loss.\\n\\nA prior operand-aware interlock attempt broke selftest, so this hypothesis is\\ndeliberately conservative: decode source-use exactly for known legal\\ninstruction classes, keep reserved or unknown encodings on the current\\nboth-source stall policy, and do not add any MEM-to-EX load-data path. The\\ngoal is to recover only provably false load-use stalls while leaving all true\\nload consumers, dmem waits, branch recovery, divider interlocks, and RVFI\\nordering unchanged.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small decode-light source-use classifier for the IF/ID instruction and\\nuse it in the load-use hazard predicate. A load in ID/EX should stall the next\\ninstruction only when the next instruction is valid and actually reads the\\nmatching source:\\n\\n- rs1 is used by OP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and legal JALR.\\n- rs2 is used by OP, STORE, and BRANCH.\\n- JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE/FENCE.I, and EBREAK use no integer source register.\\n- Reserved funct3/funct7 combinations and unknown opcodes remain\\n  conservative by treating both rs fields as possible sources.\\n\\nThe existing one-cycle bubble remains for every true load-use dependency,\\nincluding store address dependencies, branch compares, JALR bases, ALU\\nconsumers, and any conservative illegal/unknown instruction class. The change\\nshould not forward load data, alter the MEM stage, widen retirement, or\\nchange the top-level IO. It only refines when the frontend and ID/EX register\\nare stalled for a load in EX.\\n\\nThis preserves the hard invariants: decoder legality still defaults illegal,\\nRVFI traps and order remain generated at MEM/WB, channel 1 stays tied off, and\\nall memory and timing-marker behavior remains unchanged. The expected benefit\\nis a small CoreMark cycle reduction with minimal Fmax risk because the added\\nlogic is opcode/funct predecode on the existing hazard path, not a new data\\npath or stateful predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw load-use comparison against both IF/ID register fields\\nwith a source-use-aware predicate. Add conservative decode-light logic for\\nknown legal RV32IM instruction classes, defaulting unknown or reserved\\nencodings to uses_rs1=1 and uses_rs2=1. Gate the rs1 comparison with\\nuses_rs1 and the rs2 comparison with uses_rs2, while preserving the\\nexisting redirect, imem replay readiness, MEM wait, and EX long-operation\\nstall behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass any additional IF/ID instruction validity or predecoded source-use\\nsideband needed by the hazard unit. Keep the core module IO unchanged,\\npreserve the registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\ndivider interlock, RVFI NRET=2 field set, strict channel-0 order sequence,\\nand tied-off channel 1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"If useful, add compact comments or bundle fields documenting IF/ID\\nsource-use metadata for hazard detection. Do not change RVFI fields,\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, ALU op\\nencodings, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the raw load-use comparison against both IF/ID register fields\\nwith a source-use-aware predicate. Add conservative decode-light logic for\\nknown legal RV32IM instruction classes, defaulting unknown or reserved\\nencodings to uses_rs1=1 and uses_rs2=1. Gate the rs1 comparison with\\nuses_rs1 and the rs2 comparison with uses_rs2, while preserving the\\nexisting redirect, imem replay readiness, MEM wait, and EX long-operation\\nstall behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass any additional IF/ID instruction validity or predecoded source-use\\nsideband needed by the hazard unit. Keep the core module IO unchanged,\\npreserve the registered IF replay wiring, pending-store-slot arbitration,\\ndivider interlock, RVFI NRET=2 field set, strict channel-0 order sequence,\\nand tied-off channel 1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"If useful, add compact comments or bundle fields documenting IF/ID\\nsource-use metadata for hazard detection. Do not change RVFI fields,\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, ALU op\\nencodings, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 18}\n55:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-048-r18s2\", \"title\": \"Kill-Gated I-Fetch Replay Fills\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design already has the predictor feature with the strongest\\nmeasured payoff: a 256-entry registered lookahead instruction replay table\\nwith compressed tags. It hides random iStall bubbles without putting a\\nsame-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up predictor attempts\\nthat added branch-direction state, return targets, victim entries, wider\\nreplay lines, or replay-cached next-PC metadata either saved too few cycles\\nor lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor slot should therefore keep the existing replay structure\\nand target replay quality rather than add another table. The current IF\\nstage refreshes the replay table on every live `io_imemReady` fetch, even\\nwhen that fetch is being killed by an EX redirect or IF flush. Those killed\\nwords are architecturally correct immutable instructions, but they are\\nwrong-path for the current execution and can evict useful hot-loop replay\\nentries in the direct-mapped table. Avoiding this pollution is a narrow\\nreplay-predictor improvement with little timing exposure: it changes the\\nreplay write-enable policy, not the current-cycle fetch-ready, branch/JAL\\ntarget, ALU, memory, or retirement paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Gate instruction replay-table fills so only instructions that are eligible to\\nenter the frontend stream refresh the replay predictor. A live imem word\\nshould update the replay entry when `io_imemReady` is high and the IF payload\\nis not being killed by reset, flush, or EX redirect. Backend stalls that hold\\nthe current PC, such as load-use, divide-long, or MEM wait, should still be\\nallowed to refresh the held-PC replay entry because that instruction remains\\nthe next architectural fetch once the stall clears.\\n\\nOn redirect or flush cycles, suppress both the direct replay-table write and\\nany fill-bypass path that would seed the registered lookahead candidate from\\nthe killed live word. The next candidate should instead come from the\\nexisting table lookup for the selected redirect target or held PC. Live imem\\ndata still has priority whenever it is not killed, replay hits still use the\\nregistered candidate during external imem stalls, replay misses still hold\\nthe PC and inject the existing IF bubble, and the static backward-branch plus\\ndirect-JAL predictor policy remains unchanged.\\n\\nThis preserves correctness because replay entries are only a performance\\npredictor for immutable instruction words; suppressing a killed fill can only\\nturn a future replay hit into a miss. RVFI instruction reporting,\\nresolved-next-PC recovery, strict channel-0 `rvfi_order`, tied-off channel 1,\\npending-store-slot behavior, divider interlock, decoder legality rules, and\\ntop-level IO remain unchanged. The expected benefit is a modest CoreMark\\ncycle reduction from less direct-mapped replay pollution, with minimal Fmax\\nrisk because the added gating is on replay-table write/update enables rather\\nthan the hot current-cycle prediction datapath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an explicit `replay_fill_enable` derived from live imem readiness\\nand the existing IF kill conditions. Use it for replay-table writes and\\nfor the same-index fill-bypass candidate path so reset, flush, and EX\\nredirect cycles cannot seed replay state from a killed wrong-path word.\\nKeep fill enabled during ordinary backend stalls that hold the current\\nPC. Preserve the 256-entry compressed-tag replay table, resetless payload\\narrays, registered lookahead candidate with current-PC guard, live-imem\\npriority, replay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, and the existing static\\nbackward-branch/direct-JAL prediction policy.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify only the IF/ID replay comments to document that replay entries\\nare refreshed from live imem words that are not killed by reset, flush,\\nor redirect, while held-PC backend stalls may still refresh the replay\\nentry. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an explicit `replay_fill_enable` derived from live imem readiness\\nand the existing IF kill conditions. Use it for replay-table writes and\\nfor the same-index fill-bypass candidate path so reset, flush, and EX\\nredirect cycles cannot seed replay state from a killed wrong-path word.\\nKeep fill enabled during ordinary backend stalls that hold the current\\nPC. Preserve the 256-entry compressed-tag replay table, resetless payload\\narrays, registered lookahead candidate with current-PC guard, live-imem\\npriority, replay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, and the existing static\\nbackward-branch/direct-JAL prediction policy.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify only the IF/ID replay comments to document that replay entries\\nare refreshed from live imem words that are not killed by reset, flush,\\nor redirect, while held-PC backend stalls may still refresh the replay\\nentry. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state, decoder legality\\ndefaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 337.32, \"delta_pct\": -36.28, \"vs_baseline\": 12.05, \"fmax_mhz\": 116.69, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5890, \"ff\": 2223, \"seeds\": [116.69, 123.72, 113.13], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- Added `replay_fill_enable` in `rtl/if_stage.sv`, derived as `imem_ready && !fetch_kill`.\\n- Used `replay_fill_enable` for replay-table writes, so live imem words killed by reset, IF flush, or EX redirect no longer refresh replay state.\\n- Used `replay_fill_enable` for the same-index fill-bypass path, so killed live words cannot seed the registered lookahead replay candidate.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document killed-fill suppression and held-PC backend-stall refresh behavior.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No structural changes were needed. The existing replay lookup target selection already chooses the redirect target on redirect and the held PC during stalls.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known RTL concerns. Backend stalls that hold the PC still allow live imem fills because they do not assert the IF kill conditions.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` from the worktree was blocked before checks by sandbox permissions because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared tree outside the writable roots and the script stages files under that tree.\\n- Re-ran the same `run_all.sh` from a temporary writable `/tmp/auto_arch_formal_r18s2` staging tree with this worktree's `rtl/` linked in: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T07:49:32.640408\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 18}\n56:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-049-r19s0\", \"title\": \"Bitfield RV32M Control Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider\\nalso reclassifies signed versus unsigned and quotient versus remainder with\\nopcode equality checks even though those are now just low funct3 bits inside\\nthe divide group.\\n\\nThese compares are small, but they sit in always-present EX control and\\nlong-operation stall logic that feeds the frontend hold network. Recent\\nexperiments show broad datapath, reset, and replay changes often lose more\\nplacement quality than they recover, so a narrow decode cleanup in the\\nalready-proven ALU-op encoding is the most attractive micro_opt follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from making the ALU opcode encoding expose a\\nhot RV32I path and a cold RV32M path. That change improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, which suggests the next micro-opt should keep\\nexploiting the same encoding rather than adding another replay table,\\npredictor, queue, reset rewrite, or arithmetic unit.\\n\\nSome downstream control logic still decodes RV32M operations with explicit\\nequality trees. In particular, EX detects divide/remainder instructions with\\nfour full `alu_op == ALU_*` compares even though the current encoding makes\\nDIV/DIVU/REM/REMU exactly the `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` class. The divider\\nalso reclassifies signed versus unsigned and quotient versus remainder with\\nopcode equality checks even though those are now just low funct3 bits inside\\nthe divide group.\\n\\nThese compares are small, but they sit in always-present EX control and\\nlong-operation stall logic that feeds the frontend hold network. Recent\\nexperiments show broad datapath, reset, and replay changes often lose more\\nplacement quality than they recover, so a narrow decode cleanup in the\\nalready-proven ALU-op encoding is the most attractive micro_opt follow-up.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace redundant RV32M opcode equality decoders with bitfield tests derived\\nfrom the current hot/cold ALU encoding. In EX, compute divide-class detection\\nas `alu_op[4] && alu_op[2]` instead of OR-ing four equality comparisons for\\nDIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU. This remains exact because MUL/MULH/MULHSU/MULHU\\nare encoded as `5'b10000` through `5'b10011`, while DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU are\\nencoded as `5'b10100` through `5'b10111`.\\n\\nIn the real divider path, derive `want_rem` from `op[1]` and signedness from\\n`!op[0]` within the divide-class group, while keeping the existing ALTOPS\\ncase labels for formal substitutions. The divider still launches only from\\nEX's divide-class predicate and still preserves all RV32M corner cases:\\ndivide-by-zero, signed INT_MIN/-1 overflow, REM-by-zero, and signed\\nremainder sign correction.\\n\\nThis does not alter any arithmetic result, instruction latency, forwarding\\npath, branch predictor, memory behavior, pending-store slot, replay table,\\ntop-level IO, RVFI field, channel-0 retirement order, or channel-1 tie-off.\\nCoreMark cycle count should be unchanged. The expected gain is a small median\\nFmax improvement from replacing several full opcode comparators in EX and\\ndivider control with direct low-bit tests that match the current ALU encoding.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four-way DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU equality OR used for\\n`is_div_op` with the exact bitfield predicate\\n`in.ctrl.alu_op[4] && in.ctrl.alu_op[2]`. Preserve the existing\\n`in.valid`, illegal-instruction, stall, divider launch/completion,\\nEX/MEM bubble, forwarding, redirect, and RVFI metadata behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"In the real RV32M divider implementation, derive divide/remainder\\nclassification from the encoded funct3 low bits: `op[1]` selects\\nREM/REMU versus DIV/DIVU, and `!op[0]` selects signed DIV/REM versus\\nunsigned DIVU/REMU. Keep all existing special-case arithmetic semantics\\nand keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas keyed by the named ALU\\nconstants so formal behavior remains unchanged.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the four-way DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU equality OR used for\\n`is_div_op` with the exact bitfield predicate\\n`in.ctrl.alu_op[4] && in.ctrl.alu_op[2]`. Preserve the existing\\n`in.valid`, illegal-instruction, stall, divider launch/completion,\\nEX/MEM bubble, forwarding, redirect, and RVFI metadata behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"In the real RV32M divider implementation, derive divide/remainder\\nclassification from the encoded funct3 low bits: `op[1]` selects\\nREM/REMU versus DIV/DIVU, and `!op[0]` selects signed DIV/REM versus\\nunsigned DIVU/REMU. Keep all existing special-case arithmetic semantics\\nand keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas keyed by the named ALU\\nconstants so formal behavior remains unchanged.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 383.8, \"delta_pct\": -27.5, \"vs_baseline\": 27.49, \"fmax_mhz\": 132.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5868, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [153.09, 125.3, 132.77], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv`: replaced the four named DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU equality compares for `is_div_op` with the encoded divide-class predicate `in.ctrl.alu_op[4] && in.ctrl.alu_op[2]`.\\n- `rtl/div_unit.sv`: in the real divider path, replaced named-op equality classification with `op[1]` for quotient-vs-remainder and `!op[0]` for signed-vs-unsigned.\\n- Kept the `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` case labels keyed by the named `ALU_*` constants.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Added a local Verilator `UNUSEDSIGNAL` guard on the divider `op` input. In the real divider build, `op[4:2]` are now intentionally consumed by EX-stage launch decode, while the divider result control only needs `op[1:0]`. This keeps the required lint command warning-clean without adding decode logic back into the real divider path.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n- None known. The change assumes the existing decoder continues to be the only source of launched divider ops, matching the current `ALU_*` encoding.\\n\\n## Verification\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: the direct in-place run was blocked by sandbox permissions while deleting staged files under `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher`.\\n- Equivalent local formal run from a `/tmp` mirror excluding the protected `riscv-formal/cores` staging tree: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T08:11:46.823672\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 19}\n57:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-050-r19s1\", \"title\": \"Backend-Stall Shadow I-Fetch Prefetcher\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core gets its largest cycle-count win from the registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table: hot instructions can be supplied during\\nrandom imem stalls without a same-cycle replay lookup on the IF critical\\npath. The remaining structural stalls are now mostly backend stalls from\\ndmem waits and long operations. During those cycles the frontend PC is held,\\ndecode is not accepting a new instruction, and the independent imem port is\\nusually wasted refreshing the same held PC or sitting idle.\\n\\nPrior fetch queues tried to decouple fetch and decode directly, but they put\\nqueue control on the normal zero-wait frontend path and lost Fmax. A more\\ntargeted structural angle is to use backend-stall cycles only as replay-table\\nwarmup time. The architectural fetch/decode stream remains held exactly as it\\nis today, while a shadow prefetch PC walks ahead through the existing static\\nbranch/direct-JAL prediction policy and fills the replay table with future\\ninstruction words. When the backend releases, later external imem stalls are\\nmore likely to hit in the already-proven registered replay structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a shadow instruction prefetch mode inside IF that activates only while a\\nbackend stall is holding the architectural frontend and the current IF/ID\\ninstruction is already safely held or replayable. On entry, capture the\\ncurrent architectural IF/ID metadata into a tiny hold register so the hazard\\nunit and decode-facing output continue to see the same instruction while\\nthe external imem address is repurposed for prefetch. The architectural PC\\nand pipeline-visible IF/ID payload remain frozen until the backend accepts\\nthem; only the replay table is updated by shadow fetches.\\n\\nWhile shadow prefetch is active, drive `io_imemAddr` from a `prefetch_pc`\\nrather than the architectural PC. On each `io_imemReady` prefetch response,\\nfill or refresh the existing 256-entry compressed-tag replay entry for\\n`prefetch_pc`, then advance `prefetch_pc` using the same zero-state static\\nnext-PC rule already used for normal fetch: backward conditional branches\\npredict taken, direct aligned JAL predicts its target, and JALR remains\\nunpredicted. Redirects, reset, and frontend flushes cancel shadow prefetch\\nimmediately and resume normal fetch at the resolved architectural target.\\n\\nThis is not a fetch queue and it does not let prefetched instructions enter\\ndecode early. It only converts cycles where the backend cannot accept an\\ninstruction into useful instruction replay-table fills. Live architectural\\nfetches still have priority when the backend is ready, replay hits still use\\nthe registered lookahead candidate, replay misses still hold the PC and\\ninject the existing IF bubble, and EX still repairs branch/JAL prediction\\nmistakes through resolved-next-PC mismatch recovery. The top-level IO, RVFI\\nNRET=2 port set, channel-0-only retirement convention, strict `rvfi_order`\\nsequence, pending-store slot, divider interlock, memory ordering, and decoder\\nlegality rules remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a backend-stall-only shadow prefetch mode. Capture the current\\narchitectural IF/ID metadata into a hold register before repurposing\\n`io_imemAddr`, keep the decode-facing output stable from that hold\\nregister while the backend is stalled, and use a separate `prefetch_pc`\\nto fill the existing compressed-tag replay table. Advance the shadow PC\\nwith the existing static branch/direct-JAL prediction rule, cancel it on\\nreset, flush, or EX redirect, and preserve live-imem priority plus the\\nregistered replay-hit `fetch_ready` contract for architectural fetches.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire a backend-prefetch-eligible signal into IF, derived from precise\\nbackend holds such as MEM wait and EX long-operation stalls rather than\\nraw imem misses. Keep the core module IO, RVFI channel layout, pending\\nstore-slot arbitration, hot/cold ALU wiring, divider interlock, and\\nchannel-1 tie-off unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, expose or document the distinction between frontend imem\\nunavailability and backend holds so IF only enters shadow prefetch when\\ndecode is blocked by the backend and the architectural IF/ID instruction\\nmust remain stable. Preserve existing load-use, redirect, MEM wait,\\nEX-long-operation, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify IF/ID comments to document that during backend-stall shadow\\nprefetch the pipeline-visible IF/ID metadata remains held while the imem\\nport may be used only to warm the replay table. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, architectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI\\nfields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a backend-stall-only shadow prefetch mode. Capture the current\\narchitectural IF/ID metadata into a hold register before repurposing\\n`io_imemAddr`, keep the decode-facing output stable from that hold\\nregister while the backend is stalled, and use a separate `prefetch_pc`\\nto fill the existing compressed-tag replay table. Advance the shadow PC\\nwith the existing static branch/direct-JAL prediction rule, cancel it on\\nreset, flush, or EX redirect, and preserve live-imem priority plus the\\nregistered replay-hit `fetch_ready` contract for architectural fetches.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire a backend-prefetch-eligible signal into IF, derived from precise\\nbackend holds such as MEM wait and EX long-operation stalls rather than\\nraw imem misses. Keep the core module IO, RVFI channel layout, pending\\nstore-slot arbitration, hot/cold ALU wiring, divider interlock, and\\nchannel-1 tie-off unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, expose or document the distinction between frontend imem\\nunavailability and backend holds so IF only enters shadow prefetch when\\ndecode is blocked by the backend and the architectural IF/ID instruction\\nmust remain stable. Preserve existing load-use, redirect, MEM wait,\\nEX-long-operation, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify IF/ID comments to document that during backend-stall shadow\\nprefetch the pipeline-visible IF/ID metadata remains held while the imem\\nport may be used only to warm the replay table. Do not change bundle\\nwidths, architectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI\\nfields, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 19}\n58:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-051-r19s2\", \"title\": \"XOR-Indexed I-Fetch Replay Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tables lost hit rate, and adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor state is the compressed, resetless registered\\nlookahead instruction replay table. It preserves the large random-iStall\\ncycle reduction of the 256-entry replay predictor while keeping the\\ncurrent-cycle IF path free of a combinational table read. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the boundary clearly: extra branch-direction state,\\nreturn targets, replay victim entries, replay-line widening, and cached\\nnext-PC metadata either saved too few cycles or lost more Fmax than they\\nrecovered.\\n\\nThe remaining replay opportunity is likely direct-mapped conflict behavior.\\nThe table is indexed by `pc[9:2]`, so hot instructions separated by 1 KiB\\nalias even though the CoreMark timed region repeatedly visits several loop\\nbodies and helper routines. Smaller tables lost hit rate, and adding ways or\\nvictim entries added too much logic. A hashed direct-map index targets the\\nsame conflict misses while keeping the table size, registered lookahead\\ntiming model, live-imem-first policy, and compressed tag storage intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the replay table's direct index `pc[9:2]` with a simple XOR-folded\\nindex, for example `pc[9:2] ^ pc[17:10]`, for both live fills and registered\\nlookahead lookups. Keep the existing 256-entry table, high-PC tag compare,\\nresetless tag/instruction payload arrays, registered candidate PC guard,\\nsame-entry fill bypass, and live `io_imemData` priority.\\n\\nFor a fixed stored high tag `pc[31:10]`, the XOR fold is still one-to-one\\nwith the low word index, so the existing compressed tag plus registered\\ncurrent-PC guard remains correctness-preserving. A hash collision can only\\nevict a useful replay word and create a future miss; it cannot inject an\\ninstruction for the wrong PC. Replay misses still hold the PC and inject the\\nexisting IF bubble, while replay hits continue to feed the cached instruction\\ninto the current static backward-branch and direct-JAL prediction path.\\n\\nThis is intended to recover iStall cycles lost to 1 KiB direct-map conflicts\\nwithout adding another table, way, victim search, branch update path, RAS,\\npipeline stage, or load-data bypass. The top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 channel\\nlayout, strict channel-0 order sequence, tied-off channel 1, pending-store\\nslot, divider interlock, decoder legality rules, and branch/JAL resolved\\nnext-PC recovery remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the registered lookahead replay predictor index from the raw\\ndirect-mapped `pc[9:2]` bits to a small XOR-folded index such as\\n`pc[9:2] ^ pc[17:10]` for live fills, registered lookahead reads, and\\nsame-entry fill-bypass detection. Preserve the 256-entry table size,\\ncompressed `pc[31:10]` tags, resetless replay payload arrays, candidate\\nPC equality guard, live-imem priority, replay-hit `fetch_ready`\\nsemantics, redirect priority, and the existing static backward-branch\\nplus direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only the IF/ID replay comments to document that replay entries\\nuse an XOR-folded direct-map index plus the existing high-PC tag and\\nregistered current-PC guard. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the registered lookahead replay predictor index from the raw\\ndirect-mapped `pc[9:2]` bits to a small XOR-folded index such as\\n`pc[9:2] ^ pc[17:10]` for live fills, registered lookahead reads, and\\nsame-entry fill-bypass detection. Preserve the 256-entry table size,\\ncompressed `pc[31:10]` tags, resetless replay payload arrays, candidate\\nPC equality guard, live-imem priority, replay-hit `fetch_ready`\\nsemantics, redirect priority, and the existing static backward-branch\\nplus direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only the IF/ID replay comments to document that replay entries\\nuse an XOR-folded direct-map index plus the existing high-PC tag and\\nregistered current-PC guard. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 522.1, \"delta_pct\": -1.37, \"vs_baseline\": 73.43, \"fmax_mhz\": 180.64, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459875, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5912, \"ff\": 2228, \"seeds\": [178.44, 191.24, 180.64], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Changed the IF replay predictor direct-map index from raw `pc[9:2]` to\\n  `pc[9:2] ^ pc[17:10]` in `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- Applied the same folded index to live fills, registered lookahead reads,\\n  and same-entry fill bypass detection.\\n- Kept the 256-entry table, compressed `pc[31:10]` tag compare, resetless\\n  tag/instruction payload arrays, registered candidate PC guard, live-imem\\n  priority, replay-hit `fetch_ready` behavior, redirect priority, and\\n  static backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction behavior unchanged.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document the\\n  XOR-folded index plus high-PC tag and registered current-PC guard.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No architectural or structural deviations from the hypothesis.\\n- No top-level IO, RVFI, decoder, memory, branch recovery, or pipeline bundle\\n  changes were made.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The hash may improve 1 KiB conflict behavior, but it can also introduce\\n  different conflict pairs. Correctness is preserved by the high-PC tag and\\n  registered full-PC guard; collisions only affect replay hit rate.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: the repository-local invocation was blocked by\\n  sandbox permissions before any checks ran, while trying to remove staged\\n  files under the symlinked `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher`\\n  directory.\\n- Equivalent local formal run from a writable `/tmp` copy of the same runner,\\n  checks config, wrapper, and current `rtl/*.sv` completed with\\n  `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T08:08:19.813594\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 19}\n59:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-052-r20s0\", \"title\": \"Funct3-Aligned Hot ALU Encoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should shrink\\ndecode and mux logic on the single-cycle EX result path while preserving the\\nsuccessful `op[4]` M-extension discriminator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result came from an RTL-only hot/cold ALU opcode split:\\nRV32M operations were moved behind `alu_op[4]`, letting the common RV32I\\nresult path avoid the cold M-extension mux. That improved Fmax without\\nchanging CoreMark cycles, so the most promising micro_opt follow-up is to\\nkeep refining the same hot EX datapath rather than adding new replay state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or arithmetic units.\\n\\nThe remaining RV32I/simple ALU encoding is still arbitrary and dense:\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI are assigned sequential localparams, so the\\ndecoder emits constants unrelated to the instruction funct3 bits and the ALU\\nstill needs an 11-way simple-result case. RISC-V already groups the hot\\nOP/OP-IMM operations by funct3, with only SUB/SRA needing a modifier bit.\\nRe-encoding the simple ALU controls around that structure should shrink\\ndecode and mux logic on the single-cycle EX result path while preserving the\\nsuccessful `op[4]` M-extension discriminator.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode the RV32I/simple ALU operations so `alu_op[2:0]` matches the\\nRISC-V funct3 value for ADD/SUB, SLL, SLT, SLTU, XOR, SRL/SRA, OR, and AND,\\nwhile `alu_op[3]` distinguishes the alternate ADD/SUB and SRL/SRA variants.\\nKeep `alu_op[4]=0` for every simple operation and keep all RV32M constants\\nat the current `alu_op[4]=1` contiguous encoding that produced the best\\nresult. Assign LUI to one otherwise-unused simple encoding, such as\\n`op[3]=1` with low bits `111`, and keep AUIPC as ADD with operand A = PC as\\ntoday.\\n\\nRefactor the hot simple ALU mux to decode mostly by `op[2:0]`: one case arm\\neach for ADD/SUB, SLL, SLT, SLTU, XOR, SRL/SRA, OR, and AND/LUI, with\\n`op[3]` only used inside the ADD/SUB, SRL/SRA, and LUI-vs-AND arms. The cold\\nM mux and final `op[4] ? m_out : simple_out` selection remain unchanged.\\n\\nDecoder behavior stays architecturally identical: legal OP and OP-IMM\\ninstructions emit the new funct3-aligned constants, reserved encodings still\\nleave `is_illegal=1`, and every RVFI-visible arithmetic result is unchanged.\\nNo instruction latency, divider protocol, forwarding path, branch predictor,\\nmemory behavior, replay table, top-level IO, RVFI field, channel-0\\nretirement ordering, or channel-1 tie-off changes. CoreMark cycles should be\\nidentical; the expected gain is a modest median Fmax improvement from a\\nshallower hot ALU control/mux path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign the RV32I/simple ALU localparams so the low three bits match\\ninstruction funct3 where possible, with bit 3 as the SUB/SRA/simple\\nspecial modifier. Preserve the current `alu_op[4]=1` RV32M constants\\nand do not change any bundle widths, RVFI fields, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the new funct3-aligned ALU constants for OP and OP-IMM decode.\\nADD/ADDI and the logical/compare/shift operations should map directly\\nfrom funct3, while SUB and SRA/SRAI set the modifier bit. LUI keeps its\\ndedicated simple encoding, AUIPC remains the ADD path with `is_auipc`,\\nand all reserved opcode/funct combinations must retain the existing\\nillegal-default behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewrite the `simple_out` mux around the new encoding: case primarily on\\n`op[2:0]`, use `op[3]` only for ADD-vs-SUB, SRL-vs-SRA, and\\nAND-vs-LUI, and keep the cold M-extension mux plus ALTOPS multiply\\nsubstitutions unchanged. The final output remains selected by\\n`op[4] ? m_out : simple_out`.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign the RV32I/simple ALU localparams so the low three bits match\\ninstruction funct3 where possible, with bit 3 as the SUB/SRA/simple\\nspecial modifier. Preserve the current `alu_op[4]=1` RV32M constants\\nand do not change any bundle widths, RVFI fields, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit the new funct3-aligned ALU constants for OP and OP-IMM decode.\\nADD/ADDI and the logical/compare/shift operations should map directly\\nfrom funct3, while SUB and SRA/SRAI set the modifier bit. LUI keeps its\\ndedicated simple encoding, AUIPC remains the ADD path with `is_auipc`,\\nand all reserved opcode/funct combinations must retain the existing\\nillegal-default behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Rewrite the `simple_out` mux around the new encoding: case primarily on\\n`op[2:0]`, use `op[3]` only for ADD-vs-SUB, SRL-vs-SRA, and\\nAND-vs-LUI, and keep the cold M-extension mux plus ALTOPS multiply\\nsubstitutions unchanged. The final output remains selected by\\n`op[4] ? m_out : simple_out`.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 334.55, \"delta_pct\": -36.8, \"vs_baseline\": 11.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 115.73, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3459314, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5896, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [115.73, 122.94, 107.75], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes made\\n\\n- Re-encoded the RV32I/simple ALU constants in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` so\\n  `alu_op[2:0]` matches the RISC-V OP/OP-IMM `funct3` value for ADD/SUB,\\n  SLL, SLT, SLTU, XOR, SRL/SRA, OR, and AND.\\n- Assigned `alu_op[3]` as the simple-operation modifier for SUB and SRA, and\\n  placed LUI at the unused simple encoding