: \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r13s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use detection must inspect the replay-selected decode instruction rather than the live IF/ID opcode, or replay can make the qualifier disagree with the consumed rs2 and allow stale register data into execution.\"}\n39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Split registered fast and multiply result lanes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split execute into parallel one-cycle fast-ALU and mapper-native multiply\\nresult lanes, and register both lanes independently in EX/MEM together with\\none is_mul bit. The fast lane contains only RV32I arithmetic, address, and\\nlogical results, so its register input no longer passes through a mux fed by\\nthe 64-bit product or signed-high correction network. The multiply lane keeps\\nthe currently successful single unsigned 32x32 product and exact high-half\\ncorrections, with no partial-product decomposition and no new pipeline stall.\\n\\nSelect the already-registered lane at the MEM boundary for forwarding,\\nwriteback, and RVFI, while memory operations use the fast lane directly so\\nno new mux is placed on the dmem address path. This trades 32 result flops and\\na shallow post-register selector for removal of the multiplier from the\\nordinary EX result cone, preserving CoreMark cycles and all existing bus,\\nreplay, trap, and nret=1 retirement behavior while targeting higher routed\\nFmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 427.64, \"delta_pct\": -7.81, \"vs_baseline\": 51.21, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.59, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10307, \"ff\": 1993, \"seeds\": [190.62, 177.02, 181.59], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:48:16.774552Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r13s1 (regression, -7.81%): Independently registering fast and multiply result lanes cannot raise Fmax while the single-cycle 32x32 product still sets the ID/EX-to-EX/MEM critical path, and the extra 32-bit lane plus post-register forwarding/writeback selectors only add FPGA placement and routing pressure.\"}\n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-006-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Protocol-qualified replay fill capture\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The exact-target backward-branch instruction replay is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but its pending-fill completion still\\nperforms a full 32-bit comparison between the accepted fetch PC and the saved\\nredirect target. The redirect and flush protocol already guarantees that no\\nyounger instruction can be accepted before that target fetch, so this equality\\nconsumes LUT and routing resources without distinguishing a legal execution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The exact-target backward-branch instruction replay is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but its pending-fill completion still\\nperforms a full 32-bit comparison between the accepted fetch PC and the saved\\nredirect target. The redirect and flush protocol already guarantees that no\\nyounger instruction can be accepted before that target fetch, so this equality\\nconsumes LUT and routing resources without distinguishing a legal execution.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Treat the first ID-accepted instruction after an eligible confirmed replay miss\\nas the pending target instruction, and remove the fetch_pc equality from fill\\ncompletion. Keep the saved pending target unchanged for the eventual resident\\nexact-target tag; only the completion qualification is simplified. Redirect\\npriority, ID/EX flushing, and pipeline holds ensure that instruction-memory or\\ndata-memory stalls merely delay this first acceptance and cannot substitute a\\ndifferent PC.\\n\\nThis deletes a 32-bit comparator from the replay update network without adding\\nstate, replacement policy, prediction metadata, address selection, or logic on\\nio_imemAddr. Replay hits remain exact-target and EX-confirmed, so architectural\\ncontrol flow, traps, RVFI ordering, and nret=1 behavior are unchanged. The\\nreduced local routing should modestly improve median Fmax with identical\\nCoreMark cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Complete a pending replay fill on the first qualified fetch_accept event, remove the redundant fetch_pc-to-pending-target equality and fetch_pc input, and preserve exact-target hit matching plus all stall-safe pending state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused fetch_pc connection to the replay buffer while retaining the existing fetch_accept qualification, redirect priority, pipeline flushes, and nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks proving that redirect flushes and instruction/data stalls delay but cannot misassociate the first accepted target fetch, including reset, cold fill, replacement fill, and exact-target replay cases.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Complete a pending replay fill on the first qualified fetch_accept event, remove the redundant fetch_pc-to-pending-target equality and fetch_pc input, and preserve exact-target hit matching plus all stall-safe pending state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused fetch_pc connection to the replay buffer while retaining the existing fetch_accept qualification, redirect priority, pipeline flushes, and nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks proving that redirect flushes and instruction/data stalls delay but cannot misassociate the first accepted target fetch, including reset, cold fill, replacement fill, and exact-target replay cases.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 467.91, \"delta_pct\": 0.87, \"vs_baseline\": 65.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 198.69, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10292, \"ff\": 1969, \"seeds\": [196.7, 200.12, 198.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:45:21.187978Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-006-r13s2 (improvement, +0.87%): When redirect flushing guarantees the first fetch_accept after a replay miss is the saved target, using that protocol invariant instead of rechecking fetch_pc removes a redundant 32-bit fill comparator and modestly improves FPGA timing without weakening the resident exact-target tag.\"}\n41:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Replay-aligned source-qualified load-use hazard\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use detector treats both register-shaped instruction fields as consumed\\nsources for every opcode. Accidental matches therefore insert bubbles after loads\\neven when the following instruction does not read rs1 or rs2. The previous rs2-only\\nqualification attempt targeted this useful CPI opportunity but became incorrect\\nwhen branch replay replaced the live IF instruction without replacing the opcode\\nused by hazard detection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use detector treats both register-shaped instruction fields as consumed\\nsources for every opcode. Accidental matches therefore insert bubbles after loads\\neven when the following instruction does not read rs1 or rs2. The previous rs2-only\\nqualification attempt targeted this useful CPI opportunity but became incorrect\\nwhen branch replay replaced the live IF instruction without replacing the opcode\\nused by hazard detection.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Derive the hazard register tags and compact uses-rs1/uses-rs2 qualifiers from the\\nexact instruction selected by ID after replay arbitration, then require a load\\ndestination match only for sources that instruction actually consumes. OP, STORE,\\nand BRANCH consume rs2; OP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR consume rs1.\\nReplay and ordinary fetch will consequently use one coherent instruction identity.\\n\\nThis should eliminate false load-use bubbles without forwarding memory data into\\nEX, changing pipeline latency, or adding state. Keeping the opcode classification\\nbeside the existing ID selection and sending only two qualifier bits plus the\\nalready-selected register tags to the hazard unit minimizes timing and routing\\nrisk while preserving every true dependency, bus stall, trap, RVFI, and nret=1\\nbehavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose hazard rs1/rs2 tags and compact source-use qualifiers derived from the same replay-selected instruction that decode and the register file consume.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the replay-aligned ID hazard metadata into the load-use detector instead of using raw live IF instruction fields, preserving all existing pipeline handshakes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate each load destination comparison with its selected instruction source-use bit while retaining true load dependencies and the existing redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ordinary-fetch and replay cases proving true rs1/rs2 dependencies bubble and non-source field matches do not, including OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and SYSTEM encodings.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose hazard rs1/rs2 tags and compact source-use qualifiers derived from the same replay-selected instruction that decode and the register file consume.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the replay-aligned ID hazard metadata into the load-use detector instead of using raw live IF instruction fields, preserving all existing pipeline handshakes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate each load destination comparison with its selected instruction source-use bit while retaining true load dependencies and the existing redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed ordinary-fetch and replay cases proving true rs1/rs2 dependencies bubble and non-source field matches do not, including OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and SYSTEM encodings.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r14s0 (broken, n/a): Replay-aligned load-use detection is still unsafe when replay.valid has priority over flush/stall in the ID/EX register, because a detected dependency cannot inject its required bubble; replay capture must obey the same interlock as ordinary decode.\"}\n42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Uniform two-stage execute pipeline\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Routed timing evidence and the last structural experiment both identify the\\nsingle-cycle ID/EX-to-EX/MEM multiply path as the remaining Fmax limiter.\\nPrior multiplier sidecars tried to shorten it with operation-specific busy\\nstate and global interlocks, but those controls burdened ordinary cycles and\\nthe completion mux still sat on the long arithmetic path. A pipeline stage\\nused uniformly by every instruction can provide a true timing cut without\\ndispatch, busy, or completion arbitration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Routed timing evidence and the last structural experiment both identify the\\nsingle-cycle ID/EX-to-EX/MEM multiply path as the remaining Fmax limiter.\\nPrior multiplier sidecars tried to shorten it with operation-specific busy\\nstate and global interlocks, but those controls burdened ordinary cycles and\\nthe completion mux still sat on the long arithmetic path. A pipeline stage\\nused uniformly by every instruction can provide a true timing cut without\\ndispatch, busy, or completion arbitration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert execute into two fixed pipeline stages. EX1 performs forwarding,\\noperand selection, the fast ALU, and the existing shared unsigned multiply,\\nthen registers both the selected architectural result and all instruction\\nmetadata in a new EX1/EX2 payload. EX2 performs only the shallow downstream\\nrouting into the existing memory-stage register. Resolve branches in EX1 so\\nredirect and replay latency do not increase, and extend forwarding to use\\nthe registered EX1/EX2 result as the youngest producer.\\n\\nEvery instruction traverses the new stage, so there is no M-specific state,\\nglobal multi-cycle interlock, or completion mux. The pipeline retains a\\nthroughput of one instruction per cycle; only load-use dependencies require\\nan additional bubble because a load reaches memory one cycle later. Holding\\nEX1/EX2 together with the existing stages under dmem backpressure preserves\\nprecise in-order retirement. A hard register boundary after the mapper-native\\nproduct should materially raise Fmax, and that gain should outweigh the\\nmodest CoreMark cost of the extra load-use delay.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX1/EX2 pipeline payload carrying the registered result, store operand, destination, control, PC, instruction, and RVFI source metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split execute into EX1 arithmetic and branch resolution followed by a shallow EX2 transfer, with a true register cut after the shared multiplier and no operation-specific handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the added execute stage, align redirect and replay signals with EX1, propagate dmem holds through the new register, and preserve the nret=1 retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the EX1/EX2 registered result as the youngest forwarding source and retain correct priority across the deeper pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Track loads through both execute stages and insert the required extra load-use bubble while preserving redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency, branch, replay, load-use, random bus-stall, and RVFI ordering checks for the deeper fixed-throughput execute pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX1/EX2 pipeline payload carrying the registered result, store operand, destination, control, PC, instruction, and RVFI source metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split execute into EX1 arithmetic and branch resolution followed by a shallow EX2 transfer, with a true register cut after the shared multiplier and no operation-specific handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the added execute stage, align redirect and replay signals with EX1, propagate dmem holds through the new register, and preserve the nret=1 retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the EX1/EX2 registered result as the youngest forwarding source and retain correct priority across the deeper pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Track loads through both execute stages and insert the required extra load-use bubble while preserving redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency, branch, replay, load-use, random bus-stall, and RVFI ordering checks for the deeper fixed-throughput execute pipeline.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 176.51, \"delta_pct\": -62.28, \"vs_baseline\": -37.59, \"fmax_mhz\": 79.65, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4512585, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10385, \"ff\": 2183, \"seeds\": [80.18, 76.83, 79.65], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:03:47.025369Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r14s1 (regression, -62.28%): Adding EX2 after the existing product-capture boundary does not split the ID/EX-to-32x32-multiply path; it only duplicates a wide pipeline payload and expands forwarding/load-hazard routing, so a useful execute retime must register inside the arithmetic cone rather than downstream of it.\"}\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-006-r14s2\", \"title\": \"One-bit hysteretic replay-entry replacement\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The confirmed backward-branch replay entry improves this core, but every\\nexact-target miss immediately displaces the resident loop head. In nested\\nloops, an infrequent outer-loop backedge can therefore evict the frequently\\nreused inner-loop target just before execution returns to that inner loop.\\nA second candidate or victim tag is too expensive on this FPGA, but a single\\nreplacement-protection bit can distinguish a proven hot entry without adding\\nanother wide comparator or target register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The confirmed backward-branch replay entry improves this core, but every\\nexact-target miss immediately displaces the resident loop head. In nested\\nloops, an infrequent outer-loop backedge can therefore evict the frequently\\nreused inner-loop target just before execution returns to that inner loop.\\nA second candidate or victim tag is too expensive on this FPGA, but a single\\nreplacement-protection bit can distinguish a proven hot entry without adding\\nanother wide comparator or target register.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one-bit replacement hysteresis to the existing exact-target replay entry.\\nSet the protection bit whenever the resident entry hits. On the first miss\\nagainst a protected entry, retain the entry, clear protection, and do not\\nstart a fill; a subsequent hit restores protection, while a subsequent miss\\nmay replace the entry through the existing pending-fill protocol. Invalid or\\nunprotected entries continue to fill exactly as they do today.\\n\\nThis policy keeps a repeatedly useful inner-loop instruction resident across\\na single intervening outer-loop branch, yet eventually admits a new loop after\\nworkload phase changes. It adds only one flop and local control, keeps the\\nexact 32-bit resident-target match, and adds no lookup, target mux, metadata,\\nor combinational logic to io_imemAddr. Replay remains EX-confirmed, so wrong\\ninstructions are never injected and architectural control flow, stalls,\\ntraps, RVFI ordering, and the nret=1 interface remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit hit-protection state and hysteretic miss replacement: retain a proven resident entry across one miss, re-protect it on a hit, and otherwise use the existing stall-safe pending-fill protocol unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed nested-loop and phase-change checks covering hit protection, first-miss retention, hit re-protection, second-miss replacement, cold fill, and behavior across instruction and data stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit hit-protection state and hysteretic miss replacement: retain a proven resident entry across one miss, re-protect it on a hit, and otherwise use the existing stall-safe pending-fill protocol unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed nested-loop and phase-change checks covering hit protection, first-miss retention, hit re-protection, second-miss replacement, cold fill, and behavior across instruction and data stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.79, \"delta_pct\": -25.03, \"vs_baseline\": 24.03, \"fmax_mhz\": 148.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4236397, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9979, \"ff\": 1963, \"seeds\": [148.61, 161.08, 133.49], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:00:47.969364Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-006-r14s2 (regression, -25.03%): Reprotecting a one-entry replay target on every hit can starve an alternating target because each resident re-hit rearms the one-miss shield before replacement, so hysteresis needs an admission policy that guarantees progress across recurring loop heads.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
