 1 bonetto  staff    1924 Jul 11 15:21 hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-00{1,2,3}-r11s*.yaml; do echo \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,220p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-00{1,2,3}-r11s*.yaml; do echo \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,220p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s0.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\ntitle: \"Fold replay refill target into the resident tag\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The confirmed branch replay buffer stores the same 32-bit target twice while\n  a miss is being filled: once as the resident entry target and once as a\n  separate pending-fill target. The in-order redirect prevents the old resident\n  entry from being usefully hit before the redirected target fetch is accepted,\n  so retaining both target banks adds flops, routing, and update muxing without\n  preserving exploitable parallelism.\nhypothesis: |\n  On a confirmed eligible replay miss, immediately invalidate the resident\n  instruction and write the resolved target into the existing entry-target\n  register while setting fill_pending. Match the accepted redirected fetch\n  directly against that entry target; on a match, capture the instruction and\n  revalidate the entry. This deletes fill_target_pc_q end to end while retaining\n  the exact-target hit equation, backward-only admission, accepted-fetch fill\n  discipline, replay timing, and registered-PC-only instruction address path.\n\n  Give a same-cycle newer miss priority over completion of an older pending fill,\n  so an accepted stale target can never be validated under the newly written tag.\n  Because a miss already redirects and flushes all younger work, invalidating the\n  old entry during refill cannot remove a reachable replay opportunity. Removing\n  32 target flops and their duplicate compare and update routing should improve\n  placement and median Fmax at identical CoreMark cycles and architectural state.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Remove the separate pending-fill target register, reuse the invalid resident target as the pending tag, and define miss-over-fill priority so simultaneous refill completion and replacement cannot associate an instruction with the wrong target.\"\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed checks for cold fill, miss-time invalidation, stalled accepted-fetch matching, and simultaneous old-fill completion plus newer miss replacement while preserving exact-target replay behavior.\"\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s1.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Replicated single-read register-file banks\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The reset-valid experiment established that the asynchronous register-file\n  read path is timing-sensitive, and the falling-edge-write experiment showed\n  that its write-first behavior cannot simply be removed. The current 2R1W\n  array makes every stored bit drive both independently addressed read mux\n  trees, creating high fanout and coupled placement on the decode-to-ID/EX\n  path even though FPGA flops are plentiful relative to the fitness benefit\n  available from higher routed Fmax.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the shared two-read-port register array with two physically distinct\n  1R1W copies, one dedicated to rs1 and one to rs2. Broadcast every qualified\n  architectural write and reset to both banks, but build each asynchronous\n  read from only its local bank; preserve the existing per-port x0 handling\n  and same-cycle write-first bypass exactly. Prevent synthesis from merging the\n  copies so each read mux can be placed near its operand capture logic without\n  every register bit feeding both mux networks.\n\n  This deliberately spends roughly one extra 32x32 bank to reduce storage-bit\n  fanout and placement coupling rather than adding any new selection level to\n  the already sensitive read paths. Pipeline latency, CoreMark cycles,\n  forwarding, bus handshakes, reset-visible zero state, RVFI register data, and\n  the nret=1 interface remain unchanged. If decode/register-read routing is a\n  limiting path, the Fmax gain should outweigh the extra flops and write/reset\n  fanout while avoiding all fetch, execute, and memory critical cones.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Implement two explicitly independent 32x32 single-read banks with broadcast synchronous reset and writes, local asynchronous rs1/rs2 reads, x0 suppression, and identical write-first bypass semantics; retain the copies through synthesis.\"\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: \"Add directed and randomized checks that both replicated banks remain coherent across reset, back-to-back writes, simultaneous different-address reads, x0 accesses, and same-cycle write/read collisions on either or both ports.\"\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"Fill-once backward-loop replay entry\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\n  that has improved fitness, but its replace-on-miss policy lets later taken\n  branches dislodge the loop head that earned the entry. Recent admission and\n  forward-coverage experiments show that extra policy state or broader fills\n  cost substantial Fmax and can amplify this thrashing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make the replay entry fill only while invalid and keep that first captured\n  backward-loop target instruction resident until reset. Once valid, an exact\n  target hit continues to replay normally, while every nonmatching backward\n  branch follows the existing redirect path without starting or replacing a\n  pending fill. This removes steady-state entry writes and destructive\n  replacement without adding counters, tags, muxes, or fetch-address logic.\n\n  CoreMark repeatedly executes its earliest hot initialization and benchmark\n  loops, so preserving the first eligible loop head should provide stable reuse\n  and avoid the phase-to-phase or nested-loop churn seen with replace-on-miss.\n  Cold fill remains qualified by accepted fetch, and EX remains authoritative\n  for direction, legality, alignment, redirect, stalls, and RVFI behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 2\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Permit miss-triggered pending fill only while the replay entry is invalid, then keep the captured exact-target instruction resident until reset while leaving the existing confirmed-hit path unchanged.\"\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed checks for cold accepted-fetch fill, repeated exact-target hits, immunity to later conflicting backward-branch misses, reset replacement, and unchanged behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
