{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'structural|hyp-20260711-005-r11s1|hyp-20260711-005-r12s1|hyp-20260711-002-r9s1|hyp-20260711-002-r7s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -n 160\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:16:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering the IF/ID instruction response is an elastic-buffer protocol change, not a pure retime; request-PC advance, response valid, decode consume/refill, and redirect/replay invalidation must be tracked independently or instructions can be dropped or stranded, as the liveness, PC-forwarding, and register-state checks expose.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:21:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): Direct combinational commit from EX/MEM makes post-edge RVFI observe a newly advanced load with stale io_dmemRData from the prior address, so load retirement still needs a response/commit holding boundary even if the full MEM/WB payload is removed.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:26:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r11s1 (regression, -52.87%): Splitting a mapper-optimized 32x32 product into four registered 16x16 partials does not isolate timing when their wide recombination/correction still feeds an EX/MEM completion mux and mul_dispatch enters centralized fetch/decode stalls, so a multiplier sidecar must decouple both its result and interlock paths from ordinary cycles.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with a two-entry instruction FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend ties the fetch PC directly to backend stalls. Whenever a\\ndata-memory operation freezes EX/MEM, an otherwise-ready instruction-memory\\ncycle is discarded; later random instruction-memory backpressure then creates\\nanother bubble. A shallow fetch queue can use those idle frontend cycles to\\nbuild slack without adding a cache or changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend ties the fetch PC directly to backend stalls. Whenever a\\ndata-memory operation freezes EX/MEM, an otherwise-ready instruction-memory\\ncycle is discarded; later random instruction-memory backpressure then creates\\nanother bubble. A shallow fetch queue can use those idle frontend cycles to\\nbuild slack without adding a cache or changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the tightly coupled IF path with a two-entry fall-through instruction\\nFIFO carrying PC and instruction, and give fetch its own request PC. Enqueue\\nonly when io_imemReady is asserted, dequeue only when ID can advance, and keep\\nfetching during backend and load-use stalls while space remains. On an EX\\nredirect, invalidate all queued wrong-path entries and restart the request PC\\nat the redirect target. Hazard control should stall decode on FIFO-empty rather\\nthan directly on io_imemReady. This should overlap independent iStall and\\ndStall intervals, improving CoreMark iterations/cycle with only a small bank of\\nflops and shallow control muxes, so median Fmax should remain nearly unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two-entry PC/instruction FIFO, independent fetch request PC, ready-qualified enqueue, decode-qualified dequeue, and redirect flush/restart behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady into the decoupled frontend and connect FIFO-valid/consume control between fetch, hazard control, and ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct instruction-bus backpressure handling with frontend-empty and dequeue control while allowing fetch to run during backend stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_fetch_fifo.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb checks for ready-qualified fetches, prefetch during backend stalls, FIFO-empty bubbles, in-order delivery, and complete wrong-path invalidation on redirects.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the two-entry PC/instruction FIFO, independent fetch request PC, ready-qualified enqueue, decode-qualified dequeue, and redirect flush/restart behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire io_imemReady into the decoupled frontend and connect FIFO-valid/consume control between fetch, hazard control, and ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace direct instruction-bus backpressure handling with frontend-empty and dequeue control while allowing fetch to run during backend stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_fetch_fifo.py\", \"description\": \"Add cocotb checks for ready-qualified fetches, prefetch during backend stalls, FIFO-empty bubbles, in-order delivery, and complete wrong-path invalidation on redirects.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.25, \"delta_pct\": 8.99, \"vs_baseline\": 8.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.83, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4341607, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9940, \"ff\": 1981, \"seeds\": [133.83, 137.16, 133.6], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:00:58.223364Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative shared divider with pipeline interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU hardware with one shared\\niterative radix-2 quotient/remainder unit. A divide-class instruction starts\\nthe unit from EX, remains resident in ID/EX, and interlocks PC, ID/EX, and\\nEX/MEM until the result is ready; it then advances exactly once through the\\nexisting retirement path. Signed operations use operand magnitudes plus final\\nquotient/remainder sign correction, with explicit divide-by-zero and\\nINT_MIN/-1 handling to retain exact RV32M semantics.\\n\\nOrdinary ALU and multiply instructions must bypass the divider with no new\\nstate or muxing on the fetch PC feedback path. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep\\nthe existing one-cycle algebraic stand-ins so the bounded formal checks retain\\ntheir current liveness depth; normal simulation and FPGA synthesis exercise\\nthe iterative engine. Removing inferred combinational division and sharing\\none subtract/shift datapath should reduce LUT and routing pressure enough to\\nraise median Fmax, while the added cycles affect only divide-class dynamic\\ninstructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 299.19, \"delta_pct\": -26.08, \"vs_baseline\": 5.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5688, \"ff\": 2111, \"seeds\": [140.57, 129.47, 134.39], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:25:06.711843Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (regression, -26.08%): Feeding an iterative divider's div_wait into centralized hazard control makes rare DIV/REM operations add a high-fanout PC/IF-ID/ID-EX stall path to every cycle, so multi-cycle-unit interlocks must be localized or completion-decoupled to preserve FPGA Fmax.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Fast aligned-LW MEM-to-EX bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a fast forwarding path from the current MEM-stage aligned LW data directly\\nto the following instruction's EX operand mux. Suppress the load-use bubble\\nonly for legal aligned word loads; retain the existing one-cycle interlock for\\nbyte and halfword loads so their shift/sign-extension network is not placed in\\nthe EX critical path. The existing dmem-stall freeze remains authoritative: a\\ndependent instruction is held in ID/EX while io_dmemReady is low and advances\\nexactly once using io_dmemRData when readiness returns.\\n\\nReuse the existing EX/MEM forwarding select and substitute raw dmem read data\\nonly when the producer in EX/MEM is an aligned LW; all ALU producers continue\\nto forward the registered ALU result. This removes a common pipeline bubble\\nwith a single local result mux and modest hazard qualification, while leaving\\nretirement, RVFI memory fields, misalignment traps, subword semantics, and the\\nnret=1 interface unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a combinational fast-forward value that selects raw dmem_rdata for a legal aligned word load and the registered ALU result otherwise, without changing load extraction or RVFI capture.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the MEM-stage fast-forward value into the existing EX/MEM operand-forwarding input and provide the hazard unit with the load width needed to distinguish fast LW dependencies from subword loads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the load-use bubble for aligned word-load producers while retaining the current interlock for byte and halfword loads and preserving all redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Document and use the existing EX/MEM forwarding input as a producer result that may come from same-cycle MEM-stage LW data rather than assuming it is always the registered ALU result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_load_bypass.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that dependent LW consumers execute without a bubble, subword consumers still interlock, and random dmem stalls hold the dependency until ready without stale data, duplicate writes, or duplicate retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a combinational fast-forward value that selects raw dmem_rdata for a legal aligned word load and the registered ALU result otherwise, without changing load extraction or RVFI capture.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the MEM-stage fast-forward value into the existing EX/MEM operand-forwarding input and provide the hazard unit with the load width needed to distinguish fast LW dependencies from subword loads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Suppress the load-use bubble for aligned word-load producers while retaining the current interlock for byte and halfword loads and preserving all redirect and bus-stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Document and use the existing EX/MEM forwarding input as a producer result that may come from same-cycle MEM-stage LW data rather than assuming it is always the registered ALU result.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_load_bypass.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that dependent LW consumers execute without a bubble, subword consumers still interlock, and random dmem stalls hold the dependency until ready without stale data, duplicate writes, or duplicate retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 301.72, \"delta_pct\": -25.45, \"vs_baseline\": 6.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4372553, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10286, \"ff\": 1869, \"seeds\": [131.93, 123.33, 135.37], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:40:55.193247Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -25.45%): Forwarding raw io_dmemRData through the EX/MEM operand mux into the following instruction's ALU trades a load-use bubble for a dmem-read-to-EX combinational path whose FPGA timing cost outweighs the CoreMark CPI gain.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"One-entry ordered store buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"A data-bus stall currently freezes every pipeline stage even when the blocked\\noperation is a store whose address and data are already complete. With the\\nevaluation's roughly 22% random dmem backpressure, those store stalls waste\\nexecution bandwidth and can combine with later instruction stalls. Earlier\\nstructural attempts added timing-sensitive paths to fetch or EX; buffering a\\ncompleted store confines the new state and arbitration to the MEM boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"A data-bus stall currently freezes every pipeline stage even when the blocked\\noperation is a store whose address and data are already complete. With the\\nevaluation's roughly 22% random dmem backpressure, those store stalls waste\\nexecution bandwidth and can combine with later instruction stalls. Earlier\\nstructural attempts added timing-sensitive paths to fetch or EX; buffering a\\ncompleted store confines the new state and arbitration to the MEM boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry write buffer for ordinary RAM stores. When a RAM store reaches\\nMEM while io_dmemReady is low, capture its address, replicated data, and byte\\nenables, allow that store to advance to retirement, and let following\\nnon-memory instructions continue while the buffer drains. If a younger load\\nor store reaches MEM before the entry drains, hold it behind the buffered\\nstore rather than adding load forwarding or permitting memory reordering.\\n\\nUART and timing-marker writes in the 0x10000000-0x10000200 window remain\\nunbuffered and retire only on an actual bus handshake. The buffered request\\nhas priority on the external dmem port, is issued exactly once, and may be\\nreplaced by a waiting RAM store only after the older entry is accepted. This\\nshould recover cycles from isolated store backpressure while leaving the ALU,\\noperand-forwarding, and instruction-address timing cones unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry RAM-store queue with capture, stable drain request, single-handshake completion, and explicit empty/full state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate buffered stores onto dmem, enqueue stalled ordinary-RAM stores, keep MMIO stores strongly ordered, and expose whether the current memory operation may advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw store backpressure freezing with a MEM-stage blocked indication so non-memory instructions can proceed while a queued store drains, while younger memory operations remain ordered.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the store-buffer advance/block controls into the existing pipeline hold network without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_store_buffer.py\", \"description\": \"Check stalled-store capture and single issue, progress of following non-memory instructions, blocking of younger memory operations, random-stall ordering, and unbuffered UART/marker behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry RAM-store queue with capture, stable drain request, single-handshake completion, and explicit empty/full state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Arbitrate buffered stores onto dmem, enqueue stalled ordinary-RAM stores, keep MMIO stores strongly ordered, and expose whether the current memory operation may advance.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw store backpressure freezing with a MEM-stage blocked indication so non-memory instructions can proceed while a queued store drains, while younger memory operations remain ordered.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the store-buffer advance/block controls into the existing pipeline hold network without changing the nret=1 interface or RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_store_buffer.py\", \"description\": \"Check stalled-store capture and single issue, progress of following non-memory instructions, blocking of younger memory operations, random-stall ordering, and unbuffered UART/marker behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Muxing buffered versus live address, data, and byte enables onto the dmem port can break block-RAM inference for the 8 KiB wrapper memory, expanding it into 1,024 RAM16SDP4 cells and exceeding the FPGA's 648-cell placement capacity.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Late store-data forwarding at the MEM boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently treats both encoded source fields of every following\\ninstruction as EX-stage consumers. In particular, a store immediately after\\na load stalls when the load destination is only the store's rs2 data, even\\nthough that value is not required until the store reaches MEM one cycle\\nlater. CoreMark performs frequent memory copies and structure updates, so\\nthese conservative load-to-store bubbles reduce iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently treats both encoded source fields of every following\\ninstruction as EX-stage consumers. In particular, a store immediately after\\na load stalls when the load destination is only the store's rs2 data, even\\nthough that value is not required until the store reaches MEM one cycle\\nlater. CoreMark performs frequent memory copies and structure updates, so\\nthese conservative load-to-store bubbles reduce iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the architectural consumption point for store rs2 data from EX to MEM.\\nPermit a store whose address is independent of the preceding load to enter\\nEX without a load-use bubble, carry its rs2 register tag with the existing\\nEX/MEM payload, and select the registered MEM/WB result as store data in MEM\\nwhen the tags match. The normal EX forwarding path remains the source for\\nall other store dependencies, and a dependency through store rs1 still\\nstalls because the address is needed in EX.\\n\\nThis is a pipeline-topology change rather than a raw load bypass: load data\\ncrosses the existing MEM/WB register before it reaches the store-data mux,\\nso no io_dmemRData-to-EX combinational path is introduced. Only write data,\\nnot address or byte enables, gains a late registered bypass, preserving the\\nwrapper RAM's simple address/control inference. Qualifying the load-use\\ninterlock for store rs2 should recover bubbles with much less routed-Fmax\\nrisk than the prior aligned-LW EX bypass or ordered store buffer.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize a following STORE and suppress a load-use stall only when the match is exclusively on store rs2; retain the stall for store-address rs1 dependencies and all ordinary EX consumers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Provide the next-instruction store classification to hazard control and route the registered MEM/WB destination and result into the MEM-stage late store-data bypass without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select store write data and RVFI rs2/mem_wdata from the registered MEM/WB result on a matching nonzero rs2 tag, retaining the selected value and tag-correct behavior across dmem backpressure while leaving dmem address and byte-enable generation unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-store tests proving that an rs2-only dependency has no bubble, an rs1 address dependency still stalls, byte and halfword stores use the forwarded value, and random dmem stalls neither lose nor duplicate the store or retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize a following STORE and suppress a load-use stall only when the match is exclusively on store rs2; retain the stall for store-address rs1 dependencies and all ordinary EX consumers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Provide the next-instruction store classification to hazard control and route the registered MEM/WB destination and result into the MEM-stage late store-data bypass without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select store write data and RVFI rs2/mem_wdata from the registered MEM/WB result on a matching nonzero rs2 tag, retaining the selected value and tag-correct behavior across dmem backpressure while leaving dmem address and byte-enable generation unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-store tests proving that an rs2-only dependency has no bubble, an rs1 address dependency still stalls, byte and halfword stores use the forwarded value, and random dmem stalls neither lose nor duplicate the store or retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 330.47, \"delta_pct\": -23.48, \"vs_baseline\": 16.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 140.33, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246318, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10660, \"ff\": 2024, \"seeds\": [143.2, 140.33, 137.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T14:38:20.977237Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -23.48%): Even a registered late load-to-store bypass puts a MEM/WB tag compare and writeback-data mux directly on the dmem write-data path, so removing CoreMark's rs2-only load-use bubbles can lose more FPGA Fmax than it recovers in IPC.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Half-cycle register-file writeback\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The asynchronous register-file read path currently includes, on both ports,\\na write-enable gate, a five-bit WB address comparison, and a 32-bit bypass\\nmux. The reset-valid-mask experiment showed that even a small extra select on\\nthese read paths substantially damages routed Fmax, identifying them as a\\nsensitive structural boundary. Removing the existing bypass logic could\\ntherefore raise frequency without changing CoreMark's instruction count or\\nplacing new logic on the fetch, execute, or memory interfaces.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The asynchronous register-file read path currently includes, on both ports,\\na write-enable gate, a five-bit WB address comparison, and a 32-bit bypass\\nmux. The reset-valid-mask experiment showed that even a small extra select on\\nthese read paths substantially damages routed Fmax, identifying them as a\\nsensitive structural boundary. Removing the existing bypass logic could\\ntherefore raise frequency without changing CoreMark's instruction count or\\nplacing new logic on the fetch, execute, or memory interfaces.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Clock architectural register-file writes on the falling edge while retaining\\nID/EX operand capture on the rising edge. A value presented by MEM/WB after a\\nrising edge will then be committed half a cycle later and will already be in\\nthe array before the following rising-edge ID capture. This preserves the\\ncurrent same-cycle WB-to-ID dependency behavior while allowing both read\\nports to become direct x0-or-array selections with no WB tag comparison or\\nwrite-data bypass mux.\\n\\nThe MEM/WB-to-register-file write path is short and registered at its source,\\nso it should fit the half-cycle constraint more comfortably than the current\\nregister-read-to-ID/EX path fits the full-cycle critical cone. Pipeline\\nhazards, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, retirement order, RVFI data, bus-stall\\nbehavior, and the nret=1 interface remain unchanged. The implementation must\\nverify reset and held-MEM/WB cases explicitly so a retirement writes exactly\\nonce and x0 remains hardwired to zero.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Move the single architectural write port to the falling clock edge and remove same-cycle WB address comparisons and data bypass muxes from both combinational read ports, while preserving reset clearing and x0 semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Document the half-cycle WB-to-register-file timing contract and keep the existing MEM/WB write controls stable across the falling-edge commit point without changing pipeline or top-level interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-accurate checks for falling-edge writes, direct reads, x0 suppression, reset behavior, back-to-back writes, and WB-to-ID dependencies including instruction- and data-bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Move the single architectural write port to the falling clock edge and remove same-cycle WB address comparisons and data bypass muxes from both combinational read ports, while preserving reset clearing and x0 semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Document the half-cycle WB-to-register-file timing contract and keep the existing MEM/WB write controls stable across the falling-edge commit point without changing pipeline or top-level interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-accurate checks for falling-edge writes, direct reads, x0 suppression, reset behavior, back-to-back writes, and WB-to-ID dependencies including instruction- and data-bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\n52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:04  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:05  Status: failed\\nSBY 11:52:46 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (return