a cycle too late for an ID instruction that captures operands on the same rising edge its producer reaches MEM/WB, so removing the WB-to-ID write-first bypass requires an interlock or an earlier architectural write point.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:32:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Hierarchical native-funct3 M-operation encoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The decoder currently recognizes M-extension instructions with a wide\\n{funct7, funct3} case and translates them into eight arbitrary five-bit ALU\\ncodes. The ALU then selects those operations in the same flat case as every\\nRV32I operation, adding decode and result-selection depth to a timing-critical\\ncombinational execute path even though funct3 already uniquely identifies all\\neight M operations once funct7=0000001 has been validated.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The decoder currently recognizes M-extension instructions with a wide\\n{funct7, funct3} case and translates them into eight arbitrary five-bit ALU\\ncodes. The ALU then selects those operations in the same flat case as every\\nRV32I operation, adding decode and result-selection depth to a timing-critical\\ncombinational execute path even though funct3 already uniquely identifies all\\neight M operations once funct7=0000001 has been validated.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Reserve the high ALU-operation bit as an M-extension class bit and encode the\\nlow three bits directly from instruction funct3. Keep RV32I operation codes in\\nthe low class. In the ALU, first select the base or M class, then decode only\\nthe compact class-local operation bits; preserve the existing shared unsigned\\nmultiplier, signed-high corrections, native division corner cases, and the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions exactly.\\n\\nThis removes the eight-entry wide decoder translation and replaces the flat\\nnineteen-way ALU result mux with a hierarchical class selection. It changes no\\npipeline state, latency, CoreMark cycle count, bus behavior, trap discipline,\\nRVFI field, or nret setting, so a modest routed-Fmax gain should translate\\ndirectly into fitness while retaining all existing RV32M correctness checks.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign the eight ALU_M operation constants to one M-class bit plus native funct3, while keeping all RV32I operations in the base class.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"After validating funct7=0000001, emit the compact M-class/funct3 ALU code directly instead of translating eight wide {funct7, funct3} patterns.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat nineteen-operation result case with a base-versus-M hierarchy and compact class-local cases, preserving shared multiply logic, every real DIV/REM corner case, and every ALTOPS formula.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend encoding-level checks across every RV32I and RV32M ALU operation so the compact code assignment and both multiply/divide result paths remain exact.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Reassign the eight ALU_M operation constants to one M-class bit plus native funct3, while keeping all RV32I operations in the base class.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"After validating funct7=0000001, emit the compact M-class/funct3 ALU code directly instead of translating eight wide {funct7, funct3} patterns.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the flat nineteen-operation result case with a base-versus-M hierarchy and compact class-local cases, preserving shared multiply logic, every real DIV/REM corner case, and every ALTOPS formula.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend encoding-level checks across every RV32I and RV32M ALU operation so the compact code assignment and both multiply/divide result paths remain exact.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 375.0, \"delta_pct\": -19.16, \"vs_baseline\": 32.59, \"fmax_mhz\": 159.24, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10409, \"ff\": 1956, \"seeds\": [156.89, 159.24, 182.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:09:23.002476Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r11s0 (regression, -19.16%): Hierarchically selecting an M-extension class with op[4] before a funct3-local ALU case adds a serial result-mux level on the combinational execute path, so eliminating wide decoder translation can worsen FPGA Fmax when ALU output selection is already timing-critical.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Two-stage partial-product multiplier sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Sharing the MUL-family hardware produced the largest improvement in the\\ntournament, which strongly suggests that the remaining 32x32 combinational\\nproduct and its routing still materially constrain the execute-stage Fmax.\\nThe current ALU forms the complete 64-bit product and signed-high corrections\\nin the same cycle as every ordinary ALU operation, even though only\\nMUL-family instructions need that structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Sharing the MUL-family hardware produced the largest improvement in the\\ntournament, which strongly suggests that the remaining 32x32 combinational\\nproduct and its routing still materially constrain the execute-stage Fmax.\\nThe current ALU forms the complete 64-bit product and signed-high corrections\\nin the same cycle as every ordinary ALU operation, even though only\\nMUL-family instructions need that structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extract MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU from the common combinational ALU into a\\nfixed-latency two-stage multiplier sidecar. On dispatch, register four\\nunsigned 16x16 partial products plus the operand sign-correction metadata;\\nin the following cycle, combine the registered partials into the exact\\n64-bit unsigned product, apply the existing high-half signedness corrections,\\nand inject the saved instruction into EX/MEM exactly once. This creates a\\nhard pipeline cut inside the 32x32 multiply rather than merely registering\\nits already-computed result, and removes multiplier outputs from the normal\\nALU result case cone.\\n\\nA MUL-family dispatch holds younger IF/ID state for the single completion\\ncycle, while older EX/MEM work drains normally; if dmem backpressure blocks\\ncompletion, the sidecar retains its registered request and result metadata\\nuntil EX/MEM accepts it. Keep non-M instructions on the existing one-cycle\\nexecute path with unchanged forwarding, branch resolution, memory behavior,\\nand retirement. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, retain the current one-cycle\\nalgebraic stand-ins so bounded formal depth and semantics are unchanged.\\nThe expected Fmax gain from shorter DSP/LUT and routing depth should exceed\\nthe CoreMark cost of one extra cycle on MUL-family instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/multiplier.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stallable two-stage MUL-family sidecar that registers four unsigned 16x16 partial products, combines them exactly into one 64-bit product, applies signed-high corrections, and exposes a single ready/accept completion handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production MUL-family arithmetic from the ordinary combinational result cone while preserving the exact existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and all non-M ALU behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the compact saved MUL request metadata needed to preserve destination, PC, instruction, operands, control, and RVFI source values until sidecar completion.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL-family instructions into the sidecar only when EX/MEM can advance, suppress their ordinary EX/MEM write, and arbitrate each registered completion into EX/MEM exactly once without changing the normal execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed-latency MUL dispatch/completion interlock with dmem-stall priority, holding younger fetch/decode state without duplicating or dropping the resident instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the multiplier-sidecar handshake and localized pipeline hold signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, forwarding rules, RVFI order, and existing replay behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_multiplier.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cycle-aware checks for all MUL variants, partial-product carry boundaries, mixed-sign high halves, dependent consumers, single retirement, and completion held across instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/multiplier.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stallable two-stage MUL-family sidecar that registers four unsigned 16x16 partial products, combines them exactly into one 64-bit product, applies signed-high corrections, and exposes a single ready/accept completion handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production MUL-family arithmetic from the ordinary combinational result cone while preserving the exact existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and all non-M ALU behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the compact saved MUL request metadata needed to preserve destination, PC, instruction, operands, control, and RVFI source values until sidecar completion.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL-family instructions into the sidecar only when EX/MEM can advance, suppress their ordinary EX/MEM write, and arbitrate each registered completion into EX/MEM exactly once without changing the normal execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed-latency MUL dispatch/completion interlock with dmem-stall priority, holding younger fetch/decode state without duplicating or dropping the resident instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the multiplier-sidecar handshake and localized pipeline hold signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, forwarding rules, RVFI order, and existing replay behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_multiplier.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cycle-aware checks for all MUL variants, partial-product carry boundaries, mixed-sign high halves, dependent consumers, single retirement, and completion held across instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 218.63, \"delta_pct\": -52.87, \"vs_baseline\": -22.7, \"fmax_mhz\": 94.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4340258, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11178, \"ff\": 2316, \"seeds\": [94.89, 89.75, 96.86], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:12:34.841280Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r11s1 (regression, -52.87%): Splitting a mapper-optimized 32x32 product into four registered 16x16 partials does not isolate timing when their wide recombination/correction still feeds an EX/MEM completion mux and mul_dispatch enters centralized fetch/decode stalls, so a multiplier sidecar must decouple both its result and interlock paths from ordinary cycles.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-004-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Range-qualified narrow replay target tags\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The confirmed backward-branch replay buffer still stores and compares full\\n32-bit target addresses even though every permitted instruction access lies\\nin the aligned 1 MiB executable window. A prior compression attempt lost its\\nbenefit by repeatedly rechecking upper bits inside the buffer; the current\\ntarget-only design makes it possible to enforce that condition once upstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The confirmed backward-branch replay buffer still stores and compares full\\n32-bit target addresses even though every permitted instruction access lies\\nin the aligned 1 MiB executable window. A prior compression attempt lost its\\nbenefit by repeatedly rechecking upper bits inside the buffer; the current\\ntarget-only design makes it possible to enforce that condition once upstream.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Qualify replay eligibility in EX with branch_target[31:20] equal to zero,\\nthen carry only the aligned word index branch_target[19:2] into the replay\\nbuffer. Store resident and pending targets as 18-bit tags, compare accepted\\nfetches and confirmed hits using only PC[19:2], and reconstruct replay.pc and\\nreplay_next_pc with constant zero upper and low bits. Because an entry can be\\ncreated only after the single upstream range check, the buffer needs no local\\nupper-bit or alignment guards; out-of-range branches simply use the unchanged\\narchitectural redirect path and never populate or hit replay state.\\n\\nThis deletes 28 target-state bits and narrows both replay equality networks\\nwithout adding a fetch-address mux, pipeline field, or recurring guard. It\\nshould preserve CoreMark cycles, exact in-range identity, stalls, traps, RVFI,\\nand nret=1 behavior while modestly improving placement and routed Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the sole executable-range qualification to legal aligned taken-backward branch replay eligibility and expose the 18-bit aligned target tag while leaving architectural redirects unrestricted.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow resolved target tag into the replay buffer without changing the top-level interface, pipeline control, or instruction-memory address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace both full-width resident and pending target registers and their comparisons with 18-bit word tags, reconstruct full replay PCs from constants, and add no local range guards.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check exact narrow-tag fill and replay at executable-range boundaries, exclusion of out-of-range targets, replacement, and accepted-fetch behavior across bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the sole executable-range qualification to legal aligned taken-backward branch replay eligibility and expose the 18-bit aligned target tag while leaving architectural redirects unrestricted.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the narrow resolved target tag into the replay buffer without changing the top-level interface, pipeline control, or instruction-memory address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace both full-width resident and pending target registers and their comparisons with 18-bit word tags, reconstruct full replay PCs from constants, and add no local range guards.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check exact narrow-tag fill and replay at executable-range boundaries, exclusion of out-of-range targets, replacement, and accepted-fetch behavior across bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 428.41, \"delta_pct\": -7.64, \"vs_baseline\": 51.48, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.92, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10242, \"ff\": 1960, \"seeds\": [181.92, 180.08, 194.17], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:34:11.461579Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r12s0 (regression, -7.64%): Narrowing replay targets to PC[19:2] still loses when the required branch_target[31:20] range qualification is inserted into EX branch-valid control, so moving address guards upstream can trade comparator width for timing pressure on a more critical branch-resolution cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Split registered fast and multiply result lanes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split execute into parallel one-cycle fast-ALU and mapper-native multiply\\nresult lanes, and register both lanes independently in EX/MEM together with\\none is_mul bit. The fast lane contains only RV32I arithmetic, address, and\\nlogical results, so its register input no longer passes through a mux fed by\\nthe 64-bit product or signed-high correction network. The multiply lane keeps\\nthe currently successful single unsigned 32x32 product and exact high-half\\ncorrections, with no partial-product decomposition and no new pipeline stall.\\n\\nSelect the already-registered lane at the MEM boundary for forwarding,\\nwriteback, and RVFI, while memory operations use the fast lane directly so\\nno new mux is placed on the dmem address path. This trades 32 result flops and\\na shallow post-register selector for removal of the multiplier from the\\nordinary EX result cone, preserving CoreMark cycles and all existing bus,\\nreplay, trap, and nret=1 retirement behavior while targeting higher routed\\nFmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 427.64, \"delta_pct\": -7.81, \"vs_baseline\": 51.21, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.59, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10307, \"ff\": 1993, \"seeds\": [190.62, 177.02, 181.59], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:48:16.774552Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r13s1 (regression, -7.81%): Independently registering fast and multiply result lanes cannot raise Fmax while the single-cycle 32x32 product still sets the ID/EX-to-EX/MEM critical path, and the extra 32-bit lane plus post-register forwarding/writeback selectors only add FPGA placement and routing pressure.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share one multiplier across all RV32M multiply variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one 64-bit unsigned product for every MUL-family operation and derive the\\nsigned high halves with 32-bit correction subtractors: subtract b from the high\\nhalf when a is negative, and subtract a as well when b is negative. MUL continues\\nto use the common low half. This identity is exact modulo 2^64 and should remove\\ntwo large parallel multipliers, lowering LUT pressure and improving routed Fmax\\nwithout changing pipeline latency, CoreMark IPC, or the existing ALTOPS behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 404.74, \"delta_pct\": 43.11, \"vs_baseline\": 43.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10073, \"ff\": 1860, \"seeds\": [181.79, 175.38, 185.53], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T12:55:00.760698Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product across RV32M MUL variants and correcting only the signed high halves avoids parallel inferred multipliers, substantially reducing multiplier fabric and routing pressure without adding pipeline latency.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"EX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\\n\\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 221.94, \"delta_pct\": -45.16, \"vs_baseline\": -21.53, \"fmax_mhz\": 93.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4234052, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 13089, \"ff\": 2982, \"seeds\": [93.97, 105.74, 93.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:23:20.366277Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Fast aligned-LW MEM-to-EX bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline always inserts a bubble after a load whose destination is used\\nby the following instruction, even though an aligned LW's complete 32-bit\\nresult is already present on io_dmemRData during the load's MEM cycle. CoreMark\\nperforms many pointer and structure word loads followed immediately by address,\\ncompare, or arithmetic uses, so this fixed interlock directly reduces useful\\niterations per cycle. Prior failures argue for a local datapath change that\\navoids both the fetch-PC feedback cone and any new global stall source.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a fast forwarding path from the current MEM-stage aligned LW data directly\\nto the following instruction's EX operand mux. Suppress the load-use bubble\\nonly for legal aligned word loads; retain the existing one-cycle interlock for\\nbyte and halfword loads so their shift/sign-extension network is not placed in\\nthe EX critical path. The existing dmem-stall freeze remains authoritative: a\\ndependent instruction is held in ID/EX while io_dmemReady is low and advances\\nexactly once using io_dmemRData when readiness returns.\\n\\nReuse the existing EX/MEM forwarding select and substitute raw dmem read data\\nonly when the producer in EX/MEM is an aligned LW; all ALU producers continue\\nto forward the registered ALU result. This removes a common pipeline bubble\\nwith a single local result mux and modest hazard qualification, while leaving\\nretirement, RVFI memory fields, misalignment traps, subword semantics, and the\\nnret=1 interface unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": 