ple trace: liveness_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_liveness_check_sv_38_58 at rvfi_liveness_check.sv:38.5-38.28 step 20\\nSBY 14:01:18 [liveness_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/last_run-53177.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-002-r7s1/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-53177/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering the IF/ID instruction response is an elastic-buffer protocol change, not a pure retime; request-PC advance, response valid, decode consume/refill, and redirect/replay invalidation must be tracked independently or instructions can be dropped or stranded, as the liveness, PC-forwarding, and register-state checks expose.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Completion-decoupled divide execute island\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Dispatch DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of ID/EX into a dedicated iterative\\nexecute island and immediately enter a registered divide mode. On dispatch,\\nallow ID/EX to capture exactly the single sequential successor already at\\ndecode, then hold that register and the fetch PC using the registered mode\\nbit until completion. Older EX/MEM work drains normally while the divider\\nruns; when both the result is ready and EX/MEM can accept it, inject one\\nsaved divide payload and result into EX/MEM, clear divide mode, and resume\\nthe held successor on the following cycle.\\n\\nThis completion-decoupled topology removes combinational div_busy/div_done\\nfeedback from hazard_unit and from the normal ALU-result path. Ordinary\\ninstructions see only the existing pipeline, while the divide island owns\\nits operands, RVFI metadata, corner-case state, and one completion-valid\\nbit. Keep the existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results on the normal\\npath so bounded formal depth is unchanged. The large divider-area reduction\\nobserved previously should then translate into routed-Fmax headroom without\\nthe high-fanout interlock that caused that experiment's regression.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 387.51, \"delta_pct\": -10.27, \"vs_baseline\": 37.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 164.55, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246317, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5903, \"ff\": 2385, \"seeds\": [160.57, 166.94, 164.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:37:13.351777Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.27%): Registering divide mode is not true completion decoupling when it still drives PC and ID/EX holds and gates branch redirect and EX/MEM control, because rare DIV/REM state then burdens ordinary-cycle clock-enable and execute timing cones.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Collapse MEM and WB into direct commit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline carries a wide MEM/WB payload for every\\ninstruction and keeps a second writeback forwarding source on both EX operand\\nmuxes. The register exists mainly to delay retirement by one cycle: EX/MEM\\nalready holds all architectural metadata, and the memory stage already forms\\nthe final load value, masks, trap state, and writeback value combinationally.\\nOn the Tang Nano fabric, deleting a pipeline boundary and a forwarding tier is\\na promising way to reduce both routed state and ordinary-instruction mux depth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline carries a wide MEM/WB payload for every\\ninstruction and keeps a second writeback forwarding source on both EX operand\\nmuxes. The register exists mainly to delay retirement by one cycle: EX/MEM\\nalready holds all architectural metadata, and the memory stage already forms\\nthe final load value, masks, trap state, and writeback value combinationally.\\nOn the Tang Nano fabric, deleting a pipeline boundary and a forwarding tier is\\na promising way to reduce both routed state and ordinary-instruction mux depth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert the core to IF-ID-EX/MEM-commit by making the memory stage a purely\\ncombinational completion block over the held EX/MEM entry. An instruction\\ncommits directly when EX/MEM is valid and either is not a memory operation or\\nits dmem handshake is ready; a stalled memory entry remains held exactly as it\\nis today and cannot retire or write twice. Feed the final commit value to the\\nregister file's existing write-first port and drive RVFI directly from the\\nsame completion bundle, then remove the MEM/WB register, WB stage, and MEM/WB\\noperand-forwarding tier.\\n\\nPreserve the current one-cycle load-use interlock. Thus a load result crosses\\nonly into register-file writeback and same-edge ID capture, never directly\\ninto the following instruction's EX ALU cone; immediate ALU dependencies still\\nuse the registered EX/MEM bypass, while dependencies two positions back see\\nthe direct commit through the existing write-first register-file behavior.\\nThis should leave steady-state CoreMark IPC and bus semantics unchanged while\\nremoving hundreds of pipeline flops, their routing, and one 32-bit input from\\neach EX forwarding mux, increasing routed Fmax without speculative frontend\\nlogic, new global interlocks, or any change to the nret=1 contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the registered MEM/WB payload with a compact combinational commit bundle containing final writeback, RVFI, memory-mask, trap, and valid fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB register and emit a ready-qualified combinational completion bundle from EX/MEM, preserving fixed lane selection, alignment traps, memory masks, and exact single-handshake commit semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retire and write the register file directly from the completion bundle, increment RVFI order only on commit, remove MEM/WB and WB-stage wiring, and keep dmem-stalled EX/MEM entries held until ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce each EX operand selector to registered EX/MEM forwarding versus the ID/EX value, deleting the MEM/WB comparison and select encoding while retaining x0 and youngest-writer rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the reduced two-source forwarding selection without adding completion data or dmem input to the execute datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the load-use bubble and EX/MEM dmem hold, but remove MEM/WB hold generation because completion valid is directly qualified by dmem readiness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the obsolete standalone WB stage after its final-result selection and write qualification move into the direct commit path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for ALU and load dependency distances, write-first commit-to-ID capture, memory stalls, single store issue, single retirement, RVFI order, and load or misalignment trap behavior in the four-stage topology.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the registered MEM/WB payload with a compact combinational commit bundle containing final writeback, RVFI, memory-mask, trap, and valid fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB register and emit a ready-qualified combinational completion bundle from EX/MEM, preserving fixed lane selection, alignment traps, memory masks, and exact single-handshake commit semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retire and write the register file directly from the completion bundle, increment RVFI order only on commit, remove MEM/WB and WB-stage wiring, and keep dmem-stalled EX/MEM entries held until ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Reduce each EX operand selector to registered EX/MEM forwarding versus the ID/EX value, deleting the MEM/WB comparison and select encoding while retaining x0 and youngest-writer rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the reduced two-source forwarding selection without adding completion data or dmem input to the execute datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain the load-use bubble and EX/MEM dmem hold, but remove MEM/WB hold generation because completion valid is directly qualified by dmem readiness.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the obsolete standalone WB stage after its final-result selection and write qualification move into the direct commit path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for ALU and load dependency distances, write-first commit-to-ID capture, memory stalls, single store issue, single retirement, RVFI order, and load or misalignment trap behavior in the four-stage topology.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): Direct combinational commit from EX/MEM makes post-edge RVFI observe a newly advanced load with stale io_dmemRData from the prior address, so load retirement still needs a response/commit holding boundary even if the full MEM/WB payload is removed.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed shared PC-relative target adder\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one shared PC-plus-immediate value combinationally in ID and register\\nit alongside each instruction in ID/EX. Use that registered value in EX for\\nconditional-branch targets, direct-JAL targets, and AUIPC results; retain the\\nexisting forwarded rs1-plus-immediate adder only for JALR. This retimes the\\nwide PC-relative addition across the ID/EX boundary and removes it from the\\nhigh-frequency EX-to-fetch redirect path without changing branch latency,\\nprediction policy, instruction count, or any bus handshake.\\n\\nThe registered sum must follow the instruction through every existing hold,\\nflush, replay, and reset case. EX remains authoritative for branch condition,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, target-alignment traps, redirect qualification, and\\nRVFI pc_wdata. Sharing the retimed value with AUIPC also prevents synthesis\\nfrom retaining a redundant PC-relative adder. The expected result is higher\\nmedian Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles, for a net fitness improvement with\\nonly 32 additional pipeline bits and no new global control or fetch-PC mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 422.03, \"delta_pct\": -9.02, \"vs_baseline\": 49.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 179.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10363, \"ff\": 1990, \"seeds\": [190.22, 179.21, 153.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:19:59.820672Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -9.02%): Retiming the shared 32-bit PC-plus-immediate adder from EX into combinational ID can merely trade the redirect cone for a decode-to-ID/EX path plus 32 routed payload bits, so PC-relative target precomputation is not a timing win unless the added decode and pipeline-routing cost is cheaper.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Two-stage partial-product multiplier sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Sharing the MUL-family hardware produced the largest improvement in the\\ntournament, which strongly suggests that the remaining 32x32 combinational\\nproduct and its routing still materially constrain the execute-stage Fmax.\\nThe current ALU forms the complete 64-bit product and signed-high corrections\\nin the same cycle as every ordinary ALU operation, even though only\\nMUL-family instructions need that structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Sharing the MUL-family hardware produced the largest improvement in the\\ntournament, which strongly suggests that the remaining 32x32 combinational\\nproduct and its routing still materially constrain the execute-stage Fmax.\\nThe current ALU forms the complete 64-bit product and signed-high corrections\\nin the same cycle as every ordinary ALU operation, even though only\\nMUL-family instructions need that structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extract MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU from the common combinational ALU into a\\nfixed-latency two-stage multiplier sidecar. On dispatch, register four\\nunsigned 16x16 partial products plus the operand sign-correction metadata;\\nin the following cycle, combine the registered partials into the exact\\n64-bit unsigned product, apply the existing high-half signedness corrections,\\nand inject the saved instruction into EX/MEM exactly once. This creates a\\nhard pipeline cut inside the 32x32 multiply rather than merely registering\\nits already-computed result, and removes multiplier outputs from the normal\\nALU result case cone.\\n\\nA MUL-family dispatch holds younger IF/ID state for the single completion\\ncycle, while older EX/MEM work drains normally; if dmem backpressure blocks\\ncompletion, the sidecar retains its registered request and result metadata\\nuntil EX/MEM accepts it. Keep non-M instructions on the existing one-cycle\\nexecute path with unchanged forwarding, branch resolution, memory behavior,\\nand retirement. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, retain the current one-cycle\\nalgebraic stand-ins so bounded formal depth and semantics are unchanged.\\nThe expected Fmax gain from shorter DSP/LUT and routing depth should exceed\\nthe CoreMark cost of one extra cycle on MUL-family instructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/multiplier.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stallable two-stage MUL-family sidecar that registers four unsigned 16x16 partial products, combines them exactly into one 64-bit product, applies signed-high corrections, and exposes a single ready/accept completion handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production MUL-family arithmetic from the ordinary combinational result cone while preserving the exact existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and all non-M ALU behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the compact saved MUL request metadata needed to preserve destination, PC, instruction, operands, control, and RVFI source values until sidecar completion.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL-family instructions into the sidecar only when EX/MEM can advance, suppress their ordinary EX/MEM write, and arbitrate each registered completion into EX/MEM exactly once without changing the normal execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed-latency MUL dispatch/completion interlock with dmem-stall priority, holding younger fetch/decode state without duplicating or dropping the resident instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the multiplier-sidecar handshake and localized pipeline hold signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, forwarding rules, RVFI order, and existing replay behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_multiplier.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cycle-aware checks for all MUL variants, partial-product carry boundaries, mixed-sign high halves, dependent consumers, single retirement, and completion held across instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/multiplier.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stallable two-stage MUL-family sidecar that registers four unsigned 16x16 partial products, combines them exactly into one 64-bit product, applies signed-high corrections, and exposes a single ready/accept completion handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production MUL-family arithmetic from the ordinary combinational result cone while preserving the exact existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and all non-M ALU behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the compact saved MUL request metadata needed to preserve destination, PC, instruction, operands, control, and RVFI source values until sidecar completion.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch MUL-family instructions into the sidecar only when EX/MEM can advance, suppress their ordinary EX/MEM write, and arbitrate each registered completion into EX/MEM exactly once without changing the normal execute path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed-latency MUL dispatch/completion interlock with dmem-stall priority, holding younger fetch/decode state without duplicating or dropping the resident instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the multiplier-sidecar handshake and localized pipeline hold signals while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface, forwarding rules, RVFI order, and existing replay behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_multiplier.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cycle-aware checks for all MUL variants, partial-product carry boundaries, mixed-sign high halves, dependent consumers, single retirement, and completion held across instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 218.63, \"delta_pct\": -52.87, \"vs_baseline\": -22.7, \"fmax_mhz\": 94.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4340258, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11178, \"ff\": 2316, \"seeds\": [94.89, 89.75, 96.86], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:12:34.841280Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r11s1 (regression, -52.87%): Splitting a mapper-optimized 32x32 product into four registered 16x16 partials does not isolate timing when their wide recombination/correction still feeds an EX/MEM completion mux and mul_dispatch enters centralized fetch/decode stalls, so a multiplier sidecar must decouple both its result and interlock paths from ordinary cycles.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Stage-specialized narrow pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current packed pipeline bundles carry control and data long after their\\nlast consumer. In particular, EX/MEM retains branch outcome and target fields\\nthat no downstream stage reads, and both EX/MEM and MEM/WB forward the complete\\ndecode control record even though execute-only ALU and control-flow bits are\\ndead. At the current high Fmax, these unnecessary flops and wide inter-stage\\nroutes can increase placement congestion and clock-enable fanout without\\nimproving IPC or architectural observability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current packed pipeline bundles carry control and data long after their\\nlast consumer. In particular, EX/MEM retains branch outcome and target fields\\nthat no downstream stage reads, and both EX/MEM and MEM/WB forward the complete\\ndecode control record even though execute-only ALU and control-flow bits are\\ndead. At the current high Fmax, these unnecessary flops and wide inter-stage\\nroutes can increase placement congestion and clock-enable fanout without\\nimproving IPC or architectural observability.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the uniform carried-forward control record with stage-specific control\\npayloads. Keep the full decoded control only in ID/EX; EX/MEM will retain only\\nmemory, writeback, and trap controls, while MEM/WB will retain only the controls\\nneeded for final writeback and rvfi_trap. Remove EX/MEM branch_taken and\\nbranch_target after branch resolution because pc_next already captures the\\narchitectural outcome and the replay buffer consumes its resolved signals\\ndirectly from EX.\\n\\nPreserve every RVFI operand, PC, instruction, memory, and result field through\\nthe existing registered retirement boundary, and preserve the exact reset,\\nflush, dmem-hold, forwarding, and single-retirement behavior. Construct each\\nnarrower payload directly at its producing stage so this change deletes state\\nand routing rather than adding decode or selection logic to active datapaths.\\nCoreMark cycles should therefore remain unchanged while reduced pipeline width\\nand control fanout improve routed median Fmax on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB control records, remove dead post-EX branch outcome fields, and retain every field required by forwarding, memory behavior, writeback, and RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate only the narrowed EX/MEM memory, writeback, and trap controls while keeping resolved branch signals local to redirect and replay handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and register only the MEM/WB writeback and trap controls, preserving alignment traps, held-load forwarding data, masks, and retirement metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, writeback, and RVFI wiring for the stage-specialized control types without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline handshakes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the compact MEM/WB writeback controls while preserving the existing final-result mux and register-file write qualification.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_payloads.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks across ALU, branch, jump, load, store, illegal, misaligned, forwarding, and random bus-stall cases to prove narrowed payloads preserve side effects, RVFI fields, ordering, and single retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB control records, remove dead post-EX branch outcome fields, and retain every field required by forwarding, memory behavior, writeback, and RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate only the narrowed EX/MEM memory, writeback, and trap controls while keeping resolved branch signals local to redirect and replay handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM controls and register only the MEM/WB writeback and trap controls, preserving alignment traps, held-load forwarding data, masks, and retirement metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, writeback, and RVFI wiring for the stage-specialized control types without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline handshakes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the compact MEM/WB writeback controls while preserving the existing final-result mux and register-file write qualification.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_payloads.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks across ALU, branch, jump, load, store, illegal, misaligned, forwarding, and random bus-stall cases to prove narrowed payloads preserve side effects, RVFI fields, ordering, and single retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 414.9, \"delta_pct\": -10.55, \"vs_baseline\": 46.7, \"fmax_mhz\": 176.18, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10310, \"ff\": 1959, \"seeds\": [172.62, 194.17, 176.18], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:31:18.058416Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Split registered fast and multiply result lanes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The shared unsigned multiplier was the largest proven improvement, but its\\nproduct, signed-high corrections, and M/non-M result selection still feed the\\nsame EX/MEM result register as every ordinary ALU instruction. The failed\\npipelined multiplier sidecar showed that extra latency, recombination, and a\\ncentralized interlock overwhelm any timing benefit; isolating the result\\nlanes without changing instruction latency avoids those costs.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split execute into parallel one-cycle fast-ALU and mapper-native multiply\\nresult lanes, and register both lanes independently in EX/MEM together with\\none is_mul bit. The fast lane contains only RV32I arithmetic, address, and\\nlogical results, so its register input no longer passes through a mux fed by\\nthe 64-bit product or signed-high correction network. The multiply lane keeps\\nthe currently successful single unsigned 32x32 product and exact high-half\\ncorrections, with no partial-product decomposition and no new pipeline stall.\\n\\nSelect the already-registered lane at the MEM boundary for forwarding,\\nwriteback, and RVFI, while memory operations use the fast lane directly so\\nno new mux is placed on the dmem address path. This trades 32 result flops and\\na shallow post-register selector for removal of the multiplier from the\\nordinary EX result cone, preserving CoreMark cycles and all existing bus,\\nreplay, trap, and nret=1 retirement behavior while targeting higher routed\\nFmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate the RV32I fast result from the shared unsigned multiply result and signed-high corrections, preserving all production RV32M values and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single EX/MEM ALU result field with independently registered fast and multiply result lanes plus an is_mul selector bit, while leaving architectural and RVFI payloads intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture fast and multiply results into separate EX/MEM lanes in the same cycle, without a multiplier sidecar, completion arbitration, added latency, or any new pipeline interlock.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the selected registered result for EX/MEM forwarding while routing the fast result directly for memory addresses and preserving the existing hazard and nret=1 interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the registered multiply lane only for M-extension writeback and RVFI propagation, while keeping load/store address generation on the unmuxed fast-result lane.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain and extend exact RV32M result checks across low and signed or unsigned high halves, and add mixed M/non-M sequences that verify forwarding, writeback, and single retirement from the split lanes.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 427.64, \"delta_pct\": -7.81, \"vs_baseline\": 51.21, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.59, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10307, \"ff\": 1993, \"seeds\": [190.62, 177.02, 181.59], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T19:48:16.774552Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-005-r13s1 (regression, -7.81%): Independently registering fast and multiply result lanes cannot raise Fmax while the single-cycle 32x32 product still sets the ID/EX-to-EX/MEM critical path, and the extra 32-bit lane plus post-register forwarding/writeback selectors only add FPGA placement and routing pressure.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-005-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Uniform two-stage execute pipeline\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Routed timing evidence and the last structural experiment both identify the\\nsingle-cycle ID/EX-to-EX/MEM multiply path as the remaining Fmax limiter.\\nPrior multiplier sidecars tried to shorten it with operation-specific busy\\nstate and global interlocks, but those controls burdened ordinary cycles and\\nthe completion mux still sat on the long arithmetic path. A pipeline stage\\nused uniformly by every instruction can provide a true timing cut without\\ndispatch, busy, or completion arbitration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Routed timing evidence and the last structural experiment both identify the\\nsingle-cycle ID/EX-to-EX/MEM multiply path as the remaining Fmax limiter.\\nPrior multiplier sidecars tried to shorten it with operation-specific busy\\nstate and global interlocks, but those controls burdened ordinary cycles and\\nthe completion mux still sat on the long arithmetic path. A pipeline stage\\nused uniformly by every instruction can provide a true timing cut without\\ndispatch, busy, or completion arbitration.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert execute into two fixed pipeline stages. EX1 performs forwarding,\\noperand selection, the fast ALU, and the existing shared unsigned multiply,\\nthen registers both the selected architectural result and all instruction\\nmetadata in a new EX1/EX2 payload. EX2 performs only the shallow downstream\\nrouting into the existing memory-stage register. Resolve branches in EX1 so\\nredirect and replay latency do not increase, and extend forwarding to use\\nthe registered EX1/EX2 result as the youngest producer.\\n\\nEvery instruction traverses the new stage, so there is no M-specific state,\\nglobal multi-cycle interlock, or completion mux. The pipeline retains a\\nthroughput of one instruction per cycle; only load-use dependencies require\\nan additional bubble because a load reaches memory one cycle later. Holding\\nEX1/EX2 together with the existing stages under dmem backpressure preserves\\nprecise in-order retirement. A hard register boundary after the mapper-native\\nproduct should materially raise Fmax, and that gain should outweigh the\\nmodest CoreMark cost of the extra load-use delay.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX1/EX2 pipeline payload carrying the registered result, store operand, destination, control, PC, instruction, and RVFI source metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split execute into EX1 arithmetic and branch resolution followed by a shallow EX2 transfer, with a true register cut after the shared multiplier and no operation-specific handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the added execute stage, align redirect and replay signals with EX1, propagate dmem holds through the new register, and preserve the nret=1 retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_