struction-memory backpressure. Prior attempts show that another wide tag\\ncomparison is too expensive, but a second data word under the existing tag\\ncan extend useful coverage without another associative lookup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The one-entry confirmed branch-target instruction replay buffer is the only\\npredictor design that has improved fitness: it removes a hot loop bubble\\nwithout placing target selection on the instruction-address timing cone.\\nIt currently caches only the first target instruction, so target+4 must still\\nbe fetched normally and is exposed to the benchmark's roughly 22 percent\\ninstruction-memory backpressure. Prior attempts show that another wide tag\\ncomparison is too expensive, but a second data word under the existing tag\\ncan extend useful coverage without another associative lookup.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Turn the existing entry into a two-instruction loop-head replay line sharing\\nthe same exact branch-PC and target tags. During a cold fill, capture both the\\ntarget and target+4 instructions on their respective accepted fetches. On an\\nEX-confirmed hit, inject the target instruction immediately as today, queue\\nthe cached target+4 instruction for the following accepted ID slot, and move\\nthe registered fetch PC directly to target+8. The queued second instruction\\ntherefore hides an instruction-bus stall at target+4 and extends the useful\\nreplay burst without adding any speculative lookup or mux to io_imemAddr.\\n\\nUse an explicit valid/consume handshake for the second replay beat so dmem\\nstalls and load-use hazards hold it without loss or duplication. Hazard\\nsource-address checks must select the pending replay instruction rather than\\nthe simultaneously visible live fetch. Entry validity is published only\\nafter both words have been accepted, and all legal/aligned/backward-branch,\\nexact-tag, RVFI, redirect, and retirement behavior remains unchanged. This\\nadds one 32-bit instruction register and a small replay-phase bit, but no\\nsecond tag, target adder, or parallel equality tree, so it should preserve\\nrouted Fmax while reducing CoreMark cycles under the scored stall model.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the replay payload/control representation for a stallable second cached instruction while leaving architectural pipeline and RVFI fields unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture target and target+4 instructions under one existing exact tag pair, publish the line only after both accepted fills, emit the first word on an EX-confirmed hit, and retain the second word until an explicit ID consume handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire replay-valid/consume control, qualify the second beat against pipeline acceptance, and select replay instruction source fields for load-use detection without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Advance the registered PC to cached target+8 on a confirmed two-word replay and suppress the live target+8 payload while the queued target+4 instruction is being consumed, keeping io_imemAddr driven only by the PC register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept each replay beat exactly once, hold the queued second beat across dmem or load-use stalls, and preserve flush and redirect correctness without duplicating retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for two-word fill, ordered replay, target+8 resumption, instruction-bus-stall hiding, second-word load-use hazards, dmem-stall holds, tag misses, and single retirement of each cached instruction.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the replay payload/control representation for a stallable second cached instruction while leaving architectural pipeline and RVFI fields unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture target and target+4 instructions under one existing exact tag pair, publish the line only after both accepted fills, emit the first word on an EX-confirmed hit, and retain the second word until an explicit ID consume handshake.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire replay-valid/consume control, qualify the second beat against pipeline acceptance, and select replay instruction source fields for load-use detection without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Advance the registered PC to cached target+8 on a confirmed two-word replay and suppress the live target+8 payload while the queued target+4 instruction is being consumed, keeping io_imemAddr driven only by the PC register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept each replay beat exactly once, hold the queued second beat across dmem or load-use stalls, and preserve flush and redirect correctness without duplicating retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for two-word fill, ordered replay, target+8 resumption, instruction-bus-stall hiding, second-word load-use hazards, dmem-stall holds, tag misses, and single retirement of each cached instruction.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 283.98, \"delta_pct\": -34.25, \"vs_baseline\": 0.41, \"fmax_mhz\": 119.08, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4193281, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11070, \"ff\": 2115, \"seeds\": [119.08, 116.54, 121.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:15:58.133934Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.25%): Extending a confirmed one-word replay entry to target+4 can regress badly even with shared tags, because the phase-controlled 32-bit instruction/PC selection and stall-safe consume/cancel control burden decode and hazard paths more than hiding occasional instruction-memory backpressure repays.\"}\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Target-only replay-buffer tag\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The confirmed one-entry replay buffer is the only control-flow optimization\\nthat has improved the current core, but each hit compares both the owning\\nbranch PC and the cached target PC. The payload is simply the instruction at\\nthat target, so the branch-PC identity is redundant: any legal, aligned,\\ntaken backward branch resolving to the same target can safely reuse it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The confirmed one-entry replay buffer is the only control-flow optimization\\nthat has improved the current core, but each hit compares both the owning\\nbranch PC and the cached target PC. The payload is simply the instruction at\\nthat target, so the branch-PC identity is redundant: any legal, aligned,\\ntaken backward branch resolving to the same target can safely reuse it.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove the stored branch-PC tag, its pending-fill copy, and the parallel\\n32-bit branch-PC equality from the replay-buffer hit path. Retain the exact\\n32-bit target comparison, captured target instruction, confirmed-EX lookup,\\naccepted-fetch fill protocol, and target-plus-four resume behavior unchanged.\\nThis is a strict hardware reduction with no new muxes or qualifiers, and\\nshould reduce comparator fabric, flops, and routing on the replay decision\\nwhile modestly broadening reuse when multiple backward branches share a loop\\nhead. Architectural execution, stalls, RVFI, traps, and nret remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the entry and pending branch-PC tags and qualify confirmed replay hits solely with the existing exact target-PC tag and eligibility signals, preserving fill and replay timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused resolved branch-PC output while retaining legal aligned taken-backward qualification and the exact resolved target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the redundant resolved-branch-PC wiring and update the replay-buffer connection without changing pipeline, bus-stall, or nret=1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that same-target backward branches reuse one cached instruction, different targets miss and refill, and replay remains single-shot and stall-safe.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Delete the entry and pending branch-PC tags and qualify confirmed replay hits solely with the existing exact target-PC tag and eligibility signals, preserving fill and replay timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the now-unused resolved branch-PC output while retaining legal aligned taken-backward qualification and the exact resolved target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the redundant resolved-branch-PC wiring and update the replay-buffer connection without changing pipeline, bus-stall, or nret=1 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that same-target backward branches reuse one cached instruction, different targets miss and refill, and replay remains single-shot and stall-safe.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 463.86, \"delta_pct\": 7.4, \"vs_baseline\": 64.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 196.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10217, \"ff\": 1956, \"seeds\": [186.25, 197.01, 196.97], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:40:47.746851Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (improvement, +7.40%): A confirmed backward-branch replay entry is safely keyed by its exact target alone, so dropping the redundant branch-PC tag removes 32-bit comparator, flop, and routing cost while allowing branches that share a loop head to reuse the cached instruction.\"}\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Completion-decoupled divide execute island\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The production ALU still contains four combinational divide and remainder\\noperators, while the earlier iterative-divider attempt showed that sharing\\nthem can nearly halve LUT use but lost Fmax because divider wait control was\\nplaced in the centralized, high-fanout hazard path. CoreMark executes very\\nfew divide-class instructions, so the arithmetic remains a strong candidate\\nfor serialization if its control is kept off ordinary-cycle timing cones.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Dispatch DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of ID/EX into a dedicated iterative\\nexecute island and immediately enter a registered divide mode. On dispatch,\\nallow ID/EX to capture exactly the single sequential successor already at\\ndecode, then hold that register and the fetch PC using the registered mode\\nbit until completion. Older EX/MEM work drains normally while the divider\\nruns; when both the result is ready and EX/MEM can accept it, inject one\\nsaved divide payload and result into EX/MEM, clear divide mode, and resume\\nthe held successor on the following cycle.\\n\\nThis completion-decoupled topology removes combinational div_busy/div_done\\nfeedback from hazard_unit and from the normal ALU-result path. Ordinary\\ninstructions see only the existing pipeline, while the divide island owns\\nits operands, RVFI metadata, corner-case state, and one completion-valid\\nbit. Keep the existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results on the normal\\npath so bounded formal depth is unchanged. The large divider-area reduction\\nobserved previously should then translate into routed-Fmax headroom without\\nthe high-fanout interlock that caused that experiment's regression.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an iterative shared quotient/remainder execute island with registered request and completion state, signed result correction, and exact RV32M zero-divisor and overflow behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production DIV and REM operators from the common combinational ALU while retaining their existing one-cycle RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS definitions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the saved divide payload needed to carry destination, operands, instruction, PC, control, and RVFI source metadata independently of ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch divide-class instructions into the island, prevent them from entering EX/MEM early, arbitrate a completed saved divide into a free EX/MEM slot exactly once, and leave the ordinary execute path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the single successor captured at divide dispatch while registered divide mode is active, then release it only after the divide completion has entered EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Hold the registered fetch PC directly from registered divide mode, allowing the dispatch edge to advance once so the successor resident in ID/EX is neither lost nor duplicated.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire registered divide-mode and completion-accept signals locally between fetch, decode, and execute without routing divider progress through centralized hazard logic or changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware checks for dispatch with one held successor, older memory stalls, completion backpressure, single retirement, forwarding after resume, all RV32M corner cases, and randomized bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 387.51, \"delta_pct\": -10.27, \"vs_baseline\": 37.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 164.55, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246317, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5903, \"ff\": 2385, \"seeds\": [160.57, 166.94, 164.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:37:13.351777Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.27%): Registering divide mode is not true completion decoupling when it still drives PC and ID/EX holds and gates branch redirect and EX/MEM control, because rare DIV/REM state then burdens ordinary-cycle clock-enable and execute timing cones.\"}\n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"B-immediate tag for confirmed branch replay\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure that\\nhas improved fitness, but every hit currently requires both a full 32-bit branch-PC\\nequality and a second full 32-bit resolved-target equality. The target comparison\\nis wider than necessary because an RV32I conditional branch target is completely\\ndetermined by its 12 encoded B-immediate bits plus the already-compared branch PC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure that\\nhas improved fitness, but every hit currently requires both a full 32-bit branch-PC\\nequality and a second full 32-bit resolved-target equality. The target comparison\\nis wider than necessary because an RV32I conditional branch target is completely\\ndetermined by its 12 encoded B-immediate bits plus the already-compared branch PC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the replay entry's 32-bit target tag comparison with an exact comparison of\\nthe branch instruction's B-immediate bits [12:1]. EX will export these bits directly\\nfrom the already-latched decoded immediate, and the replay buffer will store them\\nwhen a confirmed miss begins a fill. A hit will require the unchanged full branch-PC\\nmatch plus the 12-bit displacement match, while the stored full target PC remains\\navailable solely to label the cached instruction and advance fetch to target+4.\\n\\nThis is an exact tag transformation for legal conditional branches: branch PC and\\nB-immediate uniquely determine the resolved target, including backward offsets, so\\nhit behavior, CoreMark cycles, redirects, stalls, RVFI state, and illegal/misaligned\\nhandling remain unchanged. Removing 20 comparator bits and their routed resolved-\\ntarget inputs from the replay-hit cone should raise FPGA Fmax without adding fetch-PC\\nfeedback logic, replay phases, range guards, or another entry.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export the exact B-immediate displacement tag from the valid legal conditional branch in EX alongside the existing resolved branch PC and target signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the compact resolved displacement tag between EX and the confirmed replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface or any hazard and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Store and compare the 12-bit B-immediate tag instead of comparing the full 32-bit target tag, while retaining the full target PC for fill matching, replay PC, and target-plus-four resumption.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed alias checks showing that identical branch PCs with different positive and negative B-immediates miss, while exact displacement matches replay correctly across instruction and data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export the exact B-immediate displacement tag from the valid legal conditional branch in EX alongside the existing resolved branch PC and target signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the compact resolved displacement tag between EX and the confirmed replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface or any hazard and redirect priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Store and compare the 12-bit B-immediate tag instead of comparing the full 32-bit target tag, while retaining the full target PC for fill matching, replay PC, and target-plus-four resumption.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed alias checks showing that identical branch PCs with different positive and negative B-immediates miss, while exact displacement matches replay correctly across instruction and data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 450.2, \"delta_pct\": 4.24, \"vs_baseline\": 59.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 191.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10248, \"ff\": 2052, \"seeds\": [195.96, 191.17, 189.5], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:34:13.579251Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Prune post-execute pipeline payloads\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX/MEM register still carries resolved branch_taken and branch_target values\\nthat no downstream stage consumes, and the full execute control bundle is copied\\nthrough EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most fields are dead after EX. These wide,\\nfrequently toggling registers consume flops and routing on an FPGA where routed\\nFmax now dominates fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX/MEM register still carries resolved branch_taken and branch_target values\\nthat no downstream stage consumes, and the full execute control bundle is copied\\nthrough EX/MEM and MEM/WB even though most fields are dead after EX. These wide,\\nfrequently toggling registers consume flops and routing on an FPGA where routed\\nFmax now dominates fitness.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce stage-specific compact control payloads and remove resolved-branch\\nmetadata after it has produced pc_next and the replay-buffer signals in EX.\\nEX/MEM will retain only memory, writeback, and trap controls; MEM/WB will retain\\nonly register-write, result-select, and trap controls. This is an end-to-end\\ndeletion of unused state with no new mux, comparator, interlock, or fetch-PC\\nfeedback and no change to instruction timing, CoreMark cycles, RVFI fields,\\nbus-stall behavior, or nret. Reducing pipeline-register width and control fanout\\nshould improve placement and routed Fmax while also lowering FF and LUT use.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control types, replace the full ctrl_t fields in downstream payloads, and delete unused registered branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM and stop registering branch outcome metadata after redirect, pc_next, and replay qualification have been computed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM control, propagate only retirement-relevant writeback and trap bits into MEM/WB, and preserve all alignment, memory-mask, held-forwarding, and RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, retirement, and trap references for the stage-specific control payloads without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select and qualify register writeback from the compact MEM/WB control payload with behavior identical to the existing full-control path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control types, replace the full ctrl_t fields in downstream payloads, and delete unused registered branch_taken and branch_target fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pack only memory, writeback, and trap controls into EX/MEM and stop registering branch outcome metadata after redirect, pc_next, and replay qualification have been computed.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed EX/MEM control, propagate only retirement-relevant writeback and trap bits into MEM/WB, and preserve all alignment, memory-mask, held-forwarding, and RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, retirement, and trap references for the stage-specific control payloads without changing the nret=1 interface or pipeline timing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select and qualify register writeback from the compact MEM/WB control payload with behavior identical to the existing full-control path.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 436.99, \"delta_pct\": -5.79, \"vs_baseline\": 54.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 185.56, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10185, \"ff\": 1971, \"seeds\": [179.92, 185.56, 198.93], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:52:35.726601Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9}\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Two-strike replay-entry admission\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The exact-target one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\\nthat has improved fitness, but its replace-on-every-miss policy lets a single\\ncold taken backward branch evict a frequently reused loop-head instruction.\\nAdding capacity or another identity tag has already imposed much more FPGA\\ntiming cost than CoreMark recovered, so replacement policy is the remaining\\nway to improve effective coverage without widening the replay datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The exact-target one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\\nthat has improved fitness, but its replace-on-every-miss policy lets a single\\ncold taken backward branch evict a frequently reused loop-head instruction.\\nAdding capacity or another identity tag has already imposed much more FPGA\\ntiming cost than CoreMark recovered, so replacement policy is the remaining\\nway to improve effective coverage without widening the replay datapath.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one miss-history bit implementing two-strike admission for a valid replay\\nentry. A confirmed eligible miss immediately after a replay hit preserves the\\ncurrent entry and merely arms replacement; a second eligible miss before the\\nnext hit may start the existing accepted-fetch fill and replace the entry. Any\\nreplay hit clears the armed bit, while an invalid entry still fills on its\\nfirst eligible miss so cold-start behavior remains fast.\\n\\nThis policy should keep a hot CoreMark loop head resident across isolated\\ntaken branches and reduce destructive one-entry thrashing. It adds no target\\nstate, comparator, instruction mux, replay phase, or logic on io_imemAddr; the\\nreplay hit equation and all pipeline, stall, trap, RVFI, and nret=1 behavior\\nremain unchanged. If execution moves to a new phase, two eligible misses are\\nsufficient to admit its target, bounding the loss from stale-entry retention.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single replacement-armed bit and two-strike admission policy around the existing miss-to-fill state update, with immediate first fill when the entry is invalid and hit-based re-protection, while leaving the exact-target replay hit path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for cold first-miss fill, preservation after one isolated conflicting miss, replacement after two misses without an intervening hit, hit-based re-protection, and identical behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single replacement-armed bit and two-strike admission policy around the existing miss-to-fill state update, with immediate first fill when the entry is invalid and hit-based re-protection, while leaving the exact-target replay hit path unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for cold first-miss fill, preservation after one isolated conflicting miss, replacement after two misses without an intervening hit, hit-based re-protection, and identical behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 322.0, \"delta_pct\": -30.58, \"vs_baseline\": 13.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 136.43, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4236949, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10211, \"ff\": 1961, \"seeds\": [119.22, 157.06, 136.43], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T17:50:50.397008Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Valid-only reset for pipeline payload banks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB synchronously clear every bit of their wide packed\\npayloads even though invalid entries are architecturally ignored. This puts\\nreset and flush selection on hundreds of PC, instruction, operand, result,\\nand RVFI-data flops, increasing global control fanout and placement pressure\\nin a design whose fitness is now dominated by routed Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB synchronously clear every bit of their wide packed\\npayloads even though invalid entries are architecturally ignored. This puts\\nreset and flush selection on hundreds of PC, instruction, operand, result,\\nand RVFI-data flops, increasing global control fanout and placement pressure\\nin a design whose fitness is now dominated by routed Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split each pipeline register into a small validity/control bank and a wide\\ndata bank. Reset and bubble flushes clear only valid plus the control bits\\nneeded to suppress register writes, memory requests, forwarding, and traps;\\ndata fields retain or capture don't-care values while invalid and never\\nacquire a reset/flush mux. Keep the existing stall and MEM/WB held-forwarding\\nbehavior exactly intact. Unlike the failed register-file valid-mask attempt,\\nthis adds no lookup or select to an active operand path: validity already\\nqualifies retirement and side effects. Removing reset/flush fanout from the\\nwide payload flops should improve placement and median Fmax without changing\\nCoreMark cycles, pipeline latency, bus handshakes, RVFI semantics, or nret.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor ID/EX into separately clocked control-valid and data banks so reset and flush invalidate the entry without clearing PC, instruction, immediate, register tags, or operand payloads; preserve replay and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor EX/MEM so reset clears valid and side-effect controls only, while the wide execute and RVFI payload bank has no reset mux and continues to hold on data-memory stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor MEM/WB into valid, control, and payload banks, retaining the existing hold behavior needed for forwarding while removing reset from wide result, memory, instruction, PC, and operand fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_reset.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed reset, redirect-flush, load-use bubble, replay, and data-stall checks proving invalid payload values cannot retire, write registers, issue memory requests, perturb forwarding, or violate RVFI once a valid instruction arrives.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor ID/EX into separately clocked control-valid and data banks so reset and flush invalidate the entry without clearing PC, instruction, immediate, register tags, or operand payloads; preserve replay and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor EX/MEM so reset clears valid and side-effect controls only, while the wide execute and RVFI payload bank has no reset mux and continues to hold on data-memory stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor MEM/WB into valid, control, and payload banks, retaining the existing hold behavior needed for forwarding while removing reset from wide result, memory, instruction, PC, and operand fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline_reset.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed reset, redirect-flush, load-use bubble, replay, and data-stall checks proving invalid payload values cannot retire, write registers, issue memory requests, perturb forwarding, or violate RVFI once a valid instruction arrives.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 402.27, \"delta_pct\": -13.28, \"vs_baseline\": 42.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 170.82, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10010, \"ff\": 1957, \"seeds\": [170.82, 170.56, 173.25], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:16:52.246722Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -13.28%): Splitting packed pipeline registers into separate valid/control and unreset payload always_ff banks can cost more in FPGA register-control packing and routing than it saves in reset fanout, so valid-only reset is not automatically a timing win for wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB payloads.\"}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed shared PC-relative target adder\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken conditional branch currently closes a 32-bit path from the ID/EX\\nPC and immediate through an EX-stage target adder and redirect selection back\\nto the fetch PC. CoreMark repeatedly exercises backward branches, while prior\\nresults show that adding prediction or buffering to the fetch path sacrifices\\ntoo much routed Fmax. The ID stage already has both the instruction PC and its\\ndecoded immediate before the ID/EX boundary, so this addition need not remain\\non the branch-resolution feedback cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one shared PC-plus-immediate value combinationally in ID and register\\nit alongside each instruction in ID/EX. Use that registered value in EX for\\nconditional-branch targets, direct-JAL targets, and AUIPC results; retain the\\nexisting forwarded rs1-plus-immediate adder only for JALR. This retimes the\\nwide PC-relative addition across the ID/EX boundary and removes it from the\\nhigh-frequency EX-to-fetch redirect path without changing branch latency,\\nprediction policy, instruction count, or any bus handshake.\\n\\nThe registered sum must follow the instruction through every existing hold,\\nflush, replay, and reset case. EX remains authoritative for branch condition,\\nJALR bit-zero clearing, target-alignment traps, redirect qualification, and\\nRVFI pc_wdata. Sharing the retimed value with AUIPC also prevents synthesis\\nfrom retaining a redundant PC-relative adder. The expected result is higher\\nmedian Fmax at unchanged CoreMark cycles, for a net fitness improvement with\\nonly 32 additional pipeline bits and no new global control or fetch-PC mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered PC-relative sum to the ID/EX payload so the value remains aligned with its instruction across stalls, flushes, and replay injection.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Form one selected-PC-plus-immediate sum and capture it in every normal and replay ID/EX update while preserving the existing reset, hold, and bubble semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the registered sum for branch and direct-JAL targets and AUIPC writeback, leaving only JALR's operand-dependent addition in EX and preserving redirect, alignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependency- and stall-aware checks for taken and untaken branches, JAL, JALR, AUIPC, replay, target misalignment, and RVFI next-PC behavior after the PC-relative adder retime.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 422.03, \"delta_pct\": -9.02, \"vs_baseline\": 49.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 179.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4246355, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10363, \"ff\": 1990, \"seeds\": [190.22, 179.21, 153.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:19:59.820672Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -9.02%): Retiming the shared 32-bit PC-plus-immediate adder from EX into combinational ID can merely trade the redirect cone for a decode-to-ID/EX path plus 32 routed payload bits, so PC-relative target precomputation is not a timing win unless the added decode and pipeline-routing cost is cheaper.\"}\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Direction-agnostic confirmed branch replay\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The exact-target, one-instruction confirmed replay buffer is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but it currently excludes every taken\\nforward conditional branch and spends a full-width comparison deciding whether\\nthe resolved target is below the branch PC. CoreMark repeats forward as well as\\nbackward control decisions inside loops, so this restriction leaves useful\\nreplay opportunities uncovered while retaining direction logic in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The exact-target, one-instruction confirmed replay buffer is the only predictor\\nstructure that has improved this core, but it currently excludes every taken\\nforward conditional branch and spends a full-width comparison deciding whether\\nthe resolved target is below the branch PC. CoreMark repeats forward as well as\\nbackward control decisions inside loops, so this restriction leaves useful\\nreplay opportunities uncovered while retaining direction logic in EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Admit every valid, legal, aligned, taken conditional branch to the existing\\nexact-target replay buffer, irrespective of displacement direction. A matching\\nentry will replay the cached target instruction only after EX confirms the\\nbranch is taken, exactly as today; misses will use the unchanged accepted-fetch\\nfill protocol. Remove the branch_target < branch_pc comparison and replace the\\nresolved_taken_backward interface with a simple resolved_taken qualification.\\n\\nThis broadens dynamic coverage and deletes a 32-bit magnitude comparator without\\nadding tags, entries, history bits, instruction mux levels, or any selection on\\nio_imemAddr. EX remains authoritative, so not-taken branches, illegal encodings,\\nmisaligned targets, redirects, bus stalls, RVFI ordering, and retirement behavior\\nremain unchanged. The expected gain comes from both fewer repeated forward-branch\\nbubbles and reduced execute-to-replay timing and routing cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace backward-only replay eligibility with a valid legal aligned taken-conditional-branch signal, eliminating the full-width target-versus-PC direction comparison while leaving redirect and trap resolution authoritative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the direction-agnostic confirmed-taken signal from EX to the existing replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface, hazard priorities, or fetch-address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow the unchanged exact-target hit and accepted-fetch fill protocol to operate for taken forward and backward conditional branches, with no added predictor state or datapath width.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for repeated taken forward branches, alternating taken and not-taken outcomes, exact-target conflicts, misaligned targets, and replay behavior across imem and dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace backward-only replay eligibility with a valid legal aligned taken-conditional-branch signal, eliminating the full-width target-versus-PC direction comparison while leaving redirect and trap resolution authoritative.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the direction-agnostic confirmed-taken signal from EX to the existing replay buffer without changing the nret=1 interface, hazard priorities, or fetch-address path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow the unchanged exact-target hit and accepted-fetch fill protocol to operate for taken forward and backward conditional branches, with no added predictor state or datapath width.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed tests for repeated taken forward branches, alternating taken and not-taken outcomes, exact-target conflicts, misaligned targets, and replay behavior across imem and dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 191.82, \"delta_pct\": -58.65, \"vs_baseline\": -32.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 81.65, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4256598, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10714, \"ff\": 1955, \"seeds\": [88.79, 81.17, 81.65], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T18:14:04.794042Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -58.65%): Admitting taken forward branches to a one-entry exact-target replay buffer makes every confirmed miss replace its pending or hot target, so CoreMark's forward control flow can thrash the high-value backward-loop entry despite removing the EX direction comparator.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"The history confirms that only confirmed, post-EX instruction replay has helped; speculative fetch-address steering and extra replacement/tag policy have been costly. I’m narrowing the next idea to a minimal change on the existing replay mechanism."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct|tostring),(.fitness|tostring),(.fmax_mhz|tostring),(.cycles|tostring),(.lut4|tostring),(.ff|tostring),(.lesson // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && sed -n '1,220p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json && find cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses -maxdepth 1 -type f | sort | tail -n 8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct|tostring),(.fitness|tostring),(.fmax_mhz|tostring),(.cycles|tostring),(.lut4|tostring),(.ff|tostring),(.lesson // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && sed -n '1,220p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json && find cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses -maxdepth 1 -type f | sort | tail -n 8\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product across RV32M MUL variants and correcting only the signed high halves avoids parallel inferred multipliers, substantially reducing multiplier fabric and routing pressure without adding pipeline latency.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.76%): Combinational IF opcode/immediate decode, parallel B/J target adders, and a 32-bit predicted-next-PC path can cost more FPGA Fmax than CoreMark recovers from avoided control bubbles, so prediction must stay off the fetch PC feedback cone.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (regression, -75.51%): Unrolling a 32-step restoring divider in always_comb creates a deep compare-subtract recurrence that overwhelms any area benefit from sharing DIV/REM arithmetic, so divider reuse must be iterative or pipelined rather than fully combinational.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (regression, -26.08%): Feeding an iterative divider's div_wait into centralized hazard control makes rare DIV/REM operations add a high-fanout PC/IF-ID/ID-EX stall path to every cycle, so multi-cycle-unit interlocks must be localized or completion-decoupled to preserve FPGA Fmax.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -25.45%): Forwarding raw io_dmemRData through the EX/MEM operand mux into the following instruction's ALU trades a load-use bubble for a dmem-read-to-EX combinational path whose FPGA timing cost outweighs the CoreMark CPI gain.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (improvement, +0.45%): Replaying a cached target instruction only after EX confirms a repeated taken backward branch can recover CoreMark loop bubbles without putting target selection on io_imemAddr, though the small gain shows this narrow one-entry opportunity has limited coverage.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (improvement, +6.22%): Replacing variable subword shifts with explicit byte/halfword lane muxes and constant store-mask decoding reduces FPGA LUT depth on the dmem-to-MEM/WB path while preserving load latency and RVFI memory semantics.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Muxing buffered versus live address, data, and byte enables onto the dmem port can break block-RAM inference for the 8 KiB wrapper memory, expanding it into 1,024 RAM16SDP4 cells and exceeding the FPGA's 648-cell placement capacity.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.07%): Adding a second 64-bit branch-PC/target tag and parallel candidate equality to a one-entry replay buffer can collapse routed FPGA Fmax even off io_imemAddr, so narrow CoreMark replacement coverage cannot repay wide tag-comparison and routing cost.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r5s0 (regression, -22.71%): Replacing register-file data reset with an address-indexed valid mask adds a valid-bit lookup and zero-select mux to both asynchronous read paths, so reset-routing savings do not pay when register-read timing is the stronger FPGA constraint.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r5s1 (regression, -23.48%): Even a registered late load-to-store bypass puts a MEM/WB tag compare and writeback-data mux directly on the dmem write-data path, so removing CoreMark's rs2-only load-use bubbles can lose more FPGA Fmax than it recovers in IPC.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r6s0 (regression, -7.66%): Manually sharing SLL/SRL/SRA as a five-level right-shift mux chain with op-dependent bit reversal and fill can constrain synthesis into extra selection and routing that is slower than native variable-shift inference, so explicit operator sharing must be validated against the FPGA mapper's existing optimization.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Falling-edge register-file writeback is half a cycle too late for an ID instruction that captures operands on the same rising edge its producer reaches MEM/WB, so removing the WB-to-ID write-first bypass requires an interlock or an earlier architectural write point.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -3.58%): Compressing exact replay-buffer PCs to PC[19:2] can still regress when local upper-bit and alignment guards replace the removed 32-bit equality work, so range compression only pays if address validity is enforced once upstream rather than requalified on the hit/fill paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering the IF/ID instruction response is an elastic-buffer protocol change, not a pure retime; request-PC advance, response valid, decode consume/refill, and redirect/replay invalidation must be tracked independently or instructions can be dropped or stranded, as the liveness, PC-forwarding, and register-state checks expose.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.25%): Extending a confirmed one-word replay entry to target+4 can regress badly even with shared tags, because the phase-controlled 32-bit instruction/PC selection and stall-safe consume/cancel control burden decode and hazard paths more than hiding occasional instruction-memory backpressure repays.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (improvement, +7.40%): A confirmed backward-branch replay entry is safely keyed by its exact target alone, so dropping the redundant branch-PC tag removes 32-bit comparator, flop, and routing cost while allowing branches that share a loop head to reuse the cached instruction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.27%): Registering divide mode is not true completion decoupling when it still drives PC and ID/EX holds and gates branch redirect and EX/MEM control, because rare DIV/REM state then burdens ordinary-cycle clock-enable and execute timing cones.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (broken, n/a): Direct combinational commit from EX/MEM makes post-edge RVFI observe a newly advanced load with stale io_dmemRData from the prior address, so load retirement still needs a response/commit holding boundary even if the full MEM/WB payload is removed.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -13.28%): Splitting packed pipeline registers into separate valid/control and unreset payload always_ff banks can cost more in FPGA register-control packing and routing than it saves in reset fanout, so valid-only reset is not automatically a timing win for wide ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB payloads.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -9.02%): Retiming the shared 32-bit PC-plus-immediate adder from EX into combinational ID can merely trade the redirect cone for a decode-to-ID/EX path plus 32 routed payload bits, so PC-relative target precomputation is not a timing win unless the added decode and pipeline-routing cost is cheaper.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -58.65%): Admitting taken forward branches to a one-entry exact-target replay buffer makes every confirmed miss replace its pending or hot target, so CoreMark's forward control flow can thrash the high-value backward-loop entry despite removing the EX direction comparator.\nhyp-20260711-003-r1s2\tStatic backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor\tregression\t8.76\t307.6\t131.03\t4259699\t10330\t1895\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.76%): Combinational IF opcode/immediate decode, parallel B/J target adders, and a 32-bit predicted-next-PC path can cost more FPGA Fmax than CoreMark recovers from avoided control bubbles, so prediction must stay off the fetch PC feedback cone.\nhyp-20260711-003-r2s2\tEX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\tregression\t-45.16\t221.94\t93.97\t4234052\t13089\t2982\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\nhyp-20260711-003-r3s2\tOne-entry taken-target instruction replay buffer\timprovement\t0.45\t406.58\t172.65\t4246355\t10508\t2026\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (improvement, +0.45%): Replaying a cached target instruction only after EX confirms a repeated taken backward branch can recover CoreMark loop bubbles without putting target selection on io_imemAddr, though the small gain shows this narrow one-entry opportunity has limited coverage.\nhyp-20260711-003-r4s2\tHysteretic replacement for the loop replay entry\tregression\t-22.07\t316.84\t134.28\t4238065\t10427\t2088\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.07%): Adding a second 64-bit branch-PC/target tag and parallel candidate equality to a one-entry replay buffer can collapse routed FPGA Fmax even off io_imemAddr, so narrow CoreMark replacement coverage cannot repay wide tag-comparison and routing cost.\nhyp-20260711-003-r5s2\tBranch-PC-only replay-buffer tag\tregression\t-6.2\t405.12\t172.03\t4246355\t10170\t2028\t\nhyp-20260711-003-r6s2\tRange-compressed replay-buffer PC tags\tregression\t-3.58\t416.43\t176.83\t4246355\t10689\t1997\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -3.58%): Compressing exact replay-buffer PCs to PC[19:2] can still regress when local upper-bit and alignment guards replace the removed 32-bit equality work, so range compression only pays if address validity is enforced once upstream rather than requalified on the hit/fill paths.\nhyp-20260711-003-r7s2\tTwo-instruction confirmed loop-head replay line\tregression\t-34.25\t283.98\t119.08\t4193281\t11070\t2115\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.25%): Extending a confirmed one-word replay entry to target+4 can regress badly even with shared tags, because the phase-controlled 32-bit instruction/PC selection and stall-safe consume/cancel control burden decode and hazard paths more than hiding occasional instruction-memory backpressure repays.\nhyp-20260711-003-r8s2\tB-immediate tag for confirmed branch replay\tregression\t4.24\t450.2\t191.17\t4246355\t10248\t2052\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\nhyp-20260711-003-r9s2\tTwo-strike replay-entry admission\tregression\t-30.58\t322.0\t136.43\t4236949\t10211\t1961\t\nhyp-20260711-003-r10s2\tDirection-agnostic confirmed branch replay\tregression\t-58.65\t191.82\t81.65\t4256598\t10714\t1955\t- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -58.65%): Admitting taken forward branches to a one-entry exact-target replay buffer makes every confirmed miss replace its pending or hot target, so CoreMark's forward control flow can thrash the high-value backward-loop entry despite removing the EX direction comparator.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260711-003-r9s2.last\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260711-003-r9s2.log\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260711-004-r11s0.log\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260711-005-r11s1.log\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260711-006-r11s2.log\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s0.yaml\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s1.yaml\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && for f in cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-00{1,2,3}-r11s{0,1,2}.yaml; do echo \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,220p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done && jq -r '\"'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\"+.id+\\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\"+.title+\\\"\\\\nHYP:\\\\n\\\"+.hypothesis.hypothesis+\\\"\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\"+(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && for f in cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-00{1,2,3}-r11s{0,1,2}.yaml; do echo \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,220p' \\\"\"'$f\"; done && jq -r '\"'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | \\\"ID: \\\"+.id+\\\"\\\\nTITLE: \\\"+.title+\\\"\\\\nHYP:\\\\n\\\"+.hypothesis.hypothesis+\\\"\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\"+(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")+\\\"\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s0.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-001-r11s0\ntitle: \"Fold replay refill target into the resident tag\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The confirmed branch replay buffer stores the same 32-bit target twice while\n  a miss is being filled: once as the resident entry target and once as a\n  separate pending-fill target. The in-order redirect prevents the old resident\n  entry from being usefully hit before the redirected target fetch is accepted,\n  so retaining both target banks adds flops, routing, and update muxing without\n  preserving exploitable parallelism.\nhypothesis: |\n  On a confirmed eligible replay miss, immediately invalidate the resident\n  instruction and write the resolved target into the existing entry-target\n  register while setting fill_pending. Match the accepted redirected fetch\n  directly against that entry target; on a match, capture the instruction and\n  revalidate the entry. This deletes fill_target_pc_q end to end while retaining\n  the exact-target hit equation, backward-only admission, accepted-fetch fill\n  discipline, replay timing, and registered-PC-only instruction address path.\n\n  Give a same-cycle newer miss priority over completion of an older pending fill,\n  so an accepted stale target can never be validated under the newly written tag.\n  Because a miss already redirects and flushes all younger work, invalidating the\n  old entry during refill cannot remove a reachable replay opportunity. Removing\n  32 target flops and their duplicate compare and update routing should improve\n  placement and median Fmax at identical CoreMark cycles and architectural state.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Remove the separate pending-fill target register, reuse the invalid resident target as the pending tag, and define miss-over-fill priority so simultaneous refill completion and replacement cannot associate an instruction with the wrong target.\"\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed checks for cold fill, miss-time invalidation, stalled accepted-fetch matching, and simultaneous old-fill completion plus newer miss replacement while preserving exact-target replay behavior.\"\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s1.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s1.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s2.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-001-r11s2.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s0.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s0.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s1.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Replicated single-read register-file banks\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The reset-valid experiment established that the asynchronous register-file\n  read path is timing-sensitive, and the falling-edge-write experiment showed\n  that its write-first behavior cannot simply be removed. The current 2R1W\n  array makes every stored bit drive both independently addressed read mux\n  trees, creating high fanout and coupled placement on the decode-to-ID/EX\n  path even though FPGA flops are plentiful relative to the fitness benefit\n  available from higher routed Fmax.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the shared two-read-port register array with two physically distinct\n  1R1W copies, one dedicated to rs1 and one to rs2. Broadcast every qualified\n  architectural write and reset to both banks, but build each asynchronous\n  read from only its local bank; preserve the existing per-port x0 handling\n  and same-cycle write-first bypass exactly. Prevent synthesis from merging the\n  copies so each read mux can be placed near its operand capture logic without\n  every register bit feeding both mux networks.\n\n  This deliberately spends roughly one extra 32x32 bank to reduce storage-bit\n  fanout and placement coupling rather than adding any new selection level to\n  the already sensitive read paths. Pipeline latency, CoreMark cycles,\n  forwarding, bus handshakes, reset-visible zero state, RVFI register data, and\n  the nret=1 interface remain unchanged. If decode/register-read routing is a\n  limiting path, the Fmax gain should outweigh the extra flops and write/reset\n  fanout while avoiding all fetch, execute, and memory critical cones.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Implement two explicitly independent 32x32 single-read banks with broadcast synchronous reset and writes, local asynchronous rs1/rs2 reads, x0 suppression, and identical write-first bypass semantics; retain the copies through synthesis.\"\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: \"Add directed and randomized checks that both replicated banks remain coherent across reset, back-to-back writes, simultaneous different-address reads, x0 accesses, and same-cycle write/read collisions on either or both ports.\"\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s2.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-002-r11s2.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s0.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s0.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s1.yaml\nsed: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s1.yaml: No such file or directory\ncores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r11s2.yaml\nid: hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"Fill-once backward-loop replay entry\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The confirmed one-instruction replay buffer is the only predictor structure\n  that has improved fitness, but its replace-on-miss policy lets later taken\n  branches dislodge the loop head that earned the entry. Recent admission and\n  forward-coverage experiments show that extra policy state or broader fills\n  cost substantial Fmax and can amplify this thrashing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Make the replay entry fill only while invalid and keep that first captured\n  backward-loop target instruction resident until reset. Once valid, an exact\n  target hit continues to replay normally, while every nonmatching backward\n  branch follows the existing redirect path without starting or replacing a\n  pending fill. This removes steady-state entry writes and destructive\n  replacement without adding counters, tags, muxes, or fetch-address logic.\n\n  CoreMark repeatedly executes its earliest hot initialization and benchmark\n  loops, so preserving the first eligible loop head should provide stable reuse\n  and avoid the phase-to-phase or nested-loop churn seen with replace-on-miss.\n  Cold fill remains qualified by accepted fetch, and EX remains authoritative\n  for direction, legality, alignment, redirect, stalls, and RVFI behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 2\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_target_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Permit miss-triggered pending fill only while the replay entry is invalid, then keep the captured exact-target instruction resident until reset while leaving the existing confirmed-hit path unchanged.\"\n  - file: test/test_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed checks for cold accepted-fetch fill, repeated exact-target hits, immunity to later conflicting backward-branch misses, reset replacement, and unchanged behavior under instruction and data bus stalls.\"\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\nTITLE: Static backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor\nHYP:\nPredict legal backward conditional branches taken and all word-aligned direct\nJAL targets in IF, while predicting forward branches and JALR fall-through.\nCarry the predicted next PC with the instruction to EX and redirect only when\nthe resolved architectural next PC differs from that prediction. Correct loop\nand direct-call predictions therefore avoid the existing two-younger-instruction\nflush, while wrong predictions recover through the existing redirect path.\n\nKeep prediction combinational and stateless: recognize the B-type opcode plus\nlegal funct3 and use the immediate sign bit for backward-taken, recognize JAL,\nform each target directly from instruction bits, and require word alignment.\nEX remains authoritative for branch conditions, JALR targets, misalignment\ntraps, and RVFI pc_wdata. Redirect must retain priority over all bus stalls, and\nprediction metadata must remain attached to a held instruction so random imem\nand dmem backpressure cannot cause stale or duplicate execution.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\nTITLE: EX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\nHYP:\nAdd a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\n\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r3s2\nTITLE: One-entry taken-target instruction replay buffer\nHYP:\nAdd a one-entry branch target instruction buffer specialized for a repeatedly\ntaken backward conditional branch. On the first taken instance, use the\nexisting EX redirect normally and capture the target instruction when that\nredirected fetch is accepted. On a later taken instance of the same branch,\nEX already knows the architectural outcome; replay the registered target\ninstruction directly into ID/EX and redirect the registered fetch PC to the\ncached target-plus-four address. This replaces the two wrong-path bubbles with\nuseful target execution for the hottest loop while making no speculative\nchange to io_imemAddr and adding no lookup to the sequential PC feedback path.\n\nThe entry holds branch-PC and target tags, the target instruction, and the\ntarget-plus-four address. Fill, replay, and pending-fill state must advance\nonly on accepted instruction fetches and must remain aligned across imem and\ndmem stalls. Nonmatching branches, forward branches, jumps, illegal or\nmisaligned controls, and not-taken outcomes retain the current behavior.\nEX remains authoritative for direction, traps, RVFI pc_wdata, and recovery,\nso this optimization cannot execute an unconfirmed target instruction.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\nTITLE: Hysteretic replacement for the loop replay entry\nHYP:\nProtect the valid replay entry with a one-candidate, two-strike replacement\nfilter. A miss by a new backward branch records only its branch PC and target;\na second miss by that same challenger authorizes the existing accepted-target\nfill and replaces the resident instruction. A hit clears the challenger so a\nfrequently reused resident entry is not displaced by isolated or alternating\nmisses. Initial fill remains immediate when no valid resident exists.\n\nThis adds only a candidate tag and a small equality test in the EX-confirmed\nupdate path. It neither widens the replay payload nor changes io_imemAddr,\nsequential PC feedback, branch resolution, or RVFI behavior. By eliminating\ndestructive one-miss thrashing, the same single cached target instruction\nshould cover more CoreMark loop iterations with negligible routed-Fmax cost.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r5s2\nTITLE: Branch-PC-only replay-buffer tag\nHYP:\nMatch the replay entry using only the full resolved branch PC. Keep the cached\ntarget PC and target instruction for replay and retain the existing accepted-\nfetch fill discipline, but remove target equality from hit detection. For an\nRV32 conditional branch, identical branch PC implies the same instruction and\ntherefore the same PC-relative target in the immutable benchmark instruction\nmemory, so this does not introduce aliasing or change architectural behavior.\n\nRemoving one 32-bit comparator and its reduction logic should shorten and\nlighten the confirmed-replay path while preserving the current CoreMark cycle\nsavings, the registered-PC-only imem address path, all stall behavior, and\nRVFI semantics. This deliberately improves the proven one-entry mechanism\ninstead of adding another tag, speculative fetch mux, or PC feedback path.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r6s2\nTITLE: Range-compressed replay-buffer PC tags\nHYP:\nStore and compare only PC[19:2] for the resident branch tag, resident target,\npending branch tag, and pending target. Reconstruct replay PCs as\n{12'b0, tag, 2'b00}; alignment and the enforced instruction-address range make\nthis representation exact rather than probabilistic. Keep both branch-PC and\ntarget equality, the existing accepted-fetch fill protocol, and the registered\nPC-only io_imemAddr path unchanged.\n\nNarrowing four address registers and both hit/fill comparisons should reduce\nflip-flop count, equality-reduction depth, and local routing while preserving\nthe replay buffer's measured CoreMark cycle savings. This avoids the failed\napproaches of adding entries, removing an exact comparison, or introducing a\nspeculative target mux on the instruction-fetch timing cone.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\nTITLE: Two-instruction confirmed loop-head replay line\nHYP:\nTurn the existing entry into a two-instruction loop-head replay line sharing\nthe same exact branch-PC and target tags. During a cold fill, capture both the\ntarget and target+4 instructions on their respective accepted fetches. On an\nEX-confirmed hit, inject the target instruction immediately as today, queue\nthe cached target+4 instruction for the following accepted ID slot, and move\nthe registered fetch PC directly to target+8. The queued second instruction\ntherefore hides an instruction-bus stall at target+4 and extends the useful\nreplay burst without adding any speculative lookup or mux to io_imemAddr.\n\nUse an explicit valid/consume handshake for the second replay beat so dmem\nstalls and load-use hazards hold it without loss or duplication. Hazard\nsource-address checks must select the pending replay instruction rather than\nthe simultaneously visible live fetch. Entry validity is published only\nafter both words have been accepted, and all legal/aligned/backward-branch,\nexact-tag, RVFI, redirect, and retirement behavior remains unchanged. This\nadds one 32-bit instruction register and a small replay-phase bit, but no\nsecond tag, target adder, or parallel equality tree, so it should preserve\nrouted Fmax while reducing CoreMark cycles under the scored stall model.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\nTITLE: B-immediate tag for confirmed branch replay\nHYP:\nReplace the replay entry's 32-bit target tag comparison with an exact comparison of\nthe branch instruction's B-immediate bits [12:1]. EX will export these bits directly\nfrom the already-latched decoded immediate, and the replay buffer will store them\nwhen a confirmed miss begins a fill. A hit will require the unchanged full branch-PC\nmatch plus the 12-bit displacement match, while the stored full target PC remains\navailable solely to label the cached instruction and advance fetch to target+4.\n\nThis is an exact tag transformation for legal conditional branches: branch PC and\nB-immediate uniquely determine the resolved target, including backward offsets, so\nhit behavior, CoreMark cycles, redirects, stalls, RVFI state, and illegal/misaligned\nhandling remain unchanged. Removing 20 comparator bits and their routed resolved-\ntarget inputs from the replay-hit cone should raise FPGA Fmax without adding fetch-PC\nfeedback logic, replay phases, range guards, or another entry.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\nTITLE: Two-strike replay-entry admission\nHYP:\nAdd one miss-history bit implementing two-strike admission for a valid replay\nentry. A confirmed eligible miss immediately after a replay hit preserves the\ncurrent entry and merely arms replacement; a second eligible miss before the\nnext hit may start the existing accepted-fetch fill and replace the entry. Any\nreplay hit clears the armed bit, while an invalid entry still fills on its\nfirst eligible miss so cold-start behavior remains fast.\n\nThis policy should keep a hot CoreMark loop head resident across isolated\ntaken branches and reduce destructive one-entry thrashing. It adds no target\nstate, comparator, instruction mux, replay phase, or logic on io_imemAddr; the\nreplay hit equation and all pipeline, stall, trap, RVFI, and nret=1 behavior\nremain unchanged. If execution moves to a new phase, two eligible misses are\nsufficient to admit its target, bounding the loss from stale-entry retention.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\nID: hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\nTITLE: Direction-agnostic confirmed branch replay\nHYP:\nAdmit every valid, legal, aligned, taken conditional branch to the existing\nexact-target replay buffer, irrespective of displacement direction. A matching\nentry will replay the cached target instruction only after EX confirms the\nbranch is taken, exactly as today; misses will use the unchanged accepted-fetch\nfill protocol. Remove the branch_target < branch_pc comparison and replace the\nresolved_taken_backward interface with a simple resolved_taken qualification.\n\nThis broadens dynamic coverage and deletes a 32-bit magnitude comparator without\nadding tags, entries, history bits, instruction mux levels, or any selection on\nio_imemAddr. EX remains authoritative, so not-taken branches, illegal encodings,\nmisaligned targets, redirects, bus stalls, RVFI ordering, and retirement behavior\nremain unchanged. The expected gain comes from both fewer repeated forward-branch\nbubbles and reduced execute-to-replay timing and routing cost.\n\nNOTES:\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The strongest new angle is an exact-behavior simplification: fold the pending refill target into the invalidated resident tag. It preserves the measured replay cycle benefit while removing a duplicated 32-bit state bank and comparison/update routing, with no new prediction policy or fetch-path logic."}}
