{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'predictor|replay|BTB|branch|hyp-20260711-006-r13s2' cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 120\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:5:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:7:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (improvement, +0.45%): Replaying a cached target instruction only after EX confirms a repeated taken backward branch can recover CoreMark loop bubbles without putting target selection on io_imemAddr, though the small gain shows this narrow one-entry opportunity has limited coverage.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:10:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.07%): Adding a second 64-bit branch-PC/target tag and parallel candidate equality to a one-entry replay buffer can collapse routed FPGA Fmax even off io_imemAddr, so narrow CoreMark replacement coverage cannot repay wide tag-comparison and routing cost.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r6s2 (regression, -3.58%): Compressing exact replay-buffer PCs to PC[19:2] can still regress when local upper-bit and alignment guards replace the removed 32-bit equality work, so range compression only pays if address validity is enforced once upstream rather than requalified on the hit/fill paths.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:16:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Registering the IF/ID instruction response is an elastic-buffer protocol change, not a pure retime; request-PC advance, response valid, decode consume/refill, and redirect/replay invalidation must be tracked independently or instructions can be dropped or stranded, as the liveness, PC-forwarding, and register-state checks expose.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:17:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -34.25%): Extending a confirmed one-word replay entry to target+4 can regress badly even with shared tags, because the phase-controlled 32-bit instruction/PC selection and stall-safe consume/cancel control burden decode and hazard paths more than hiding occasional instruction-memory backpressure repays.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:18:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r8s0 (improvement, +7.40%): A confirmed backward-branch replay entry is safely keyed by its exact target alone, so dropping the redundant branch-PC tag removes 32-bit comparator, flop, and routing cost while allowing branches that share a loop head to reuse the cached instruction.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:19:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -10.27%): Registering divide mode is not true completion decoupling when it still drives PC and ID/EX holds and gates branch redirect and EX/MEM control, because rare DIV/REM state then burdens ordinary-cycle clock-enable and execute timing cones.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:20:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r8s2 (regression, +4.24%): Replacing a replay buffer's resolved-target equality with a B-immediate tag can regress despite the narrower comparator because it adds EX-to-buffer routing and duplicate tag state while the full target must still be retained, so tag compression only pays when it removes information end to end.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:24:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -58.65%): Admitting taken forward branches to a one-entry exact-target replay buffer makes every confirmed miss replace its pending or hot target, so CoreMark's forward control flow can thrash the high-value backward-loop entry despite removing the EX direction comparator.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:27:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r12s0 (regression, -7.64%): Narrowing replay targets to PC[19:2] still loses when the required branch_target[31:20] range qualification is inserted into EX branch-valid control, so moving address guards upstream can trade comparator width for timing pressure on a more critical branch-resolution cone.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:28:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-004-r13s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use detection must inspect the replay-selected decode instruction rather than the live IF/ID opcode, or replay can make the qualifier disagree with the consumed rs2 and allow stale register data into execution.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:30:- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-006-r13s2 (improvement, +0.87%): When redirect flushing guarantees the first fetch_accept after a replay miss is the saved target, using that protocol invariant instead of rechecking fetch_pc removes a redundant 32-bit fill comparator and modestly improves FPGA timing without weakening the resident exact-target tag.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share one multiplier across all RV32M multiply variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three separate 32-by-32 products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one\\nmultiply instruction can execute at a time, so the duplicated multiplier fabric\\nconsumes scarce LUT/DSP and routing resources without increasing throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one 64-bit unsigned product for every MUL-family operation and derive the\\nsigned high halves with 32-bit correction subtractors: subtract b from the high\\nhalf when a is negative, and subtract a as well when b is negative. MUL continues\\nto use the common low half. This identity is exact modulo 2^64 and should remove\\ntwo large parallel multipliers, lowering LUT pressure and improving routed Fmax\\nwithout changing pipeline latency, CoreMark IPC, or the existing ALTOPS behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 64-bit product and compact signed-high correction logic for MULH and MULHSU, while retaining all RV32M corner semantics and the formal ALTOPS branch.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 404.74, \"delta_pct\": 43.11, \"vs_baseline\": 43.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10073, \"ff\": 1860, \"seeds\": [181.79, 175.38, 185.53], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T12:55:00.760698Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product across RV32M MUL variants and correcting only the signed high halves avoids parallel inferred multipliers, substantially reducing multiplier fabric and routing pressure without adding pipeline latency.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Static backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR currently redirects only when it reaches EX,\\nflushing the younger fetch/decode work. CoreMark contains loop-closing backward\\nbranches and direct calls, so this repeated control penalty reduces iterations\\nper cycle. A decode-light static predictor can remove most of those bubbles\\nwithout a BHT, BTB, or return stack, which is important because the current core\\nis already close to its LUT target and fitness also depends on FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every taken branch, JAL, and JALR currently redirects only when it reaches EX,\\nflushing the younger fetch/decode work. CoreMark contains loop-closing backward\\nbranches and direct calls, so this repeated control penalty reduces iterations\\nper cycle. A decode-light static predictor can remove most of those bubbles\\nwithout a BHT, BTB, or return stack, which is important because the current core\\nis already close to its LUT target and fitness also depends on FPGA Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predict legal backward conditional branches taken and all word-aligned direct\\nJAL targets in IF, while predicting forward branches and JALR fall-through.\\nCarry the predicted next PC with the instruction to EX and redirect only when\\nthe resolved architectural next PC differs from that prediction. Correct loop\\nand direct-call predictions therefore avoid the existing two-younger-instruction\\nflush, while wrong predictions recover through the existing redirect path.\\n\\nKeep prediction combinational and stateless: recognize the B-type opcode plus\\nlegal funct3 and use the immediate sign bit for backward-taken, recognize JAL,\\nform each target directly from instruction bits, and require word alignment.\\nEX remains authoritative for branch conditions, JALR targets, misalignment\\ntraps, and RVFI pc_wdata. Redirect must retain priority over all bus stalls, and\\nprediction metadata must remain attached to a held instruction so random imem\\nand dmem backpressure cannot cause stale or duplicate execution.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with predicted-next-PC metadata so each instruction carries the exact fetch decision that EX must validate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add decode-light BTFNT conditional-branch and direct-JAL prediction, advance the PC to aligned predicted targets, preserve redirect-over-stall priority, and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX with the same stall and flush behavior as the instruction, PC, operands, and valid bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved architectural next PC against the carried prediction and assert redirect only on mismatch, while preserving JALR recovery and all existing misalignment-trap and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as misprediction recovery rather than every taken control transfer, preserving the existing flush, load-use, and bus-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward taken and not-taken branches, forward branches, direct JAL, JALR fallback, misaligned targets, and recovery coincident with imem or dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with predicted-next-PC metadata so each instruction carries the exact fetch decision that EX must validate.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add decode-light BTFNT conditional-branch and direct-JAL prediction, advance the PC to aligned predicted targets, preserve redirect-over-stall priority, and emit the prediction metadata with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch prediction metadata into ID/EX with the same stall and flush behavior as the instruction, PC, operands, and valid bit.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved architectural next PC against the carried prediction and assert redirect only on mismatch, while preserving JALR recovery and all existing misalignment-trap and RVFI pc_wdata semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat redirect as misprediction recovery rather than every taken control transfer, preserving the existing flush, load-use, and bus-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for backward taken and not-taken branches, forward branches, direct JAL, JALR fallback, misaligned targets, and recovery coincident with imem or dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 307.6, \"delta_pct\": 8.76, \"vs_baseline\": 8.76, \"fmax_mhz\": 131.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10330, \"ff\": 1895, \"seeds\": [114.32, 133.08, 131.03], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T12:58:54.342951Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.76%): Combinational IF opcode/immediate decode, parallel B/J target adders, and a 32-bit predicted-next-PC path can cost more FPGA Fmax than CoreMark recovers from avoided control bubbles, so prediction must stay off the fetch PC feedback cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share unsigned division across RV32M DIV and REM variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU expresses signed and unsigned quotient and remainder operations as\\nseparate wide division and modulo operators. Since only one RV32M operation\\nexecutes at a time, parallel signed and unsigned divider fabrics add LUT and\\nrouting pressure without increasing throughput, much like the duplicated\\nmultipliers removed by the previous winning micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU expresses signed and unsigned quotient and remainder operations as\\nseparate wide division and modulo operators. Since only one RV32M operation\\nexecutes at a time, parallel signed and unsigned divider fabrics add LUT and\\nrouting pressure without increasing throughput, much like the duplicated\\nmultipliers removed by the previous winning micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Convert both operands to unsigned magnitudes, compute one common unsigned\\nquotient and remainder pair, and apply the RV32M sign rules afterward: negate\\nDIV when operand signs differ, negate REM when the dividend is negative, and\\nleave DIVU/REMU unchanged. Preserve the explicit divide-by-zero and signed\\noverflow results, as well as the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch. Sharing\\nthe expensive magnitude arithmetic should reduce divider fabric and routing,\\nimproving routed Fmax without changing instruction latency or CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace separate signed and unsigned DIV/REM expressions with common unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder arithmetic plus compact result sign correction, retaining every RV32M corner case and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed DIV and REM coverage around mixed signs, zero divisors, INT_MIN operands, and the INT_MIN divided by minus one overflow case.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace separate signed and unsigned DIV/REM expressions with common unsigned magnitude quotient/remainder arithmetic plus compact result sign correction, retaining every RV32M corner case and ALTOPS behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend directed DIV and REM coverage around mixed signs, zero divisors, INT_MIN operands, and the INT_MIN divided by minus one overflow case.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 99.12, \"delta_pct\": -75.51, \"vs_baseline\": -64.95, \"fmax_mhz\": 44.52, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7699, \"ff\": 1855, \"seeds\": [44.52, 43.2, 46.97], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:15:57.880711Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (regression, -75.51%): Unrolling a 32-step restoring divider in always_comb creates a deep compare-subtract recurrence that overwhelms any area benefit from sharing DIV/REM arithmetic, so divider reuse must be iterative or pipelined rather than fully combinational.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative shared divider with pipeline interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute ALU currently places four combinational divide and remainder\\nexpressions beside the common one-cycle datapath. On a LUT-based FPGA these\\noperators can create a large, deeply routed structure even though only one\\ninstruction can use it and timed CoreMark is dominated by ordinary integer,\\nload/store, branch, and multiply operations. The failed frontend structures\\nalso show that an improvement should avoid adding logic to the fetch-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU hardware with one shared\\niterative radix-2 quotient/remainder unit. A divide-class instruction starts\\nthe unit from EX, remains resident in ID/EX, and interlocks PC, ID/EX, and\\nEX/MEM until the result is ready; it then advances exactly once through the\\nexisting retirement path. Signed operations use operand magnitudes plus final\\nquotient/remainder sign correction, with explicit divide-by-zero and\\nINT_MIN/-1 handling to retain exact RV32M semantics.\\n\\nOrdinary ALU and multiply instructions must bypass the divider with no new\\nstate or muxing on the fetch PC feedback path. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, keep\\nthe existing one-cycle algebraic stand-ins so the bounded formal checks retain\\ntheir current liveness depth; normal simulation and FPGA synthesis exercise\\nthe iterative engine. Removing inferred combinational division and sharing\\none subtract/shift datapath should reduce LUT and routing pressure enough to\\nraise median Fmax, while the added cycles affect only divide-class dynamic\\ninstructions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative RV32M divider/remainder engine with start, busy, and done control, signed correction, and all mandated divide corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove production combinational divide and remainder operators from the common ALU while preserving the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS results and the unchanged shared multiplier datapath.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start and hold divide-class instructions in EX, select the completed quotient or remainder exactly once, and prevent a partial operation from entering EX/MEM.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy interlocking with explicit priority relative to redirects and memory backpressure so all younger pipeline state is preserved without duplicate retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider hold/completion control through EX and the centralized pipeline hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_divider.py\", \"description\": \"Add cycle-aware directed and randomized checks for all DIV/REM variants, corner semantics, stable pipeline holds, single completion, and interaction with instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 299.19, \"delta_pct\": -26.08, \"vs_baseline\": 5.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5688, \"ff\": 2111, \"seeds\": [140.57, 129.47, 134.39], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:25:06.711843Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r2s1 (regression, -26.08%): Feeding an iterative divider's div_wait into centralized hazard control makes rare DIV/REM operations add a high-fanout PC/IF-ID/ID-EX stall path to every cycle, so multi-cycle-unit interlocks must be localized or completion-decoupled to preserve FPGA Fmax.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"EX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The previous static predictor reduced CoreMark cycles, confirming that control\\nhazards are worth attacking, but its IF opcode decode, immediate generation,\\ntarget adders, and predicted-next-PC mux collapsed routed Fmax. The useful\\npredictor work must therefore be registered and kept out of the fetch PC\\nfeedback cone while preserving the cycle reduction on frequently taken loops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\\n\\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped tagged BTB with two-bit counters, registered lookup output, EX-resolution training, and explicit hold/flush controls matching ID/EX under hazards and bus stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the BTB, align its registered prediction with the EX instruction, and route resolved control-flow information back for training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Allow an EX-aligned predicted-taken hit to override the current imem address and emitted fetch PC, advance the sequential PC from that selected fetch address, and retain authoritative redirect-over-stall recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the aligned BTB prediction against the resolved next PC, redirect and flush only on misprediction, and expose legal resolved branch/JAL/JALR outcome and target data for predictor training without changing RVFI semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Apply existing redirect flushing only to predictor recovery and provide predictor hold/flush behavior identical to the ID/EX instruction across load-use, imem, dmem, and redirect cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_predictor.py\", \"description\": \"Check BTB cold misses, counter training, taken-loop hits, target replacement, not-taken recovery, JAL/JALR behavior, alias tags, and prediction alignment during random instruction/data bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 221.94, \"delta_pct\": -45.16, \"vs_baseline\": -21.53, \"fmax_mhz\": 93.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4234052, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 13089, \"ff\": 2982, \"seeds\": [93.97, 105.74, 93.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T13:23:20.366277Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -45.16%): Registering a BTB lookup does not protect FPGA Fmax when its EX-aligned 32-bit target still muxes directly onto imem_addr, so prediction must decouple or pipeline the instruction-memory request path as well as avoid the PC feedback cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Gate load-use hazards by actual source operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a pending load destination against both raw\\nsource-register bitfields of every following instruction. Several RV32IM\\nformats do not read one or either of those fields, so immediate bits or a\\ndestination field can accidentally match the load destination and insert a\\nneedless pipeline bubble. CoreMark contains frequent loads followed by\\nimmediate arithmetic, further loads, and control-flow instructions, making\\nthese false dependencies a direct IPC loss under the existing bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a pending load destination against both raw\\nsource-register bitfields of every following instruction. Several RV32IM\\nformats do not read one or either of those fields, so immediate bits or a\\ndestination field can accidentally match the load destination and insert a\\nneedless pipeline bubble. CoreMark contains frequent loads followed by\\nimmediate arithmetic, further loads, and control-flow instructions, making\\nthese false dependencies a direct IPC loss under the existing bus-stall model.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode only the inexpensive opcode-level rs1/rs2 usage predicates for the\\ninstruction currently in IF/ID, and qualify each load-use comparison with\\nwhether that source is architecturally read. R-type, branches, and stores use\\nboth sources; OP-IMM, loads, and JALR use rs1 only; JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\nand SYSTEM use neither for hazard purposes. This removes false stalls without\\naltering true load-use interlocks, forwarding, pipeline latency, retirement,\\nor the nret=1 interface. The added gates are local to hazard detection and\\nshould improve CoreMark iterations/cycle with little Fmax or area cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify pending-load rd comparisons with opcode-derived rs1_used and rs2_used predicates so only architectural source operands cause a load-use bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load dependencies still stall while false matches in unused instruction fields do not, covering each RV32IM opcode class.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify pending-load rd comparisons with opcode-derived rs1_used and rs2_used predicates so only architectural source operands cause a load-use bubble.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load dependencies still stall while false matches in unused instruction fields do not, covering each RV32IM opcode class.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\n.vcd\\