{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fitness//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\tStatic backward-branch and direct-JAL predictor\tregression\t8.76\t307.6\tPredict legal backward conditional branches taken and all word-aligned direct\\nJAL targets in IF, while predicting forward branches and JALR fall-through.\\nCarry the predicted next PC with the instruction to EX and redirect only when\\nthe resolved architectural next PC differs from that prediction. Correct loop\\nand direct-call predictions therefore avoid the existing two-younger-instruction\\nflush, while wrong predictions recover through the existing redirect path.\\n\\nKeep prediction combinational and stateless: recognize the B-type opcode plus\\nlegal funct3 and use the immediate sign bit for backward-taken, recognize JAL,\\nform each target directly from instruction bits, and require word alignment.\\nEX remains authoritative for branch conditions, JALR targets, misalignment\\ntraps, and RVFI pc_wdata. Redirect must retain priority over all bus stalls, and\\nprediction metadata must remain attached to a held instruction so random imem\\nand dmem backpressure cannot cause stale or duplicate execution.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r2s2\tEX-aligned registered BTB fetch bypass\tregression\t-45.16\t221.94\tAdd a small direct-mapped BTB with tags, stored targets, and two-bit direction\\ncounters. Register its lookup result when the corresponding instruction enters\\nID/EX, holding and flushing that result in lockstep with ID/EX. In the following\\ncycle, while the control instruction is resolved in EX, a predicted-taken hit\\noverrides only the current imem address and IF payload PC; it does not feed the\\nPC register's same-cycle next-PC calculation. EX compares the prediction with\\nthe resolved architectural next PC, recovers through the existing redirect and\\nflush path on a miss, and trains the BTB after resolution.\\n\\nOn a correct taken prediction, the target instruction is fetched during the\\nbranch's EX cycle and may enter ID/EX immediately, eliminating the redirect\\nbubbles. On a correct not-taken prediction, fetch remains sequential. Because\\nthe critical lookup, tag comparison, and target selection are registered one\\ncycle before use, this should retain most of the current Fmax while recovering\\nthe cycle benefit demonstrated by the failed combinational predictor.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r3s2\tOne-entry taken-target instruction replay buffer\timprovement\t0.45\t406.58\tAdd a one-entry branch target instruction buffer specialized for a repeatedly\\ntaken backward conditional branch. On the first taken instance, use the\\nexisting EX redirect normally and capture the target instruction when that\\nredirected fetch is accepted. On a later taken instance of the same branch,\\nEX already knows the architectural outcome; replay the registered target\\ninstruction directly into ID/EX and redirect the registered fetch PC to the\\ncached target-plus-four address. This replaces the two wrong-path bubbles with\\nuseful target execution for the hottest loop while making no speculative\\nchange to io_imemAddr and adding no lookup to the sequential PC feedback path.\\n\\nThe entry holds branch-PC and target tags, the target instruction, and the\\ntarget-plus-four address. Fill, replay, and pending-fill state must advance\\nonly on accepted instruction fetches and must remain aligned across imem and\\ndmem stalls. Nonmatching branches, forward branches, jumps, illegal or\\nmisaligned controls, and not-taken outcomes retain the current behavior.\\nEX remains authoritative for direction, traps, RVFI pc_wdata, and recovery,\\nso this optimization cannot execute an unconfirmed target instruction.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r4s2\tHysteretic replacement for the loop replay entry\tregression\t-22.07\t316.84\tProtect the valid replay entry with a one-candidate, two-strike replacement\\nfilter. A miss by a new backward branch records only its branch PC and target;\\na second miss by that same challenger authorizes the existing accepted-target\\nfill and replaces the resident instruction. A hit clears the challenger so a\\nfrequently reused resident entry is not displaced by isolated or alternating\\nmisses. Initial fill remains immediate when no valid resident exists.\\n\\nThis adds only a candidate tag and a small equality test in the EX-confirmed\\nupdate path. It neither widens the replay payload nor changes io_imemAddr,\\nsequential PC feedback, branch resolution, or RVFI behavior. By eliminating\\ndestructive one-miss thrashing, the same single cached target instruction\\nshould cover more CoreMark loop iterations with negligible routed-Fmax cost.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r5s2\tBranch-PC-only replay-buffer tag\tregression\t-6.2\t405.12\tMatch the replay entry using only the full resolved branch PC. Keep the cached\\ntarget PC and target instruction for replay and retain the existing accepted-\\nfetch fill discipline, but remove target equality from hit detection. For an\\nRV32 conditional branch, identical branch PC implies the same instruction and\\ntherefore the same PC-relative target in the immutable benchmark instruction\\nmemory, so this does not introduce aliasing or change architectural behavior.\\n\\nRemoving one 32-bit comparator and its reduction logic should shorten and\\nlighten the confirmed-replay path while preserving the current CoreMark cycle\\nsavings, the registered-PC-only imem address path, all stall behavior, and\\nRVFI semantics. This deliberately improves the proven one-entry mechanism\\ninstead of adding another tag, speculative fetch mux, or PC feedback path.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r6s2\tRange-compressed replay-buffer PC tags\tregression\t-3.58\t416.43\tStore and compare only PC[19:2] for the resident branch tag, resident target,\\npending branch tag, and pending target. Reconstruct replay PCs as\\n{12'b0, tag, 2'b00}; alignment and the enforced instruction-address range make\\nthis representation exact rather than probabilistic. Keep both branch-PC and\\ntarget equality, the existing accepted-fetch fill protocol, and the registered\\nPC-only io_imemAddr path unchanged.\\n\\nNarrowing four address registers and both hit/fill comparisons should reduce\\nflip-flop count, equality-reduction depth, and local routing while preserving\\nthe replay buffer's measured CoreMark cycle savings. This avoids the failed\\napproaches of adding entries, removing an exact comparison, or introducing a\\nspeculative target mux on the instruction-fetch timing cone.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r7s2\tTwo-instruction confirmed loop-head replay line\tregression\t-34.25\t283.98\tTurn the existing entry into a two-instruction loop-head replay line sharing\\nthe same exact branch-PC and target tags. During a cold fill, capture both the\\ntarget and target+4 instructions on their respective accepted fetches. On an\\nEX-confirmed hit, inject the target instruction immediately as today, queue\\nthe cached target+4 instruction for the following accepted ID slot, and move\\nthe registered fetch PC directly to target+8. The queued second instruction\\ntherefore hides an instruction-bus stall at target+4 and extends the useful\\nreplay burst without adding any speculative lookup or mux to io_imemAddr.\\n\\nUse an explicit valid/consume handshake for the second replay beat so dmem\\nstalls and load-use hazards hold it without loss or duplication. Hazard\\nsource-address checks must select the pending replay instruction rather than\\nthe simultaneously visible live fetch. Entry validity is published only\\nafter both words have been accepted, and all legal/aligned/backward-branch,\\nexact-tag, RVFI, redirect, and retirement behavior remains unchanged. This\\nadds one 32-bit instruction register and a small replay-phase bit, but no\\nsecond tag, target adder, or parallel equality tree, so it should preserve\\nrouted Fmax while reducing CoreMark cycles under the scored stall model.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r8s2\tB-immediate tag for confirmed branch replay\tregression\t4.24\t450.2\tReplace the replay entry's 32-bit target tag comparison with an exact comparison of\\nthe branch instruction's B-immediate bits [12:1]. EX will export these bits directly\\nfrom the already-latched decoded immediate, and the replay buffer will store them\\nwhen a confirmed miss begins a fill. A hit will require the unchanged full branch-PC\\nmatch plus the 12-bit displacement match, while the stored full target PC remains\\navailable solely to label the cached instruction and advance fetch to target+4.\\n\\nThis is an exact tag transformation for legal conditional branches: branch PC and\\nB-immediate uniquely determine the resolved target, including backward offsets, so\\nhit behavior, CoreMark cycles, redirects, stalls, RVFI state, and illegal/misaligned\\nhandling remain unchanged. Removing 20 comparator bits and their routed resolved-\\ntarget inputs from the replay-hit cone should raise FPGA Fmax without adding fetch-PC\\nfeedback logic, replay phases, range guards, or another entry.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r9s2\tTwo-strike replay-entry admission\tregression\t-30.58\t322.0\tAdd one miss-history bit implementing two-strike admission for a valid replay\\nentry. A confirmed eligible miss immediately after a replay hit preserves the\\ncurrent entry and merely arms replacement; a second eligible miss before the\\nnext hit may start the existing accepted-fetch fill and replace the entry. Any\\nreplay hit clears the armed bit, while an invalid entry still fills on its\\nfirst eligible miss so cold-start behavior remains fast.\\n\\nThis policy should keep a hot CoreMark loop head resident across isolated\\ntaken branches and reduce destructive one-entry thrashing. It adds no target\\nstate, comparator, instruction mux, replay phase, or logic on io_imemAddr; the\\nreplay hit equation and all pipeline, stall, trap, RVFI, and nret=1 behavior\\nremain unchanged. If execution moves to a new phase, two eligible misses are\\nsufficient to admit its target, bounding the loss from stale-entry retention.\\n\nhyp-20260711-003-r10s2\tDirection-agnostic confirmed branch replay\tregression\t-58.65\t191.82\tAdmit every valid, legal, aligned, taken conditional branch to the existing\\nexact-target replay buffer, irrespective of displacement direction. A matching\\nentry will replay the cached target instruction only after EX confirms the\\nbranch is taken, exactly as today; misses will use the unchanged accepted-fetch\\nfill protocol. Remove the branch_target < branch_pc comparison and replace the\\nresolved_taken_backward interface with a simple resolved_taken qualification.\\n\\nThis broadens dynamic coverage and deletes a 32-bit magnitude comparator without\\nadding tags, entries, history bits, instruction mux levels, or any selection on\\nio_imemAddr. EX remains authoritative, so not-taken branches, illegal encodings,\\nmisaligned targets, redirects, bus stalls, RVFI ordering, and retirement behavior\\nremain unchanged. The expected gain comes from both fewer repeated forward-branch\\nbubbles and reduced execute-to-replay timing and routing cost.\\n\nhyp-20260711-006-r11s2\tIn-place refill tag for confirmed branch replay\tregression\t-21.05\t366.2\tOn an eligible confirmed miss, invalidate the resident instruction and write\\nthe resolved target directly into its existing target-tag register, using\\nfill_pending only to distinguish an incomplete entry. Match the redirected,\\naccepted fetch against that resident tag and revalidate the entry when its\\ninstruction is captured. This removes fill_target_pc_q and its duplicate\\nequality/update network end to end while leaving hit identity, replacement\\npolicy, backward-only eligibility, replay timing, and CoreMark cycles intact.\\n\\nA miss already redirects and flushes all younger instructions, so the old\\nentry cannot produce a useful confirmed hit before the redirected target is\\naccepted. Give a newer same-cycle miss priority over completion of an older\\npending fill so stale fetch data can never validate under a new tag. The\\nchange adds no state, prediction metadata, fetch-address mux, or PC feedback\\nlogic and should improve placement and median Fmax without altering bus-stall,\\ntrap, RVFI, or nret=1 behavior.\\n\nhyp-20260711-006-r12s2\tDedicated direct-JAL target instruction replay\tregression\t-65.78\t158.75\tAdd a second, independently filled one-word replay entry specialized for legal,\\naligned direct JAL targets. On the first occurrence, retain the ordinary EX\\nredirect and capture the target instruction only when that redirected fetch is\\naccepted. On a later exact-target hit, inject the registered function-entry\\ninstruction into ID/EX after EX confirms the JAL and advance the registered PC\\nto target plus four, using the same non-speculative replay protocol as the\\nproven backward-branch entry.\\n\\nKeeping JAL state in a dedicated entry prevents repeated calls from replacing\\nthe hot conditional-loop entry and avoids the forward-branch thrashing seen in\\nprior experiments. Qualify the two hit comparators by mutually exclusive EX\\ncontrol classes and select only a confirmed replay, so no lookup or target mux\\nreaches io_imemAddr or the sequential PC feedback cone. EX remains authoritative\\nfor legality, alignment, resolved next PC, traps, and RVFI; JALR remains on the\\nnormal redirect path. The expected IPC gain from recurring CoreMark calls may\\noutweigh the modest additional registered entry and class-gated comparison.\\n\nhyp-20260711-006-r13s2\tProtocol-qualified replay fill capture\timprovement\t0.87\t467.91\tTreat the first ID-accepted instruction after an eligible confirmed replay miss\\nas the pending target instruction, and remove the fetch_pc equality from fill\\ncompletion. Keep the saved pending target unchanged for the eventual resident\\nexact-target tag; only the completion qualification is simplified. Redirect\\npriority, ID/EX flushing, and pipeline holds ensure that instruction-memory or\\ndata-memory stalls merely delay this first acceptance and cannot substitute a\\ndifferent PC.\\n\\nThis deletes a 32-bit comparator from the replay update network without adding\\nstate, replacement policy, prediction metadata, address selection, or logic on\\nio_imemAddr. Replay hits remain exact-target and EX-confirmed, so architectural\\ncontrol flow, traps, RVFI ordering, and nret=1 behavior are unchanged. The\\nreduced local routing should modestly improve median Fmax with identical\\nCoreMark cycles.\\n\nhyp-20260711-006-r14s2\tOne-bit hysteretic replay-entry replacement\tregression\t-25.03\t350.79\tAdd one-bit replacement hysteresis to the existing exact-target replay entry.\\nSet the protection bit whenever the resident entry hits. On the first miss\\nagainst a protected entry, retain the entry, clear protection, and do not\\nstart a fill; a subsequent hit restores protection, while a subsequent miss\\nmay replace the entry through the existing pending-fill protocol. Invalid or\\nunprotected entries continue to fill exactly as they do today.\\n\\nThis policy keeps a repeatedly useful inner-loop instruction resident across\\na single intervening outer-loop branch, yet eventually admits a new loop after\\nworkload phase changes. It adds only one flop and local control, keeps the\\nexact 32-bit resident-target match, and adds no lookup, target mux, metadata,\\nor combinational logic to io_imemAddr. Replay remains EX-confirmed, so wrong\\ninstructions are never injected and architectural control flow, stalls,\\ntraps, RVFI ordering, and the nret=1 interface remain unchanged.\\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
